/* ---------- E. Calgary–Cambridge consultation model for Semester 2 OSCEs ----------
   Added 30 Sep 2026. The model was taught in Semester 1 but had ZERO Semester 2
   cards or questions, despite the comm topic body stating it underpins nearly every
   OSCE station. Source: Kurtz & Silverman (1996) Calgary–Cambridge Referenced
   Observation Guides; PSA National Prescribing Competency Framework; the Semester 1
   calgary-cambridge topic body in this app.                                        */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["comm","Name the five sequential phases of the Calgary&ndash;Cambridge consultation model.","<b>1. Initiating the session. 2. Gathering information. 3. Physical examination. 4. Explanation and planning. 5. Closing the session.</b><br><br>Two further processes run <i>through</i> all five rather than sitting at a point in time: <b>providing structure</b> and <b>building the relationship</b>.<br><br>(The five phases with examination as a discrete phase, plus the two continuous threads, are the enhanced framework &mdash; Kurtz, Silverman, Benson &amp; Draper, <i>Academic Medicine</i> 2003. The original observation guides are Kurtz &amp; Silverman 1996.)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","What are the two continuous threads in Calgary&ndash;Cambridge, and why do they decide OSCE grades?","<b>Providing structure</b> and <b>building the relationship</b>. They are not phases &mdash; they run the whole consultation. Structure is visible as signposting and internal summarising; relationship is visible as eye contact, acknowledging emotion, and not talking over the patient.<br><br>They are often the difference between &lsquo;obtains sufficient information to support clinical decision-making&rsquo; and &lsquo;comprehensive, patient-centred consultation&rsquo; in a rubric. A candidate who gets every fact but never signposts reads as an interrogation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Initiating the session &mdash; list the five tasks.","Greet the patient <b>by name</b>; introduce yourself <b>and your role</b> &mdash; never assume they know who you are. Establish rapport with a warm, non-judgmental opening and attend to comfort. Identify the presenting reason with an <b>open</b> question. <b>Screen for the full agenda</b> before proceeding: &lsquo;Is there anything else you wanted to discuss?&rsquo; Negotiate the agenda if several concerns are raised.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Why is agenda-screening a distinct marked item, separate from asking why the patient came?","Because the complaint a patient opens with is frequently not the one that worries them most. Without screening, the second concern surfaces at the end when there is no time &mdash; the &lsquo;door-handle&rsquo; problem.<br><br>In an OSCE it is usually a discrete tick. In practice it prevents the consultation being derailed at minute eleven of twelve.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","What does ICE stand for, and when in the model is it elicited?","<b>I</b>deas &mdash; what the patient thinks is wrong. <b>C</b>oncerns &mdash; what they are worried about. <b>E</b>xpectations &mdash; what they hope will happen today.<br><br>Elicited during <b>gathering information</b>, as the patient&rsquo;s perspective (the <i>illness</i>) alongside the biomedical history (the <i>disease</i>). Both are required. A history that captures only the biomedical half is incomplete by the model&rsquo;s own definition.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Give a usable question for each of Ideas, Concerns and Expectations.","<b>Ideas:</b> &lsquo;What did you think might be causing it?&rsquo;<br><b>Concerns:</b> &lsquo;Was there anything in particular you were worried it might be?&rsquo;<br><b>Expectations:</b> &lsquo;What were you hoping we could do today?&rsquo;<br><br>Patients often volunteer ideas unprompted in their opening sentence. Catching one that has already been offered generally counts the same as asking for it, and sounds far better.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Why does eliciting Concerns change management, not just rapport?","Because concerns about a medicine are a major driver of intentional non-adherence. If someone believes their rash is a reaction to a new medicine, counselling on an antiviral without correcting that belief leaves the belief intact.<br><br>It also surfaces red flags: &lsquo;I was worried it was my heart&rsquo; changes the consultation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Gathering information &mdash; what does the model require beyond the biomedical history?","The <b>patient&rsquo;s perspective</b>: ICE plus the <b>effects on their life</b> (work, sleep, driving, caring responsibilities), and background information where relevant.<br><br>Effects on life is the one most often dropped. &lsquo;Can I still go to work?&rsquo; is a clinical question about infectivity, income and safety, not small talk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","What is signposting, and give two examples.","Telling the patient what you are about to do before you do it, so the consultation has a visible shape.<br><br>&lsquo;I&rsquo;m going to ask some questions about your general health now, then I&rsquo;d like to take a look.&rsquo;<br>&lsquo;Before we finish, I want to go over what to watch out for.&rsquo;<br><br>It is the most visible evidence of <b>providing structure</b> and costs about four seconds.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","What is internal summarising and what does it achieve?","Repeating back what you have understood before moving to the next section: &lsquo;So it started with burning two days before the rash, the rash came up yesterday, and it&rsquo;s only on your left side &mdash; have I got that right?&rsquo;<br><br>Three things at once: it checks accuracy, it shows the patient you listened, and it gives them a natural opening to add what they forgot. It scores under structure <i>and</i> relationship.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Explanation and planning &mdash; what is &lsquo;chunk and check&rsquo;?","Deliver information in small pieces and check understanding after each, rather than a monologue followed by &lsquo;any questions?&rsquo;<br><br>Recall of a single long explanation is poor, and &lsquo;any questions?&rsquo; reliably produces &lsquo;no&rsquo; regardless of comprehension. Chunking also lets you pitch the next chunk at the level the last answer revealed.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","What distinguishes shared decision-making from informed consent in this model?","Informed consent is telling the patient the plan and obtaining agreement. <b>Shared decision-making</b> incorporates the patient&rsquo;s own illness framework &mdash; their ideas, concerns and expectations &mdash; into <i>forming</i> the plan, and offers genuine options where they exist.<br><br>In documentation the difference matters: a note showing a decision was <i>shared</i> is stronger evidence than one saying a decision was <i>made</i>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","What is teach-back, and how is it phrased so it is not insulting?","Asking the patient to say the plan back in their own words to verify your explanation worked.<br><br>Put the burden on yourself, not them: &lsquo;Just so I know I&rsquo;ve explained it properly &mdash; can you tell me how you&rsquo;ll take these?&rsquo; Never &lsquo;do you understand?&rsquo;, which tests politeness rather than comprehension.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Closing the session &mdash; name the three tasks.","<b>Forward planning</b> (what happens next, and when), <b>safety-netting</b> (what would make them seek help sooner, and from whom), and a <b>clear point of closure</b> with a final check that nothing is outstanding.<br><br>Safety-netting sits in the closing phase of the model, and the NSW herpes zoster practice standard requires its substance under counselling &mdash; &lsquo;when to seek further care and/or treatment&rsquo; and &lsquo;when to return to the pharmacist for a review&rsquo;. Other NSW standards are likely to carry equivalent wording, but check the one for your condition rather than assuming.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","What makes safety-netting adequate rather than nominal?","It must be <b>specific</b> and <b>actionable</b>: which symptoms, how soon, and which service.<br><br>&lsquo;Come back if it gets worse&rsquo; is nominal. &lsquo;If you get any pain or redness near your eye, or the rash spreads to the other side, go to the emergency department the same day &mdash; don&rsquo;t wait for the review&rsquo; is adequate. Examiners generally reward the specificity, not the presence of the word.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Which two prescribing competencies does the Calgary&ndash;Cambridge structure map onto?","<b>Competency 1</b> (understand the patient and their context &mdash; history-taking, ICE) and <b>Competency 5</b> (communicate clearly with the patient &mdash; counselling, shared decision-making, safety-netting), within the seven prescribing competencies taught in this course.<br><br><b>Citation warning.</b> The national framework is published by <b>NPS MedicineWise</b> (<i>Prescribing Competencies Framework</i>, 2nd ed, 26 April 2021; now under Ahpra review) &mdash; <b>not</b> the PSA. The PSA publishes a separate <i>National Competency Standards Framework for Pharmacists</i>. The 2021 second edition also simplified the structure, so its numbering may not match the seven-item list your course issued. <b>Check your own course handout before citing a competency number in a written reflection.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Should you name the phases of the model out loud during an OSCE?","<b>No.</b> Nobody says &lsquo;I am now gathering information.&rsquo; Marks come from performing the skills, and naming them reads as rehearsed.<br><br>Name the model in a <i>written reflection</i>, where citing the framework is likely to earn credit. Perform it in the station.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","A candidate collects every clinical fact, prescribes correctly, and still scores Credit rather than High Distinction. Where should they look first?","The two continuous threads are a common place to look &mdash; no signposting, no internal summary, no ICE, no acknowledgement of the patient&rsquo;s concern. The content is complete but the consultation is not patient-centred.<br><br>Rubrics word this as the gap between &lsquo;obtains sufficient information to support clinical decision-making&rsquo; and &lsquo;comprehensive, patient-centred consultation, obtaining consent where required and eliciting all relevant clinical information&rsquo;. Note the rubric does not name signposting or ICE directly; it also demands consent, reasoning depth, counselling, follow-up, monitoring, referral and safety-netting, so check those too.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","How does the model help you structure a consultation where you must refer rather than treat?","The phases do not change. Explanation and planning becomes explaining <i>why</i> referral is the plan and what happens next; closing still requires forward planning and safety-netting, usually more urgently.<br><br>Referral is not the absence of a plan &mdash; it is the plan, and it is marked as one."],
["comm","Where does obtaining consent sit in the model, and why is it easy to lose the mark?","<b>Initiating the session</b>, before clinical content. It is easy to lose because it feels procedural rather than clinical, so candidates think it and do not say it.<br><br>Under the NSW herpes zoster practice standard, consent has three parts to voice: <b>participation</b> (clinical), <b>financial</b>, and consent to share the record with the patient&rsquo;s <b>usual treating medical practitioner or practice, where they have one</b>. A marker cannot infer any of them. Check the standard for your own condition &mdash; this three-part structure is taken from the zoster standard.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","How should the physical examination phase be handled so it earns communication marks as well as clinical ones?","Ask permission before touching or exposing. Signpost what you are doing and why. Describe findings aloud as you go. Explain what you found before moving to planning &mdash; do not examine in silence and then announce a diagnosis.<br><br>Narrating the examination is also what makes it assessable at all when the examiner cannot see what you see.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Why is the model particularly useful as an analytical lens when comparing your own notes with AI-generated notes?","Because it predicts <i>where</i> an AI scribe will fail. Gathering information transcribes well &mdash; biomedical facts are spoken plainly. Initiating the session (introduction, consent, agenda screening) is regulatory and relational, so it is routinely dropped. ICE is often lost because a patient&rsquo;s own explanatory model reads as chat. Building the relationship is largely unrecordable &mdash; the non-verbal channel is simply absent from a transcript.<br><br>Structuring a comparison by phase turns a list of differences into a framework.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"]
);

COURSE_MCQ.push(
["comm","Which two processes run continuously through all five phases of the Calgary&ndash;Cambridge model?",
 ["Gathering information and explanation","Providing structure and building the relationship","Consent and safety-netting","History-taking and examination"],1,
 "Providing structure and building the relationship run throughout rather than occupying a phase. The other options name phases or tasks that sit at specific points. Examiners mark the two threads continuously, which is why a factually complete consultation can still lose marks.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","A patient opens with &lsquo;I thought it might be a reaction to something.&rsquo; Which element of ICE has been offered?",
 ["Concerns","Expectations","Ideas","None &mdash; it must be asked directly"],2,
 "That is the patient&rsquo;s <b>idea</b>: their own explanatory model of what is wrong. Catching an element the patient volunteers generally counts the same as eliciting it, and sounds far more natural. Concerns are what they fear; expectations are what they want done today.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","At which point of the model should the full agenda be screened?",
 ["After the history, before examination","During initiating the session","At closing, to check nothing is outstanding","Only if the patient seems to have more to say"],1,
 "Agenda screening belongs in initiating the session, before proceeding. Screening at the end produces the &lsquo;door-handle&rsquo; problem, where the concern that mattered most surfaces with no time left to address it.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Which phrasing of teach-back is correct?",
 ["&lsquo;Do you understand how to take it?&rsquo;","&lsquo;Any questions?&rsquo;","&lsquo;Just so I know I&rsquo;ve explained it properly, can you tell me how you&rsquo;ll take these?&rsquo;","&lsquo;I&rsquo;ll write it down for you.&rsquo;"],2,
 "Teach-back places the burden on the explainer, not the patient, and asks for the plan in the patient&rsquo;s own words. &lsquo;Do you understand?&rsquo; and &lsquo;any questions?&rsquo; test politeness rather than comprehension and almost always return a no.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Which safety-netting statement would an examiner mark as adequate?",
 ["&lsquo;Come back if it gets worse.&rsquo;","&lsquo;See your GP if you are not happy.&rsquo;","&lsquo;If you get pain or redness near your eye, or the rash crosses to the other side, go to the emergency department the same day.&rsquo;","&lsquo;It should settle down over the next couple of weeks.&rsquo;"],2,
 "Adequate safety-netting names the specific symptoms, the timeframe, and the service. The others are non-specific and give the patient no decision rule. Examiners generally reward the specificity, not the presence of the phrase.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","Under the NSW herpes zoster practice standard, in which phase does obtaining consent sit, and what must you voice?",
 ["Explanation and planning; clinical consent only","Initiating the session; participation, financial, and consent to share the record with the patient&rsquo;s usual medical practitioner","Closing; financial consent only","It is assumed and need not be stated"],1,
 "Consent belongs in initiating the session, before clinical content. The standard requires informed clinical <i>and</i> financial consent, plus separate consent to share the consultation record with the patient&rsquo;s usual treating medical practitioner or practice where they have one. A marker cannot infer any of the three. This is the zoster standard&rsquo;s wording &mdash; confirm the standard for your own condition.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","What distinguishes shared decision-making from informed consent?",
 ["Shared decision-making is documented, informed consent is not","Informed consent requires a signature","Shared decision-making incorporates the patient&rsquo;s ideas, concerns and expectations into forming the plan","They are interchangeable terms"],2,
 "Informed consent presents a plan and seeks agreement. Shared decision-making builds the patient&rsquo;s own illness framework into the plan and offers genuine options where they exist. The distinction is medico-legally real: a note showing a decision was shared is stronger than one showing a decision was made.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"],
["comm","You must refer rather than treat. What happens to the structure of the consultation?",
 ["The closing phase is omitted because management is elsewhere","The phases are unchanged; explanation and planning covers why referral is the plan, and closing still requires forward planning and safety-netting","Only initiating and gathering apply","Safety-netting becomes the referring clinician&rsquo;s responsibility"],1,
 "The phases do not change. Referral is not the absence of a plan &mdash; it is the plan, and it is marked as one. Safety-netting usually becomes <i>more</i> important, not less, because the patient is leaving without treatment.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>General consultation model (Calgary&ndash;Cambridge) &mdash; not in the PHAR6202 materials. Any rubric or examiner claim here is not from the course.</div>"]
);

