/* ---------- G. Which source answers which question ----------
   Added 30 Sep 2026. The app was presenting Heart Foundation 2016 and the
   Australian CVD Risk Calculator as rival frameworks and hedging mid-answer,
   which made revision harder rather than easier. Both ARE examinable: the
   university Weeks 1-2 quiz used Heart Foundation bands (Q1, Q4) and
   AusCVDRisk (Q3, Q14) in the same paper. The fix is not to remove one, it is
   to give each a clear job.                                                    */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["htn","<b>Three sources, three different jobs.</b> Which one answers which question?","<b>&lsquo;What grade is this blood pressure?&rsquo;</b> &rarr; <b>Heart Foundation 2016.</b> Classification (optimal, normal, high-normal, grade 1 to 3) and measurement technique.<br><br><b>&lsquo;What is this patient&rsquo;s 5-year risk?&rsquo;</b> &rarr; <b>Australian CVD Risk Calculator (2023).</b> Low &lt;5%, intermediate 5 to &lt;10%, high &ge;10%.<br><br><b>&lsquo;What may I do as a pharmacist prescriber?&rsquo;</b> &rarr; <b>QLD protocol.</b> Eligibility, treatment thresholds, targets, monitoring, referral triggers.<br><br>They are not rivals. They answer different questions. The QLD protocol cites Heart Foundation 2016 for measurement and classification, and among the references for its Table 2 treatment thresholds &mdash; but its risk <i>bands</i> are AusCVDRisk."],
["htn","A question gives you a risk percentage but does not name a framework. How do you decide which bands apply?","Read what the percentage is <i>doing</i>.<br><br>If it is being used to <b>decide whether to treat, and at what BP</b>, and the numbers offered are 160/100 or 140/90 with the words low or moderate, it is <b>Heart Foundation 2016</b> (low &lt;10%, moderate 10 to 15%).<br><br>If it is being used to <b>categorise risk itself</b>, or it appears alongside reclassification factors, the assessment starting ages, or the calculator inputs, it is <b>AusCVDRisk</b> (low &lt;5%, intermediate 5 to &lt;10%, high &ge;10%).<br><br>If it sits next to eligibility or referral criteria, it is the <b>QLD protocol</b>."],
["htn","Why is carrying a band label between the two frameworks the single most dangerous error on this material?","Because the same patient can hold two different labels, and swapping them invents a disagreement that does not exist.<br><br>A 5-year risk of 7% is <b>intermediate</b> under AusCVDRisk but <b>low</b> under Heart Foundation 2016. Both therefore treat from &ge;160/100 &mdash; they <b>agree</b>. Calling that patient Heart Foundation <i>moderate</i> would wrongly drop the threshold to 140/90.<br><br>Never move a band name across frameworks. Convert the percentage, then read that framework&rsquo;s own table."]
);

/* ---------- H. COPD depth from the QLD protocol ----------
   Added 1 Oct 2026 ahead of the COPD intensive. Every figure below was read
   directly out of copd-protocol.pdf. Nothing here is from general knowledge.
   The app previously had no spirometry interpretation and no severity banding. */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["copd","Define <b>persistent airflow limitation</b> as the COPD protocol states it.","<b>FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC &le;0.7, with an FEV<sub>1</sub> of &lt;80% of the predicted value, post-bronchodilator.</b><br><br>Both halves are required. A ratio at or below 0.7 on its own does not meet the definition, and the measurement must be post-bronchodilator. <i>(QLD COPD protocol, step G.)</i>"],
["copd","State the three COPD severity bands by FEV<sub>1</sub> percent predicted.","<b>Mild</b> &mdash; FEV<sub>1</sub> 60 to 79% of predicted.<br><b>Moderate</b> &mdash; FEV<sub>1</sub> 40 to 59% of predicted.<br><b>Severe</b> &mdash; FEV<sub>1</sub> &lt;40% of predicted. <i>(QLD COPD protocol, step G.)</i>"],
["copd","Which COPD severity bands can you manage under the Program, and which force referral?","<b>Only mild COPD is manageable under the Program.</b> Spirometry indicating <b>moderate or severe</b> COPD is an explicit referral trigger.<br><br>This is the single most important consequence of interpreting the spirometry correctly &mdash; the FEV<sub>1</sub> percentage decides whether the patient is yours at all. <i>(QLD COPD protocol, step G.)</i>"],
["copd","Name the five spirometry findings that require referral to an appropriate healthcare provider.","Refer if the spirometry results:<br>1. Are <b>inconsistent with COPD</b>, or are difficult to interpret<br>2. Indicate a <b>respiratory condition other than, or coexisting with,</b> COPD that is not suitable for Program management<br>3. Show <b>abnormal restrictive or mixed ventilatory patterns</b><br>4. Indicate <b>alarm symptoms or findings</b><br>5. Indicate <b>moderate or severe</b> COPD <i>(QLD COPD protocol, step G.)</i>"],
["copd","A COPD screening device gives an FEV<sub>1</sub>/FEV<sub>6</sub> ratio. What are the cut-offs, and why does the device matter?","The cut-off <b>depends on the device</b>.<br><br><b>Piko-6 and COPD-6:</b> ratio &gt;0.75 means COPD is unlikely.<br><b>Air Smart Spirometer:</b> ratio &gt;0.7 means COPD is unlikely.<br><br>Below the device&rsquo;s cut-off, and aged &ge;35, proceed to check Program eligibility. At or above it, refer. Quoting the wrong device&rsquo;s number is an easy error. <i>(QLD COPD protocol, screening.)</i>"],
["copd","Note the difference between the screening ratio and the diagnostic ratio in COPD.","Screening devices report <b>FEV<sub>1</sub>/FEV<sub>6</sub></b> with a cut-off of 0.75 or 0.7 depending on device. Diagnostic spirometry reports <b>FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC</b>, where persistent airflow limitation is &le;0.7 with FEV<sub>1</sub> &lt;80% predicted post-bronchodilator.<br><br>FEV<sub>6</sub> is a surrogate for FVC. They are not the same measurement and the thresholds are not interchangeable. <i>(QLD COPD protocol.)</i>"],
["copd","What is the minimum age to proceed to COPD Program eligibility after screening?","<b>35 years.</b> A patient under 35 is ineligible regardless of the screening result. <i>(QLD COPD protocol, eligibility.)</i>"],
["copd","List the cardiovascular conditions that make a patient ineligible for the COPD Program.","Severe (<b>Grade 3</b>) hypertension &mdash; &ge;180 mmHg systolic and/or &ge;110 mmHg diastolic &middot; congenital heart disease &middot; rheumatic heart disease &middot; heart failure &middot; arrhythmias &middot; atrial fibrillation.<br><br>Also excluded: peripheral arterial disease, heart block, pericarditis, valvular disease, pulmonary hypertension, angina, cardiomyopathy and cardiomegaly, aortic aneurysm, ischaemic heart disease, acute coronary syndrome, stroke or other cerebrovascular disease, deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism. <i>(QLD COPD protocol, eligibility.)</i>"],
["copd","A patient in the Program has an exacerbation needing antibiotics. Are they out of the Program?","<b>No.</b> The protocol allows the patient to <b>continue to be managed in the Program following concurrent referral</b> to a medical practitioner for antibiotic therapy.<br><br>Where appropriate, support them to manage the exacerbation according to their COPD action plan. Referral for antibiotics is a parallel step, not an exit. <i>(QLD COPD protocol, exacerbation management.)</i>"],
["copd","A patient has symptoms and features of both mild COPD and asthma. Which protocol governs, and what must you still check?","Manage them under the <b>Improved Asthma Symptom Control Program</b> clinical protocol &mdash; this includes those with a diagnosis of co-existing asthma and COPD.<br><br>You must still <b>confirm they meet the COPD Monitoring Program eligibility criteria before commencing treatment</b>. <i>(QLD COPD protocol.)</i>"],
["copd","Name the non-pharmacological measures the protocol lists for COPD.","Manage risk factors &middot; avoidance of exposure to risk factors &middot; smoking cessation &middot; immunisation &middot; optimising function &middot; physical activity &middot; pulmonary rehabilitation. <i>(QLD COPD protocol, step H.)</i>"],
["copd","Why does the protocol specify <i>post-bronchodilator</i> spirometry for the airflow limitation definition?","Because the definition itself is written as post-bronchodilator: FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC &le;0.7 with FEV<sub>1</sub> &lt;80% predicted, <b>post-bronchodilator</b>. A pre-bronchodilator ratio does not establish persistent limitation, since reversible obstruction would also lower it.<br><br>The protocol states the requirement; it does not set out the reversibility testing method, which it leaves to the spirometry references it cites. <i>(QLD COPD protocol, step G.)</i>"]
);

COURSE_MCQ.push(
["copd","Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC of 0.66 and FEV<sub>1</sub> at 52% of predicted. Under the QLD COPD protocol, what follows?",
 ["Mild COPD &mdash; manage under the Program","Moderate COPD &mdash; refer, as this is outside Program management","Persistent airflow limitation is not met, so no action","Severe COPD &mdash; refer urgently to an emergency department"],1,
 "The ratio of 0.66 is &le;0.7 and the FEV<sub>1</sub> is &lt;80% predicted, so persistent airflow limitation is met. An FEV<sub>1</sub> of 52% falls in the 40 to 59% band, which is <b>moderate</b> COPD. Spirometry indicating moderate or severe COPD is an explicit referral trigger &mdash; only mild COPD is manageable under the Program. Severe is &lt;40%, and nothing here indicates an emergency."],
["copd","A screening test with a Piko-6 device returns an FEV<sub>1</sub>/FEV<sub>6</sub> ratio of 0.78 in a 58-year-old smoker. What does the protocol direct?",
 ["Proceed to check Program eligibility","Refer to an appropriate healthcare provider","Repeat the test post-bronchodilator","Diagnose mild COPD and commence a short-acting bronchodilator"],1,
 "For the Piko-6 and COPD-6, a ratio &gt;0.75 means COPD is unlikely, and a ratio &ge;0.75 is a stated referral trigger. Note the cut-off is device-specific &mdash; the Air Smart Spirometer uses 0.7. You proceed to eligibility only when the ratio is below the device&rsquo;s cut-off and the patient is aged &ge;35."],
["copd","Which single figure decides whether a COPD patient can be managed under the Program at all?",
 ["The FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC ratio","The FEV<sub>1</sub> as a percentage of predicted","The FEV<sub>1</sub>/FEV<sub>6</sub> screening ratio","The number of exacerbations in the past 12 months"],1,
 "The ratio establishes that airflow limitation exists, but the <b>FEV<sub>1</sub> percent predicted</b> assigns the severity band, and only <b>mild</b> (60 to 79%) is manageable under the Program. Moderate and severe both require referral. The screening ratio only decides whether to investigate further, and exacerbation history is a separate eligibility criterion."],
["copd","A Program patient develops an exacerbation that a prescriber decides needs antibiotics. What is the correct handling?",
 ["The patient is now ineligible and must exit the Program","Continue Program management alongside a concurrent referral for antibiotic therapy","Supply the antibiotic under the Program and document it","Refer to an emergency department"],1,
 "The protocol states the patient may continue to be managed in the Program following <b>concurrent referral</b> to a medical practitioner for antibiotic therapy, supported where appropriate by their COPD action plan. Antibiotics themselves are not pharmacist-prescribable here, but needing them does not end Program management. Note this differs from the exacerbation <i>history</i> criteria, which do affect eligibility."],
["copd","Which of these is NOT listed as a cardiovascular exclusion from the COPD Program?",
 ["Atrial fibrillation","Grade 3 hypertension at &ge;180 systolic and/or &ge;110 diastolic","Well-controlled Grade 1 hypertension","Heart failure"],2,
 "The protocol excludes <b>severe (Grade 3)</b> hypertension at &ge;180 mmHg systolic and/or &ge;110 mmHg diastolic, along with congenital and rheumatic heart disease, heart failure, arrhythmias and atrial fibrillation. Well-controlled Grade 1 hypertension is not on the exclusion list. Note the Grade 3 threshold is and/or &mdash; either limb alone qualifies."]
);


/* ================================================================
   I. Oct 2026 export: PHAR6302 Weeks 7-8 (asthma, mental health), PHAR6202
      Weeks 7-8 (interprofessional communication, when things go wrong),
      ISBAR, and additions to Weeks 1-6. Drafted from the course pages by
      agents, each item independently verified against its source page.
      APPEND-ONLY: progress is keyed by array index. Never reorder.
   ================================================================ */
S2_CONDS.push({"id":"mh","name":"Mental health","tag":"6302 · Week 8","body":"<div class='box blu'>PHAR6302 Week 8. Mental health shapes adherence, self-management and outcomes across every chronic condition in this course &mdash; and some psychotropics directly raise cardiometabolic risk.</div>\n<h3>Why it matters</h3>\n<p class='small'>The relationship is <b>bidirectional</b>: chronic disease (symptoms, treatment burden, lost independence, money, isolation) feeds distress; distress reduces medicine-taking, attendance, lifestyle change, monitoring and help-seeking.</p>\n<ul><li>People with severe mental illness (SMI) die <b>up to 20 years earlier</b>; 60% of this is due to physical illness, especially CVD (Maudsley).</li>\n<li>SMI: higher rates of CVD, type 2 diabetes, obesity, smoking, metabolic syndrome.</li>\n<li>~18% of Australians (4.8 million) filled a mental-health-related prescription in 2022&ndash;23 (AIHW 2024).</li>\n<li>Distress is not always mental illness &mdash; but it still affects self-management.</li></ul>\n<table><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Mental health link</th></tr>\n<tr><td>CVD</td><td>Depression after events, cardiac anxiety</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hypertension</td><td>Stress, adherence</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diabetes</td><td>Diabetes distress, depression</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Weight</td><td>Weight stigma, emotional eating</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Smoking</td><td>Anxiety, depression, nicotine withdrawal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Asthma</td><td>Anxiety triggered by breathlessness</td></tr>\n<tr><td>COPD</td><td>Breathlessness&ndash;panic cycle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Opioid dependence</td><td>Trauma, stigma, comorbidity</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Assessment</h3>\n<p class='small'>Your role is <b>not</b> to diagnose complex psychiatric disorders. It is to recognise, assess, identify risk, refer, monitor and collaborate. Assessment starts with a patient-centred conversation.</p>\n<ul><li><b>History:</b> presenting concerns, mood, anxiety, sleep, concentration/memory, functioning, supports, alcohol/substances, past history, medicines. Then duration, severity, trajectory, impact.</li>\n<li><b>PHQ-9</b> &ndash; suspected depression. <b>GAD-7</b> &ndash; suspected anxiety. <b>K10</b> &ndash; general distress when the cause is unclear. <b>DASS</b> &ndash; 42 items: depression, anxiety, stress.</li>\n<li><b>MSE:</b> appearance/behaviour, speech, mood/affect, thoughts, perception, insight/judgement, risk.</li>\n<li>Tools <b>do not diagnose</b>; never use in isolation. TG extract: rating-scale screening not routine, but essential postpartum.</li></ul>\n<h3>Risk and escalation</h3>\n<p class='small'>Weigh duration, severity, impact on functioning and risk. Warning signs: hopelessness, withdrawal, behaviour change, self-care decline, talk of self-harm/suicide, more alcohol/substances, disengagement from care.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Level</th><th>Examples</th><th>Action</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Lower</td><td>Mild distress, adjustment after diagnosis</td><td>Support, education, monitor, screen, follow-up, encourage GP review</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Moderate</td><td>Persistent anxiety/depression symptoms, worsening distress, affecting functioning or self-management</td><td>GP referral, support services, team communication, follow-up plan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>High</td><td>Active suicidal thoughts/intent, recent attempt, psychosis, significant risk to self or others, severe deterioration in functioning</td><td>Urgent assessment and escalation</td></tr></table>\n<div class='box red'><b>Asking directly about suicide does not increase risk.</b> Avoid guilt-inducing strategies. A patient at significant risk must <b>not</b> simply be told to seek help on their own &mdash; escalate: contact the GP urgently, local mental health crisis services, ED referral, ambulance where appropriate.</div>\n<ul><li>Non-urgent: GP, psychologist, mental health social worker, community mental health, ACCHS.</li>\n<li>Mental Health Line (routes to the LHD; culturally safe option for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people), Medicare Mental Health Centres, Medicare Mental Health Check In (digital).</li>\n<li>PACER program: for 000 mental health crises &mdash; many patients find police intervention during a crisis traumatic.</li>\n<li>Include family, friends and carers where appropriate.</li></ul>\n<h3>Psychotropics and physical health</h3>\n<ul><li>Metabolic concerns: weight, waist, insulin resistance, dyslipidaemia, T2DM, CVD, plus sleep and oral health.</li>\n<li><i>Some</i> antipsychotics: significant weight gain, raised BGL, dyslipidaemia, elevated CV risk.</li>\n<li>Antipsychotic weight-gain ranking (17 agents): <b>olanzapine &gt; clozapine</b> highest; <b>haloperidol, ziprasidone, aripiprazole, lurasidone</b> lowest.</li>\n<li>Benzodiazepines: dependence, sedation, falls, impaired concentration, cognitive impairment &mdash; especially older adults and multimorbidity. Long-term use may need regular review.</li>\n<li>Monitor (depending on patient and setting): weight, waist, BP, BGL, HbA1c, lipids, adverse effects, adherence. No monitoring intervals or thresholds are given in Week 8.</li>\n<li>Antidepressant deprescribing: collaborate with GP/psychiatrist; TaperMate, RELEASE toolkit.</li>\n<li>SMI and asthma: smoking, late presentation, fixed airflow obstruction; poor engagement linked to asthma deaths.</li></ul>\n<div class='box red'>Any change to a psychotropic must be within scope and, where appropriate, with the patient&rsquo;s treating team.</div>\n<h3>Substance use, stigma and opioid dependence</h3>\n<ul><li>Substances used for distress, trauma, anxiety, sleep, chronic pain &mdash; temporary relief, worse outcomes.</li>\n<li>Trauma common in opioid dependence: childhood adversity, violence, homelessness, grief, adverse healthcare.</li>\n<li>Stigma &rarr; delayed help-seeking, avoidance, low trust, non-disclosure, worse outcomes.</li>\n<li>People-first language: &lsquo;person living with opioid dependence&rsquo;, &lsquo;challenges with treatment adherence&rsquo;, &lsquo;negative/positive urine drug screen&rsquo;.</li></ul>\n<h3>Trauma-informed and culturally safe care</h3>\n<p class='small'>Not disclosure, not counselling &mdash; care that promotes safety, trust and collaboration. Disclosure is not required.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Safety</b> &ndash; privacy, explain what will happen. <b>Trustworthiness</b> &ndash; explain why you ask; follow through. <b>Choice</b> &ndash; ask permission; respect refusal. <b>Collaboration</b> &ndash; decide together. <b>Empowerment</b> &ndash; build on strengths.</li>\n<li>Re-phrase: &lsquo;Can you tell me what made it difficult to continue taking your medicines?&rsquo;</li>\n<li>Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander social and emotional wellbeing: family/kinship, community, culture, Country, spirituality. Build rapport first; be aware of colonisation, racism and intergenerational trauma; avoid assumptions.</li></ul>\n<h3>Communication and motivational interviewing</h3>\n<ul><li>Distress: pause, acknowledge, allow silence, check they want to continue. Validate, don&rsquo;t dismiss.</li>\n<li>Resistance: curiosity, ask permission, respect autonomy, don&rsquo;t argue. &lsquo;Would it be okay if we talked about some additional support options?&rsquo;</li>\n<li>Referral: explain why, involve the patient, state purpose, invite questions.</li>\n<li><b>OARS:</b> Open questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening, Summaries. Explore ambivalence; the goal is the patient&rsquo;s own reasons, not persuasion.</li></ul>"});
BRIEFS.mh = {"mins":6,"src":"PHAR6302 Week 8","pts":[["Bidirectional link","Chronic disease feeds distress; distress erodes adherence and self-management. SMI: death <b>up to 20 years earlier</b>, 60% from physical illness, mainly CVD."],["Your role","Recognise, assess, identify risk, refer, monitor, collaborate. <b>Not</b> diagnosing complex psychiatric disorders."],["Tools","<b>PHQ-9</b> depression &middot; <b>GAD-7</b> anxiety &middot; <b>K10</b> general distress, cause unclear &middot; <b>DASS</b> 42-item &middot; <b>MSE</b> 7 domains. Tools never diagnose or stand alone."],["Risk levels","Lower &rarr; support, monitor, follow-up. Moderate (persistent, affecting function) &rarr; GP referral + plan. High (active suicidal thoughts or intent, recent attempt, psychosis, significant risk to self or others, severe deterioration in functioning) &rarr; <b>urgent escalation</b>."],["Suicide","Asking directly and respectfully does <b>not</b> increase risk. No guilt tactics. Never just advise a patient at significant risk to seek help on their own &mdash; escalate: GP urgently, local mental health crisis services, ED, ambulance where appropriate."],["Psychotropics","Antipsychotic weight gain highest: <b>olanzapine, clozapine</b>; lowest haloperidol. Benzodiazepines: dependence, sedation, falls, cognition. Monitor weight, waist, BP, BGL, HbA1c, lipids, adverse effects, adherence (no intervals given). Changes to psychotropics: within scope, with the treating team."],["Stigma and trauma","People-first language. Trauma-informed = safety, trustworthiness, choice, collaboration, empowerment; no disclosure required. Culturally safe: rapport first, SEWB, no assumptions."],["MI","OARS &mdash; Open questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening, Summaries. Explore ambivalence; don&rsquo;t persuade."]]};
BRIEFS.asthma.pts = BRIEFS.asthma.pts.concat([["Diagnosis needs objective variability","History alone cannot confirm asthma. Positive bronchodilator response = FEV<sub>1</sub> up <b>&ge;200 mL AND &ge;12%</b>. Normal spirometry or normal FeNO does <b>not</b> exclude asthma. FeNO &ge;40 ppb supports it. Don&rsquo;t delay treatment waiting for spirometry; QLD Entry point 1 = treat concurrent to referral."],["Control vs risk &mdash; assess both","Control (4 weeks): daytime symptoms, night waking, reliever use, activity limitation. ACT 20&ndash;25 well / 15&ndash;19 poor / &lt;15 very poor; ACQ5 &ge;1.5 not well controlled. Future risk: <b>previous exacerbations are the strongest predictor</b>, plus reliever overuse, adherence, technique, smoking/vaping, comorbidities."],["Does it fit asthma?","Inspiratory wheeze or crackles argue against asthma. Red flags: sudden severe breathlessness, unilateral chest pain, asymmetrical breath sounds, haemoptysis, fever, hypoxaemia, poor bronchodilator response, symptoms despite normal spirometry &mdash; think pneumothorax, PE, ILO, heart failure, pneumonia."],["Before stepping up","Check adherence, inhaler technique (watch a demonstration), triggers, smoking/vaping, comorbidities, alternative diagnosis. Don&rsquo;t step down during respiratory infection, travel or pregnancy. OCS &gt;1000 mg lifetime is linked to cumulative harm."],["Self-management and review","Peak flow vs personal best: green 80&ndash;100%, yellow 50&ndash;79%, red &lt;50%. Review earlier if reliever &gt;2&times;/week (outside pre-exercise or AIR). Repeat spirometry after 3&ndash;6 months of ICS (personal best) and about every 2 years."]]);
BRIEFS.comm.pts = BRIEFS.comm.pts.concat([["NURSE &mdash; validate before you argue","Name the emotion &middot; Understand &middot; Respect &middot; Support &middot; Explore. Team conflict is often about emotion, perception or an unmet need, not the clinical task. &lsquo;Explore&rsquo; uncovers the root cause &mdash; miscommunication, role confusion or differing priorities."],["Raise it, then escalate: I-message &rarr; CUSS / PACE","<b>I-message:</b> I feel&hellip; when&hellip; because&hellip; I would like&hellip;<br><b>CUSS:</b> Concerned &rarr; Uncomfortable &rarr; Safety issue &rarr; Stop. <b>PACE:</b> Probe &rarr; Alert &rarr; Challenge &rarr; Emergency. Escalate when a safety concern is not being addressed."],["GP disagrees? Interests, not positions","Raise concern (feedback) &rarr; explore their rationale (interests) &rarr; suggest an alternative (negotiation) &rarr; shared plan (collaboration). Partner, not adversary. Feedback: specific, behaviour-focused, respectful, linked to patient outcomes."],["Decision challenged after the fact","Different clinicians can make different, reasonable decisions; outcomes alone do not determine decision quality. Avoid assumptions, gather information, reassess &mdash; with the patient and the GP, not in isolation."],["Reflect with Gibbs","Description &rarr; Feelings &rarr; Evaluation &rarr; Analysis &rarr; Conclusion &rarr; Action planning."]]);
BRIEFS.safe.pts = BRIEFS.safe.pts.concat([["Pick the right tool","<b>RCA</b>: serious adverse/sentinel events, formal investigation, governance. <b>SEA</b>: everyday reflective team learning &mdash; near misses, complaints, good practice. <b>Human factors / London Protocol</b>: how systems shape performance. <b>Sensemaking</b>: how decisions looked at the time; counters hindsight bias. They are complementary."],["RCA &mdash; 8 steps","Define problem &rarr; gather information (timeline) &rarr; contributing factors &rarr; root causes &rarr; develop solutions (specific, actionable, system-level, realistic, measurable) &rarr; implement action plan &rarr; monitor and evaluate &rarr; share learning. Tools: 5 Whys, fishbone."],["SEA &mdash; 7 stages","Awareness and prioritisation &rarr; information gathering &rarr; facilitated team meeting &rarr; analysis (what happened, why, what learned, what changed) &rarr; agree, implement, monitor change &rarr; write it up &rarr; report, share, review. Needs psychological safety."],["Systems, not blame","Person-centred responses (retraining, discipline) promote silence. Swiss cheese: harm when gaps in layers align. Yorkshire framework and Dirty Dozen (fatigue, stress, assumptions, interruptions, pressure&hellip;) name contributing factors."],["Second victim","Clinicians involved in errors often feel guilt, shame, anxiety, self-doubt. Seek supervision, debriefing, counselling/employee support. Poor wellbeing itself worsens patient safety."]]);
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["asthma","What two things does an asthma diagnosis require?","<b>A compatible clinical history AND objective evidence of variable airflow limitation.</b><br>Symptoms alone cannot confirm asthma, even when they strongly suggest it. Diagnosing asthma is outside the pharmacist prescriber&rsquo;s scope &mdash; but you must understand the pathway.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","What spirometry result counts as a positive bronchodilator response in asthma?","<b>FEV<sub>1</sub> increase of at least 200 mL AND at least 12% from baseline</b> after bronchodilator.<br>In a patient not using ICS, the same rise (&ge;200 mL and 12%) after an <b>ICS trial</b> also supports the diagnosis.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Spirometry is normal in a patient whose history strongly suggests asthma. Is asthma excluded?","<b>No.</b> Normal spirometry does not exclude asthma, particularly if the patient is asymptomatic when tested.<br>If suspicion remains high: repeat spirometry, FeNO, peak flow monitoring, bronchial provocation testing, or specialist referral.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","What does FeNO measure, and what level supports asthma?","<b>Fractional exhaled nitric oxide &mdash; a non-invasive breath test for eosinophilic (type-2) airway inflammation.</b><br>FeNO &ge;40 ppb supports asthma in a patient with expiratory flow limitation on spirometry and strongly suggestive symptoms (adults: sensitivity 0.61, specificity 0.82).<br>A <b>normal FeNO does not rule out asthma.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Asthma is suspected but spirometry is not immediately available. Do you wait?","<b>No &mdash; do not delay clinically indicated treatment while awaiting spirometry.</b><br>The AAH pathway allows temporary treatment as asthma when clinically suspected. QLD Entry point 1 lets an undiagnosed patient (no ineligibility criteria) start management <b>concurrent to referral</b> for review and collaborative care.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Which spirometry reference set should be used for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander patients?","<b>GLI-2012 (Global Lung Function Initiative) &lsquo;other/mixed&rsquo; category.</b><br>Lung function reference values may need adjustment for ethnicity.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","What four indicators define current asthma symptom control, and over what period?","<b>Daytime symptoms, night waking, reliever use, activity limitation &mdash; over the previous 4 weeks.</b><br>Control worsens as these become more frequent. Ask about all four even when the patient says their asthma is &lsquo;fine&rsquo;.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Interpret the Asthma Control Test (ACT) score bands.","<table><tr><th>ACT</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>20&ndash;25</td><td>Well controlled</td></tr><tr><td>15&ndash;19</td><td>Poorly controlled</td></tr><tr><td>&lt;15</td><td>Very poorly controlled</td></tr></table>5 questions, patient self-assessment, covers the previous 4 weeks (National Asthma Council).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","ACQ5: what does it cover, who is it for, and what score signals poor control?","<b>Score of 1.5 or greater suggests asthma is not well controlled.</b><br>5 questions on symptoms over the <b>previous week</b>; suitable for ages <b>12 and older</b> (Asthma Australia).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","What is the strongest predictor of future asthma exacerbation risk?","<b>Previous exacerbations</b> &mdash; ED presentations, hospital admissions, oral corticosteroid courses.<br>Good symptom control does not always mean low risk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Besides past exacerbations, list future-risk factors to assess in asthma.","<ul><li>Excessive reliever use (a signal, not a habit)</li><li>Poor adherence (less ICS than prescribed)</li><li>Incorrect inhaler technique</li><li>Smoking and vaping (also reduce corticosteroid response)</li><li>Comorbidities: allergic rhinitis, obesity, anxiety, depression, GORD, sleep apnoea</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Which asthma patients does Week 7 class as &lsquo;treat and refer&rsquo; rather than &lsquo;refer&rsquo; or &lsquo;treat&rsquo;?","<b>Diagnostic uncertainty, persistent poor control, significant comorbidities, or a need for additional investigations.</b><br>Start treatment where appropriate while arranging GP/respiratory specialist referral and follow-up.<br>Urgent <b>refer</b>: respiratory distress, red flags, recent severe exacerbations, previous ICU/intubation, frequent ED presentations, specialist-managed severe asthma.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Which history question helps identify occupational asthma?","<b>&ldquo;Do your symptoms improve when you are away from work?&rdquo;</b><br>Explore exposure to dusts, chemicals, cleaning products, fumes and strong odours.<br><i>Note:</i> suspected occupational asthma is a Program exclusion under the QLD protocol (existing card C181) &mdash; refer rather than enrol.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","What symptom pattern features increase suspicion of asthma?","<b>Variable, recurrent symptoms with symptom-free periods.</b><ul><li>Worse at night or early morning</li><li>Triggered by exercise, allergens, viral infections, cold air, smoke/irritants</li><li>Improve with reliever</li></ul>The pattern matters more than any single symptom.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Which auscultation findings argue AGAINST asthma?","<b>Inspiratory wheeze and crackles.</b> Inspiratory wheeze should prompt alternative diagnoses; crackles are not a feature of asthma.<br>Also: wheeze only on forced expiration is non-specific, and wheeze may be <b>absent</b> even in a severe exacerbation. A murmur may point to a cardiac contributor.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","A patient with &lsquo;asthma&rsquo; describes throat tightness, noisy breathing IN, and voice change with perfumes. Likely differential?","<b>Inducible laryngeal obstruction (vocal cord dysfunction).</b><br>Clue: difficulty breathing <i>in</i>, inspiratory noise, throat tightness, voice change; triggered by stress, exercise, perfumes, irritants.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Which history and examination features should raise suspicion of pneumothorax?","<b>Sudden-onset breathlessness with sudden unilateral pleuritic chest pain.</b><br>Also prior pneumothorax, recent trauma, underlying lung disease. Exam: reduced/absent breath sounds on one side, reduced chest expansion, tachycardia, tachypnoea. Typically poorly responsive to reliever.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Features of a tension pneumothorax?","<b>Severe respiratory distress, hypotension, altered consciousness, tracheal deviation, distended neck veins.</b><br>Requires immediate emergency management.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","List the red flags that suggest a diagnosis other than asthma.","<ul><li>Sudden-onset severe breathlessness</li><li>Unilateral chest pain</li><li>Asymmetrical breath sounds</li><li>Haemoptysis</li><li>Fever and systemic illness</li><li>Significant hypoxaemia</li><li>Poor response to bronchodilator</li><li>Ongoing symptoms despite normal spirometry</li></ul>Shift from &lsquo;how do I manage this asthma?&rsquo; to &lsquo;could this be something more serious?&rsquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Asthma vs COPD (QLD Appendix 3): onset and symptom pattern.","<b>Asthma:</b> onset before 40; variable airflow limitation; symptoms vary, triggered (laughter, exercise, allergens), worse night/early morning, improve with bronchodilator (minutes) or ICS (days&ndash;weeks).<br><b>COPD:</b> onset after 40; persistent airflow limitation; persistent, slowly progressive dyspnoea with limited bronchodilator relief.<br><b>Overlap:</b> features of both; onset before or after 40.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","State the peak flow traffic-light zones.","<table><tr><th>Zone</th><th>% personal best</th><th>Action</th></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td>80&ndash;100%</td><td>Continue usual treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Yellow</td><td>50&ndash;79%</td><td>Follow action plan, monitor more</td></tr><tr><td>Red</td><td>&lt;50%</td><td>Emergency &mdash; urgent care</td></tr></table>Personal best = highest reading while asthma is well controlled (not population values).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","When should a written asthma action plan be reviewed?","<b>Whenever there is a change in control, a treatment adjustment, an exacerbation, or a change in the patient&rsquo;s circumstances.</b><br>Every patient should have one. It may be symptom-based and/or PEF-based.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Which vaccinations does Week 7 recommend encouraging in asthma?","<b>Influenza, COVID-19, pertussis, pneumococcal, and RSV (where indicated).</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","What should you assess before stepping UP asthma treatment?","<b>Adherence, inhaler technique, trigger exposure, smoking/vaping, comorbidities, alternative diagnoses.</b><br>Ask: &ldquo;Is the asthma truly uncontrolled, or is there another explanation?&rdquo; Poor control is often one of these, not inadequate pharmacotherapy.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Week 7 lists times to avoid stepping DOWN asthma treatment. What are they?","<b>Respiratory infections, travel, pregnancy, and other high-risk periods.</b><br>Step-down is considered after 2&ndash;3 months of good control with low exacerbation risk and clinical stability.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","What cumulative oral corticosteroid exposure does Week 7 link to significant harm?","<b>More than 1000 mg cumulative lifetime dose.</b><br>Harms: osteoporosis/fractures, pneumonia, CVD, cataracts, T2DM, weight gain, anxiety/depression. Preventing exacerbations with preventer therapy is OCS stewardship.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","A patient asks for OTC Ventolin: &ldquo;I just need my puffer.&rdquo; Which communication techniques does Week 7 recommend?","<b>Validation before information, ask-tell-ask, motivational interviewing, shared decision-making, teach-back.</b><br>Avoid &ldquo;You&rsquo;ve been managing your asthma incorrectly.&rdquo; Instead: &ldquo;We now have evidence that treating inflammation early reduces the risk of serious asthma attacks, even for people who don&rsquo;t have symptoms every day.&rdquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Mild vs moderate vs severe asthma exacerbation &mdash; distinguishing features?","<b>Mild:</b> slightly outside usual variation; more symptoms/reliever, occasional night waking.<br><b>Moderate:</b> distressing; persistent worsening over <b>two or more days</b>, deteriorating lung function, needs treatment change.<br><b>Severe:</b> no improvement despite repeated reliever, significant breathing difficulty, needs urgent review/OCS/ED or hospital.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Is a severe exacerbation only a risk in severe asthma?","<b>No.</b> Severe asthma and a severe exacerbation are not the same &mdash; a patient with otherwise mild asthma can have a severe or life-threatening exacerbation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","What warm-up advice reduces exercise-induced bronchoconstriction, and when does EIB peak?","<b>Warming up 10&ndash;15 minutes before exercise induces a refractory period of about 2 hours without EIB.</b><br>EIB typically peaks around 15 minutes <i>after stopping</i> intense exercise. Frequent EIB may signal poor underlying control &mdash; optimise ICS-containing treatment.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Common inhaler technique errors: pMDI vs DPI vs ICS-specific?","<b>pMDI:</b> not shaking, poor hand&ndash;breath coordination, inhaling too fast, no breath-hold, no spacer when appropriate.<br><b>DPI:</b> wrong dose loading, exhaling into device, inadequate inspiratory effort.<br><b>ICS:</b> not rinsing mouth; confusing preventer and reliever.<br>Have the patient <b>demonstrate</b>, then re-check after education.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","When does Week 7 advise an EARLIER asthma review than scheduled?","<ul><li>Deterioration or poor control</li><li>Reliever <b>more than twice a week</b> (outside pre-exercise dosing or an anti-inflammatory reliever regimen)</li><li>No improvement after a treatment change</li><li>Intolerable adverse effects or device difficulty</li><li>Any alarm sign</li><li>New or worsening comorbidity</li><li>Self-initiated &lsquo;stand by&rsquo; OCS or any exacerbation</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","When should spirometry be repeated in ongoing asthma care?","<ul><li>At initial presentation (baseline) or to confirm suspected asthma</li><li>Poor control or exacerbations</li><li><b>After 3&ndash;6 months of ICS-containing treatment</b> to set a personal best</li><li>Routine periodic review <b>approximately every 2 years</b>, even if well controlled</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Asthma prevalence in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people vs the total population, and two practice actions?","<b>About 16% vs around 11%</b>, with higher hospitalisation and mortality.<br>Actions: ask and document status (a protocol history item); adapt review/referral for access barriers; engage ACCHOs and Aboriginal Health Workers/Practitioners. The <b>patient, not the clinician</b>, determines whether care was culturally safe.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","After an asthma review, what should a pharmacist prescriber communicate to the GP?","<b>Changes to therapy, symptom-control results, adherence concerns, inhaler technique issues, and recommendations for ongoing review.</b><br>Also relevant history, spirometry, risk factors, action plan updates, referrals, and planned follow-up.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What does the Week 8 module mean by a <b>bidirectional</b> relationship between mental health and chronic disease?","<b>Chronic disease can worsen mental health, and mental health can worsen chronic disease control.</b><br>Symptoms, treatment burden, lost independence, financial stress, uncertainty and isolation feed distress, anxiety or depression. Those in turn reduce medicine-taking, appointment attendance, lifestyle change, self-monitoring and help-seeking &mdash; so control deteriorates and the cycle repeats.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","How much earlier do people with severe mental illness (SMI) die, and what drives it?","<b>Up to 20 years earlier than the general population; 60% of this premature mortality is attributed to physical health conditions, in particular cardiovascular disease.</b><br>Source: Maudsley practice guidelines for physical health conditions in psychiatry (2021), as quoted in the module.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","People with SMI have higher rates of which physical conditions &mdash; and what contributes?","<b>Cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, obesity, smoking and metabolic syndrome.</b><br>Contributors: social and economic disadvantage, barriers to healthcare access, lifestyle risk factors, adverse effects of some psychotropic medicines, and lack of insight. Physical health needs often require <i>proactive</i> monitoring and collaboration.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Distress or mental illness? Which four things should you weigh when assessing a distressed patient?","<b>Duration of symptoms, severity, impact on daily functioning, and level of risk to the patient or others.</b><br>Distress (new diagnosis, worsening symptoms, finances, bereavement) does not necessarily mean mental illness &mdash; but it still affects self-management and should be taken seriously.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","List the warning signs the module gives for <b>increased risk</b>.","<b>Hopelessness; social withdrawal; significant behaviour change; marked deterioration in self-care; talk of self-harm or suicide; increased alcohol or substance use; loss of engagement with healthcare and treatment.</b><br>Patients often won&rsquo;t disclose openly &mdash; changes in behaviour, adherence or engagement may be the clue.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Does asking a patient directly about suicidal thoughts increase their risk?","<b>No.</b> Evidence shows asking directly and respectfully does not increase the likelihood of self-harm.<br>If concerned about safety, ask further questions and seek support. Avoid guilt-inducing strategies (e.g. how much it would hurt family) &mdash; they may stop the person talking openly to a helper in future.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Lower concern: give examples and the appropriate actions.","<b>Examples:</b> mild distress; adjustment difficulties after a diagnosis; uncomfortable symptoms with no immediate safety concern.<br><b>Actions:</b> support and education; monitor symptoms; screening tools where appropriate; arrange follow-up; encourage GP review.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Moderate concern: give examples and the appropriate actions.","<b>Examples:</b> persistent anxiety or depression symptoms; worsening distress; symptoms affecting daily functioning; difficulty with chronic disease self-management.<br><b>Actions:</b> GP referral for further assessment; discuss mental health support services; communicate with the healthcare team; develop a follow-up plan.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","High concern: what presentations qualify, and what do they require?","<b>Active suicidal thoughts or intent; recent suicide attempt; psychosis; significant risk to self or others; severe deterioration in functioning &rarr; urgent assessment and escalation.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Urgent safety concern: what are the escalation pathways &mdash; and what must you NOT do?","<b>Contact the GP urgently; local mental health crisis services; emergency department referral; ambulance where appropriate.</b><br>A patient at significant risk should <b>not</b> simply be advised to seek help independently without appropriate support and escalation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Name the non-urgent referral options and two Australian access points from the module.","<b>GP, psychologist, mental health social worker, community mental health services, Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Services, other support services.</b><br>Access points: the <b>Mental Health Line</b> (routes the call to the patient&rsquo;s LHD; offers a culturally safe service for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people), <b>Medicare Mental Health Centres</b>, and the free digital <b>Medicare Mental Health Check In</b>. Communicate your concerns and findings clearly when referring.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What is &mdash; and is not &mdash; the pharmacist prescriber&rsquo;s role in mental health assessment?","<b>Not to diagnose complex psychiatric disorders.</b> The role is to recognise distress, do structured assessment with validated tools, identify risk, support the patient, refer, monitor over time, document and collaborate.<br>You do not need to manage every concern independently &mdash; recognising when to refer or escalate is the key skill.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What does a structured mental health history cover?","<b>Presenting concerns; mood; anxiety; sleep; concentration and memory; daily functioning; social supports; alcohol and other substance use; past mental health history; current and previous medicines.</b><br>Then: how long, how severe, improving or worsening, effect on functioning, effect on chronic disease management.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What are screening tools for, and what is their key limitation?","<b>They support assessment and monitoring &mdash; they do not establish a diagnosis and must never be used in isolation.</b><br>Uses: flag symptoms needing assessment, gauge severity, track change over time, support communication with other professionals. Interpret with history, presentation, functioning and context. Assessment begins with a patient-centred conversation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","PHQ-9, GAD-7 or K10 &mdash; when is each used?","<b>PHQ-9:</b> depression suspected (low mood, anhedonia, sleep, fatigue, appetite, concentration, thoughts of self-harm).<br><b>GAD-7:</b> anxiety suspected (worry, restlessness, irritability, difficulty relaxing, fear something bad will happen).<br><b>K10:</b> general psychological distress when it is <i>not clear</i> whether anxiety, depression or something else is contributing.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Name the domains of the Mental State Examination (MSE).","<b>Appearance and behaviour; speech; mood and affect; thoughts; perception; insight and judgement; risk.</b><br>The Royal College of Psychiatrists calls it a &ldquo;clear, objective snapshot of someone&rsquo;s mental functioning at a given time-point&rdquo;.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","According to the Therapeutic Guidelines extract in Week 8, when is screening with depression rating scales <i>essential</i>?","<b>In the postpartum period.</b> Otherwise rating-scale screening is not routine. Scales can be clinician- or patient-rated, and should be used alongside clinical, relationship-based care &mdash; never relied on alone.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Which antipsychotics carry the highest and lowest weight-gain risk on the module&rsquo;s ranking?","<b>Highest: olanzapine, then clozapine.</b> <b>Lowest: haloperidol, then ziprasidone, aripiprazole, lurasidone.</b><br>17 agents are ranked; quetiapine (6th) and risperidone/paliperidone (7th) sit between. <i>Some</i> antipsychotics are also associated with increased blood glucose, dyslipidaemia and elevated cardiovascular risk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Which metabolic and physical-health concerns are linked to some psychotropic medicines?","<b>Weight gain, increased waist circumference, insulin resistance, dyslipidaemia, type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease</b> &mdash; plus effects on sleep and oral health (e.g. xerostomia).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What physical-health monitoring can a pharmacist contribute for a patient on psychotropics?","<b>Weight, waist circumference, blood pressure, blood glucose, HbA1c, lipids, adverse effects and adherence</b> &mdash; depending on patient and setting.<br>Purpose: catch medicine-related change early and intervene.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","A 45-year-old on olanzapine has gained significant weight over two years, with rising waist circumference and HbA1c. What is the pharmacist prescriber&rsquo;s stance?","<b>Recognise the medicine may be contributing, monitor, and work with the treating team &mdash; any psychotropic change happens within scope and in collaboration.</b><br>Olanzapine tops the module&rsquo;s weight-gain ranking. The source poses this case without a model answer; specific switching or add-on options are not given.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What benzodiazepine concerns does the module list, and who is most affected?","<b>Dependence, sedation, falls risk, impaired concentration, cognitive impairment</b> &mdash; especially important in <b>older adults</b> and people with multiple chronic conditions.<br>Long-term use <i>may require</i> regular review and consideration of alternative management strategies. (Week 8 case: a COPD patient taking diazepam most evenings for years &rarr; prompts medication review.)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What is the pharmacist&rsquo;s role in antidepressant deprescribing, and which tools does the module name?","<b>In collaboration with GPs and psychiatrists: facilitate safe tapering, minimise withdrawal symptoms, support shared decision-making.</b><br>Tools: <b>TaperMate</b> and the <b>RELEASE toolkit</b> (releasetoolkit.com.au).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Why is asthma risk higher in people with SMI (Maudsley extract)?","<b>High smoking and passive smoke exposure, poor access to and engagement with care (late presentation, fixed airflow obstruction like COPD), and comorbid obesity, sleep apnoea and substance misuse.</b><br>Poor engagement with care is associated with asthma-related deaths &mdash; disease management and concordance matter greatly.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Why might a patient use substances, and why does it matter for management?","<b>To manage emotional distress, cope with trauma, reduce anxiety, improve sleep, or deal with chronic pain.</b><br>Relief is temporary; over time physical, mental and chronic disease outcomes worsen. Understanding the reason supports more effective, compassionate care. Mental health and substance use influence each other.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What trauma is common in people living with opioid dependence, and what does it affect?","<b>Childhood adversity, violence or abuse, homelessness, grief and loss, adverse healthcare experiences.</b><br>Affects trust in clinicians, willingness to seek help, engagement with treatment and communication in consultations.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What can stigma in healthcare lead to?","<b>Delayed help-seeking, avoidance of services, reduced trust, reluctance to disclose, and poorer outcomes.</b><br>Even well-intended language can stigmatise. Compassionate care does not mean ignoring risk &mdash; it balances assessment with respect.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Give person-centred replacements for: drug addict; substance abuser; non-compliant; clean/dirty urine screen.","<b>Person living with opioid dependence; person experiencing substance use disorder; experiencing challenges with treatment adherence; negative/positive urine drug screen.</b><br>Rule: people-first, not illness-first language.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What is trauma-informed care NOT, and what IS it?","<b>Not asking patients to disclose trauma, and not trauma counselling.</b> It is recognising that trauma may shape behaviour, engagement and decisions, and providing care that promotes safety, trust and collaboration.<br>Patients do not need to disclose trauma to receive trauma-informed care.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Name the five principles of trauma-informed care with one practical action each.","<b>Safety</b> &ndash; private space, explain what will happen.<br><b>Trustworthiness</b> &ndash; explain why you&rsquo;re asking/using a screening tool; be honest about role limits; follow through.<br><b>Choice</b> &ndash; ask permission before sensitive topics; respect a decision not to discuss.<br><b>Collaboration</b> &ndash; involve the patient in referral decisions.<br><b>Empowerment</b> &ndash; acknowledge successes, build self-efficacy.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Rephrase to avoid re-traumatisation: &lsquo;Why didn&rsquo;t you take your medicines?&rsquo; and &lsquo;You missed several appointments.&rsquo;","<b>&lsquo;Can you tell me what made it difficult to continue taking your medicines?&rsquo;</b><br><b>&lsquo;I noticed it has been difficult to attend some appointments. Can you tell me about any challenges you&rsquo;ve been facing?&rsquo;</b><br>Avoid language that reproduces shame, loss of control or judgement.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","How is mental health framed for many Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and what does culturally safe care involve?","<b>Holistically, as social and emotional wellbeing &mdash; connected to family and kinship, community, culture, Country and spirituality.</b><br>Practice: build rapport before clinical issues; shared decision-making; recognise social determinants; be aware of colonisation, racism and intergenerational trauma; respect family and community roles &mdash; without assuming individual experiences. Some communities describe inner spirit as <i>Ngarlu</i>; a weakened connection may be understood as causing sadness or disconnection.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","A patient becomes distressed mid-consultation. What helps?","<b>Stay calm; pause if needed; acknowledge emotions; allow silence; stay non-judgemental; check whether they want to continue.</b><br>You don&rsquo;t need to solve everything &mdash; creating space to share can be the first step. Validate (&lsquo;That sounds like it&rsquo;s been really difficult&rsquo;) rather than dismiss (&lsquo;You shouldn&rsquo;t worry about that&rsquo;).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","A patient minimises symptoms and declines referral. How do you respond?","<b>Explore concerns with curiosity, ask permission before advising, respect autonomy, focus on shared goals, don&rsquo;t argue or persuade.</b><br>Not &lsquo;You need to see a psychologist&rsquo; but &lsquo;Would it be okay if we talked about some additional support options that might help?&rsquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","What does OARS stand for in motivational interviewing? Give an example of each.","<b>Open questions</b> &ndash; &lsquo;What concerns do you have about your current treatment plan?&rsquo;<br><b>Affirmations</b> &ndash; &lsquo;You&rsquo;ve already taken an important step by seeking help today.&rsquo;<br><b>Reflective listening</b> &ndash; &lsquo;It sounds like your symptoms have been making daily activities much more difficult.&rsquo;<br><b>Summaries</b> &ndash; &lsquo;So, you&rsquo;re concerned about your breathing, you want to become more active, but you&rsquo;re worried exercise might worsen your symptoms.&rsquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","&lsquo;I know smoking is making my breathing worse, but it&rsquo;s the only thing that helps when I&rsquo;m stressed.&rsquo; What is this, and what is the MI goal?","<b>Ambivalence</b> &mdash; recognising benefits of change alongside barriers.<br>MI helps the patient explore both sides and find <i>their own</i> reasons for change. The goal is not to persuade. Providing information alone is often insufficient for lasting behaviour change.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What does the NURSE framework stand for, and what is it for in team conflict?","<b>Name the emotion, Understand, Respect, Support, Explore.</b><br>The Week 7 notes say team conflict is often driven by emotion, perception or an unmet need rather than the clinical task; NURSE addresses that human element in a structured, compassionate way.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Give one example line for each step of NURSE when a colleague is frustrated by your change to the plan.","<ul><li><b>N</b> &ldquo;It sounds like this situation has been really challenging.&rdquo;</li><li><b>U</b> &ldquo;I can see why this change in the plan might be frustrating given your workload.&rdquo;</li><li><b>R</b> &ldquo;I really value the expertise you bring to this case.&rdquo;</li><li><b>S</b> &ldquo;I want to support you so we can resolve this together.&rdquo;</li><li><b>E</b> &ldquo;Can you tell me more about what&rsquo;s most concerning from your side?&rdquo;</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","NURSE &mdash; how should you &lsquo;Name&rsquo; the emotion?","<b>Acknowledge what the other person seems to be expressing, without labelling their internal state.</b><br>The Week 7 notes say that in team conflict, simply naming the tension often helps reduce defensiveness and open dialogue. Example: &ldquo;It sounds like this situation has been really challenging.&rdquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What does the &lsquo;Explore&rsquo; step of NURSE uncover?","<b>The root cause of the conflict</b> &mdash; per the Week 7 notes, often miscommunication, role confusion, or differing clinical priorities.<br>Invite the colleague to share more of their perspective.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","List four benefits of using NURSE in interprofessional conflict.","<ul><li>Promotes <b>psychological safety</b></li><li>Reduces <b>conflict escalation</b> by validating perspectives early</li><li>Reinforces <b>mutual respect</b></li><li>Aligns the team back toward <b>shared goals</b></li></ul>(The notes add a fifth: supports reflective practice.)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What is the four-part structure of an I-message?","<b>I feel&hellip; / When&hellip; / Because&hellip; / I would like&hellip;</b><br>It lets you raise a concern assertively without sounding accusatory. Course example: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m concerned about the risk of interactions with this regimen because of the patient&rsquo;s renal function. I&rsquo;d like us to review the options together.&rdquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","When is assertiveness particularly important for a pharmacist prescriber?","<b>When the patient&rsquo;s safety is at risk, or your clinical judgement differs from others&rsquo;.</b><br>The Week 7 notes define assertiveness as expressing your concerns clearly, respectfully and confidently.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What does CUSS stand for, in order of escalation?","<b>Concerned &rarr; Uncomfortable &rarr; Safety issue &rarr; Stop.</b><br>A graded-assertiveness tool: each step conveys more urgency if the concern is not resolved.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Give the example phrase for each CUSS step.","<ul><li><b>Concerned:</b> &ldquo;I&rsquo;m concerned about this prescription.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Uncomfortable:</b> &ldquo;I&rsquo;m uncomfortable proceeding with this plan.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Safety issue:</b> &ldquo;I believe this may be a patient safety issue.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Stop:</b> &ldquo;We need to stop and reassess before continuing.&rdquo;</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What does PACE stand for, and how does it relate to CUSS?","<b>Probe, Alert, Challenge, Emergency.</b><br>Like CUSS, it is a stepwise (graded-assertiveness) escalation of concern. Both aim to give any team member, regardless of discipline, seniority or role, a structured, respectful way to voice a safety concern.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","According to the Week 7 notes, when is graded assertiveness used?","<b>When concerns are not addressed &mdash; it gives a structured way to escalate.</b><br>It can be used when something doesn&rsquo;t seem right: a prescribing decision, a monitoring issue, or a potential medication error. The practice activity asks you to write both an I-message and a CUSS step: how you would raise the concern initially, and how you would escalate if it is not addressed.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What three qualities should feedback to a colleague have in interprofessional settings?","<ul><li><b>Specific</b> and focused on behaviour</li><li><b>Respectful</b> and non-judgemental</li><li><b>Linked to patient outcomes</b> where possible</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Reword &ldquo;That plan is not appropriate&rdquo; as constructive feedback to a GP.","<b>&ldquo;I&rsquo;m concerned that this combination may increase the risk of hypoglycaemia. Could we review the dosing together?&rdquo;</b><br>Names a specific clinical concern, ties it to patient outcome, and invites joint review rather than passing judgement.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","How should you respond when you <i>receive</i> feedback from another health professional?","<ul><li><b>Listen without interruption</b></li><li><b>Ask clarifying questions</b></li><li><b>Reflect on it before responding</b></li></ul>Even challenging feedback is a chance to refine clinical reasoning.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What shift in negotiation does the Week 7 module recommend?","<b>From positions to underlying interests, and from adversary to partner.</b><br>The module says that while negotiation is often seen as a contest between opposing sides, research shows more sustainable, positive outcomes come from approaching it as a partnership rather than a battle. Key point: understanding underlying interests improves decision-making.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","The Week 7 module gives an example sequence for using feedback and negotiation together. What are its four steps?","1. <b>Raise a concern</b> about a medication choice (feedback)<br>2. <b>Explore the rationale</b> behind the decision (understanding interests)<br>3. <b>Suggest an alternative approach</b> (negotiation)<br>4. <b>Work toward a shared plan</b> (collaboration)<br>The practice scenario (GP: &ldquo;I&rsquo;d prefer to keep the current medication for now&rdquo;) asks you to express your concern, explore their reasoning and suggest an alternative, using respectful language, clear clinical reasoning and a collaborative tone.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Name four drivers of communication challenges in interprofessional teams.","<ul><li><b>Professional language</b> and communication differences</li><li><b>Power dynamics and hierarchy</b></li><li><b>Time pressure</b> and competing priorities</li><li><b>Conflict and disagreement</b></li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What does &lsquo;making your thinking visible&rsquo; mean when talking to a GP?","<b>Stating both your clinical reasoning and your concern explicitly</b>, so a colleague whose profession prioritises something different (GP: diagnostic reasoning; nurse: monitoring and coordination; pharmacist: medication optimisation and safety) does not overlook or misread it.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","How can hierarchy affect communication in a healthcare team?","<b>Reluctance to question decisions, hesitation to raise safety concerns, and unequal participation in discussions.</b><br>The notes say recognising these dynamics is the first step to communicating effectively within them.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","A GP believes your prescribing decision contributed to a patient&rsquo;s weekend symptoms. What does the course say to do?","<b>Avoid assumptions, gather more information, and reassess &mdash; not in isolation.</b><ul><li>Talk openly with the patient about their experience</li><li>Engage the GP to exchange perspectives and clarify concerns</li><li>Keep a collaborative approach to reviewing the care plan</li></ul>It may not yet be clear whether the symptoms relate to the change.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Does a bad outcome, or a GP disagreeing, mean your prescribing decision was wrong?","<b>Not necessarily.</b> Prescribing is individualised; multiple approaches may be appropriate and uncertainty is common, so different clinicians can make different but reasonable decisions. <b>Outcomes alone do not determine the quality of a decision.</b> Expanding pharmacist roles (perceived overlap in responsibilities) may also drive tension.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What are the six stages of the Gibbs Reflective Cycle?","<b>Description &rarr; Feelings &rarr; Evaluation &rarr; Analysis &rarr; Conclusion &rarr; Action planning.</b><br>Moves reflection beyond recalling events toward insights that change practice.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What are the Big Five (OCEAN) personality traits?","<b>Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism.</b><br>e.g. a highly conscientious colleague may want detailed justification, which can feel aggressive to a relationship-focused colleague; a stressed colleague may respond defensively and need a more supportive approach.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","When should you be direct, and when collaborative, with another clinician?","<b>Direct and clear for urgent safety concerns; collaborative and exploratory when negotiating a treatment plan.</b><br>Other adaptations: adjust detail to the audience, balance clarity with sensitivity, modify tone to the situation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Phoning a GP after a prescribing error &mdash; what should the call cover?","<b>Prompt, professional, factual, collaborative.</b><ul><li>What happened and how it was identified</li><li>Actual/potential harm and monitoring completed</li><li>Corrective actions, what the patient has been told, pending follow-up</li><li>Invite collaborative decision-making where uncertainty remains</li></ul>Tutorial example opening: &ldquo;Dr Chen, I&rsquo;m calling about Priya. I identified a prescribing error involving her documented penicillin allergy. She is now stable; I&rsquo;d like to update you on what happened and the actions taken.&rdquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 tutorial &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What is the difference between a person-centred and a systems-based approach to error?","<b>Person-centred: error stems from the individual. Systems-based: error arises from interactions between people, processes, environments and technologies.</b><br>Person-centred responses are retraining, reminders or discipline &mdash; they promote blame and silence, discourage reporting and rarely prevent recurrence. Systems-based focuses on redesign, open reporting and learning.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","Explain the Swiss cheese model in one sentence, and name its four healthcare layers.","<b>Each layer of defence is a slice with gaps; harm reaches the patient when the gaps align.</b><br>Layers: individual clinicians, team processes, clinical environments, organisational systems. Errors are rarely caused by a single failure.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What does the Yorkshire Contributory Factors Framework do, and what factors does it list?","<b>A systems-based framework identifying the wide range of factors that contribute to patient safety incidents.</b><br>Communication systems; staffing and workload; policies and procedures; equipment and resources; team dynamics; leadership and safety culture; physical environment; external policy influences.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What is human factors, and give examples of human-factor risks in prescribing.","<b>The study of how people interact with systems, environments, technologies and tasks.</b><br>Risks named: interruptions during prescribing, alert fatigue, unclear handover communication, workload and time pressure, assumptions about another clinician&rsquo;s actions.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What is the &lsquo;Dirty Dozen&rsquo;, and which of its factors does the Week 8 module list?","<b>A human factors framework, adapted from the transport industry, of twelve common contributors to error in high-risk industries.</b><br>Listed in the module: communication problems, fatigue, stress, teamwork issues, lack of resources, distractions and interruptions, assumptions, pressure. (The module names only these eight.)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","Which strategies does the module give for reducing Dirty Dozen risks?","<b>Structured communication, checklists, situational awareness, double-checking high-risk medicines, and supportive team cultures.</b><br>The module flags transitions of care, busy ward rounds and multidisciplinary decision-making as times when errors are likely.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What is the London Protocol and how does it differ from traditional RCA?","<b>A systems-oriented form of RCA tailored to healthcare, built around human factors.</b><br>It examines task design, team communication, work environment, equipment and technology, and organisational influences. Traditional RCA often seeks a single linear &lsquo;root cause&rsquo; and risks oversimplification or individual blame if poorly applied; the London Protocol assumes multiple interacting factors.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","Name the four complementary approaches to analysing errors covered in Week 8, with the one-line purpose of each.","<ul><li><b>RCA</b> &mdash; formal, step-by-step investigation of serious events to find system causes</li><li><b>SEA</b> &mdash; flexible, reflective, team-based learning in everyday practice</li><li><b>Human factors analysis</b> &mdash; how system conditions shape performance</li><li><b>Sensemaking</b> &mdash; how decisions made sense to clinicians at the time</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","RCA is used after which kinds of event?","<b>Significant adverse events, near misses with potential for harm, and sentinel events.</b><br>Most useful when a detailed investigation is needed, or formal, regulatory or organisational governance requirements must be met.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","List the five key principles of effective RCA.","<ul><li>Focus on systems, not individuals</li><li>Use a structured, systematic process</li><li>Base conclusions on evidence, not assumptions</li><li>Aim to identify multiple contributing factors</li><li>Develop practical, sustainable solutions</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","Put the eight steps of Root Cause Analysis in order.","<b>1 Define the problem &rarr; 2 Gather information &rarr; 3 Identify contributing factors &rarr; 4 Identify root causes &rarr; 5 Develop solutions &rarr; 6 Implement the action plan &rarr; 7 Monitor and evaluate &rarr; 8 Share learning</b><br>The timeline built in step 2 is &lsquo;the backbone of the analysis&rsquo;.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","In RCA, what is the difference between a contributing factor and a root cause?","<b>Contributing factors are conditions that made the event more likely; root causes are the underlying factors that, if addressed, would reduce the likelihood of recurrence.</b><br>Root causes are found by analysing the contributing factors (e.g. with 5 Whys or a fishbone diagram).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","RCA solutions should have which five qualities?","<b>Specific, actionable, system-level, realistic, measurable.</b><br>Examples: new workflows, training, policy updates, redesigned communication processes.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","Contrast the 5 Whys and the fishbone diagram.","<b>5 Whys: ask &lsquo;why&rsquo; repeatedly to drill past surface issues to deeper system causes. Fishbone (cause-and-effect): sort possible causes into categories.</b><br>Fishbone categories given: people, processes, environment, equipment, policies.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","In the James Lee / Mr Hughes RCA case, where did the 5 Whys chain end?","<b>At the organisation: no formal onboarding, training or collaborative planning when the pharmacist prescriber joined the team.</b><br>Chain: vague &lsquo;review meds&rsquo; note &rarr; no structured communication template &rarr; no handover protocol &rarr; roles and workflows never clarified &rarr; no onboarding. The model answer concludes the breakdown was systemic, not individual error.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","Which four root causes did the model RCA identify in the Mr Hughes case?","<ul><li>No structured communication and handover system</li><li>Ambiguity in professional roles and expectations</li><li>Inadequate documentation standards</li><li>No shared care workflow for medication reviews</li></ul>Fixes included SBAR-style requests embedded in the EHR, a mandatory urgency field (routine / same day / immediate), daily huddles and teach-back with the patient.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What counts as a &lsquo;significant event&rsquo; for SEA?","<b>Any event that any member of the team believes is important enough to reflect on and learn from.</b><br>Includes prescribing errors, delayed follow-up of abnormal results, communication breakdowns, near misses &mdash; and examples of <b>excellent</b> teamwork or care. Consequences can range from death to none.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","Put the seven stages of Significant Event Analysis in order.","<b>1 Awareness and prioritisation &rarr; 2 Information gathering &rarr; 3 Facilitated team-based meeting &rarr; 4 Analysis &rarr; 5 Agree, implement and monitor change &rarr; 6 Write it up &rarr; 7 Report, share and review</b><br>Note the team meeting (3) comes before the formal analysis (4). The module describes SEA as a qualitative method of clinical audit.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What four questions guide the analysis stage of SEA?","<ul><li>What happened?</li><li>Why did it happen? (5 Whys or fishbone may help)</li><li>What has been learned?</li><li>What has been changed or actioned?</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What makes the SEA team meeting work, and what must come out of it?","<b>An open, fair, honest, non-threatening atmosphere &mdash; psychological safety.</b><br>Where there is fear of blame or punishment, team members are reluctant to participate. Minutes outlining learning points and actions for individual staff should always be taken and circulated to all staff; every SEA meeting should start by reviewing the agreed actions from the previous meeting&rsquo;s minutes, with a timescale for change built in.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","In the SEA amoxicillin-clavulanate near miss, which contributing factors were identified?","<ul><li><b>Individual:</b> focus on acute symptoms, assumed allergy already verified, cognitive load</li><li><b>Task:</b> allergy check not a mandatory step</li><li><b>Technology:</b> passive allergy display, no prompt for type, severity or timing</li><li><b>Environment:</b> busy, interruptions, limited time</li><li><b>Organisational:</b> no local allergy-verification process; inconsistent documentation</li></ul>The dispensing pharmacist was the safety barrier.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What is sensemaking, and what idea about clinicians&rsquo; actions underpins it?","<b>A structured, reflective post-event conversation exploring how events made sense to those involved at the time.</b><br>It assumes most actions are <b>locally rational</b> within complex systems, so it explores goals, information available, assumptions, time pressures and competing priorities &mdash; reducing hindsight bias and blame. It complements, not replaces, RCA, the London Protocol and human factors models.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What is the &lsquo;second victim&rsquo;, and what responses are common?","<b>A clinician who experiences emotional harm after involvement in an adverse event or error (the patient is the first victim).</b><br>Common: guilt, shame, anxiety, self-doubt, fear of consequences, reduced confidence; replaying the event, over-caution, avoidance, sleep disturbance, burnout. Burnout and poor wellbeing can themselves worsen patient safety.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","What support strategies does the module suggest after you are involved in an error?","<b>Talk to trusted colleagues or supervisors; reflective practice or debriefing; mentorship or supervision; professional counselling or employee support services; focus on learning, not self-blame.</b><br>A positive safety culture encourages reporting, focuses on learning not punishment and provides debriefing; blame cultures discourage disclosure and increase burnout.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","Give the eight-step order of the cardiovascular assessment from the intensive checklist.","<b>Preparation &rarr; general inspection &rarr; hands &rarr; pulses &amp; BP &rarr; face &rarr; chest inspection &rarr; palpation &rarr; auscultation.</b><br>Pulses = radial rate + rhythm, radio-radial delay, then BP. Palpation = apex beat (position + character).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 cardiovascular exam handout &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","What do you do before you touch the patient in a cardiovascular examination?","<b>Hand hygiene &middot; introduce yourself &middot; explain in patient-friendly language &middot; obtain consent &middot; ask about pain &middot; position at about 45&deg; &middot; expose chest and lower legs with dignity &middot; offer a chaperone where appropriate.</b><br>Asking about pain or discomfort first matters because it can affect the assessment. Position at 45&deg; unless contraindicated or otherwise clinically indicated.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","What do you inspect for in the hands, and what is a normal capillary refill time?","<b>Colour, tar staining, xanthomata, clubbing; compare temperature; capillary refill time &mdash; normal is less than 3 seconds.</b><br>Tar staining is a clue to smoking/nicotine. The Week 2 Feel page states an abnormal CRT is an indication to <b>seek urgent medical attention</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","What do you look for in the face during a cardiovascular examination?","<b>Eyes:</b> conjunctival pallor, corneal arcus, xanthelasma.<br><b>Mouth:</b> central cyanosis, angular stomatitis/cheilitis, high-arched palate, dental hygiene/dentition.<br>A <b>high-arched palate</b> is a feature of <b>Marfan syndrome</b>, which is associated with mitral/aortic valve prolapse and aortic dissection.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","How should stethoscope earpieces sit, and how do you use a tunable diaphragm?","<b>Earpieces angled forward, toward the nose</b> &mdash; aligns with the ear canals for a tight seal and less external noise.<br><b>Tunable diaphragm: vary the pressure, don&rsquo;t twist the chest piece.</b> Light pressure accentuates lower-pitched sounds; firmer pressure helps detect higher-pitched sounds (&lsquo;tunable, not twistable&rsquo;).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","What produces S1 and S2, and which valves are which?","<b>S1 (&lsquo;lub&rsquo;) = atrioventricular valves closing at the start of systole. S2 (&lsquo;dub&rsquo;) = semilunar valves closing at the start of diastole.</b><br>Atrioventricular: tricuspid (right atrium&ndash;right ventricle), mitral (left atrium&ndash;left ventricle). Semilunar: aortic (left ventricle&ndash;aorta), pulmonary (right ventricle&ndash;pulmonary artery).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","Where do you begin cardiac auscultation, and why listen over several areas?","<b>Begin in the aortic area &mdash; 2nd intercostal space, just right of the sternum (patient&rsquo;s right, your left) &mdash; with the diaphragm.</b><br>Different sounds radiate to different parts of the chest; moving systematically shows where a murmur is <b>loudest</b>, which points to its likely origin. Normal finding documented as <b>HSDNM</b> &mdash; heart sounds dual, no murmurs.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","You hear a murmur. What is the first thing to decide about it?","<b>Is it systolic (between S1 and S2, &lsquo;lub-shhhh-dub&rsquo;) or diastolic (after S2, &lsquo;lub-dub-shhhh&rsquo;)?</b><br>This narrows the differential and the need for investigation. Diastolic murmurs are subtler and harder to detect. Tricuspid murmurs are quiet, best at the lower left sternal border, and often need echocardiography.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","Describe the murmur of aortic stenosis and who gets it.","<b>Loud ejection systolic (crescendo&ndash;decrescendo) murmur, loudest in the aortic area (2nd ICS, right sternal border).</b><br>Older adults (age-related calcification) or younger patients with a <b>congenital bicuspid aortic valve</b> (two leaflets, calcifies earlier). Severe cases may have a palpable <b>thrill</b> over the aortic area.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","Suspected aortic stenosis: which symptoms do you ask about, and what happens next?","<b>Exertional chest pain, breathlessness, reduced exercise tolerance, dizziness, presyncope or collapse. Refer for medical assessment &mdash; echocardiography confirms diagnosis and severity.</b><br>Course example wording: &ldquo;I can hear a loud systolic murmur that is most prominent in the aortic area. This may represent aortic stenosis. Further assessment by a GP or cardiologist is indicated, and an echocardiogram should be considered.&rdquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","You detect a murmur. What four follow-up questions does the course suggest?","<ul><li>&ldquo;Have you ever been told that you have a heart murmur?&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;Have you previously been assessed by a cardiologist?&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;Have you ever had an echocardiogram (heart ultrasound)?&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;Do you experience chest pain, shortness of breath, dizziness, or episodes of fainting?&rdquo;</li></ul>They separate a <b>known</b> finding from one needing further review.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","How do you find the apex beat, and what does a displaced one suggest?","<b>Patient at about 45&deg;; count down to the 5th intercostal space; palpate at the midclavicular line with palm or finger pads. If hard to feel, roll the patient slightly onto their left side.</b><br>Normal: 5th ICS at or close to the MCL (nipple only a rough guide). <b>Laterally displaced = cardiomegaly from left ventricular enlargement</b>, commonly with heart failure &mdash; possibly one of the earliest clues.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","How do you assess for pitting oedema, and what else completes the fluid-status picture?","<b>Firm pressure on the medial shin for about 3 seconds; note whether an indentation remains and how far up it extends</b> (ankles = mild; mid-shin or knees = more significant).<br>Then ask about <b>recent weight change</b> (rapid gain over days&ndash;weeks) and <b>functional capacity</b> (&ldquo;How far could you normally walk? How far can you walk now?&rdquo;). Auscultate the <b>lung bases</b> for inspiratory crackles (pulmonary congestion).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","How do you palpate the radial pulse, and what three features do you assess?","<b>Index and middle fingers where the thumb meets the wrist &mdash; never your thumb (it has its own pulse).</b><br>Assess <b>rate</b> (full 60 s preferred, especially if irregular), <b>rhythm</b> (regular vs irregular; irregularly irregular suggests AF), and <b>quality</b> (volume/character: normal, bounding, weak or thready).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","What is radio-radial delay, how do you test for it, and what can cause it?","<b>Loss of synchronicity between the two radial pulses. Palpate both radials simultaneously &mdash; normally they coincide.</b><br>Uncommon; suggests obstruction or narrowing of flow to one upper limb: <b>coarctation of the aorta, subclavian or brachiocephalic artery disease, or external compression of a major vessel</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","An irregularly irregular pulse: which features should prompt referral?","<b>New-onset AF, symptomatic AF, or AF with a ventricular rate greater than 100 beats/min.</b><br>Management considerations named: rate control, rhythm control, and assessment of stroke risk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","How should an automated BP cuff be sized and placed?","<b>Bladder encircles 80&ndash;100% of arm circumference; snug, lower edge about 2&ndash;3 cm above the antecubital fossa; sensor/artery marker over the brachial artery.</b><br>Correct sensor placement matters more than lining up the printed marker exactly. Either arm can be used; the right arm is the usual standard.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","Does systolic or diastolic BP matter more &mdash; and does age change the answer?","<b>Under 50: elevated diastolic is often the more important predictor of CV risk. 50 and over: systolic is more strongly associated with outcomes and becomes the primary treatment target.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","Why are acute rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease relevant to CVD assessment in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander patients?","<b>They disproportionately affect Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. ARF can follow untreated Group A Streptococcus infection &mdash; including skin infections such as impetigo &mdash; and recurrences can cause permanent valve damage (RHD).</b><br>Recognising and managing skin infections and identifying a history of ARF/RHD are part of preventing long-term CV complications.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 1 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","CAC score is 0 and treatment is deferred. When is CAC repeated &mdash; and after a positive score leads to a statin?","<b>CAC = 0: reassessment in roughly 3&ndash;7 years for selected borderline/intermediate-risk adults &mdash; years, not months. Once lipid-lowering is started because CAC is positive, routine repeat CAC is generally not indicated.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 1 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["lipid","How does the Dutch Lipid Clinic Network Score grade the untreated LDL-C, and how is the total interpreted?","<table><tr><th>Untreated LDL-C (mmol/L)</th><th>Points</th></tr><tr><td>&ge;8.5</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>6.5&ndash;8.4</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>5.0&ndash;6.4</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>4.0&ndash;4.9</td><td>1</td></tr></table><b>Total: &gt;8 definite FH &middot; 6&ndash;8 probable &middot; 3&ndash;5 possible &middot; &lt;3 unlikely.</b><br>Premature = men &lt;55, women &lt;60. Own premature CAD 2; premature cerebral/peripheral disease 1; first-degree relative with premature disease 1.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Dutch Lipid Clinic Network score (PHAR6302) &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["lipid","Why is non-HDL-C useful when triglycerides are significantly elevated?","<b>It includes atherogenic triglyceride-rich lipoproteins as well as LDL-C, so may better reflect ASCVD risk than LDL-C alone.</b><br>Its target is less extensively evaluated than LDL-C&rsquo;s, but it is a useful <b>secondary target</b> when triglycerides are high.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["wt","Which waist circumference cut-offs differ by ethnicity in TG Table 3.3?","<b>Males, increased risk: Caucasian 94 cm; South Asian, Chinese or Japanese 90 cm.</b> Substantially increased: 102 cm. Females: 80 / 88 cm.<br>Figures are for European-origin populations; <b>higher cut-offs may be considered in Pacific peoples and lower cut-offs for Aboriginal males.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 4 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["wt","What is the Edmonton Obesity Staging System, and how do its treatment tiers map to stages?","<b>Four progressive stages (absence of disease &rarr; end-stage) across medical, mental and functional health.</b><br>Tier <b>A</b> lifestyle &mdash; all stages. Tier <b>B</b> medical intervention &mdash; started at <b>Stage 2</b>. Tier <b>C</b> surgery &mdash; considered at <b>Stages 3 and 4</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 4 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["wt","A patient on a GLP-1 RA is having an elective colonoscopy. Is it withheld in Australia?","<b>No. GLP-1 RAs are NOT withheld for at least one dosing interval in Australia. The 2025 ADS/ANZCA/GESA/NACOS recommendations advise against elective pre-procedural cessation &mdash; 24-hour clear-fluid diet, then standard 6-hour fasting.</b><br>The course notes the US position differs.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 4 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["wt","What muscle risk comes with GLP-1 weight loss, who is most at risk, and what do you advise?","<b>Loss of lean mass, including skeletal muscle &rarr; sarcopenia</b>, particularly in older adults and with rapid weight loss.<br>Advise <b>adequate protein intake</b> and <b>regular resistance/strength-based exercise</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 4 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["smoke","Which quit-history questions should a smoking assessment include?","<b>What has been tried previously? What worked well? What led to relapse?</b> Also ask about <b>passive smoke</b>.<br>Each attempt gives insight into triggers, barriers and effective strategies; more than half of smokers make at least one serious quit attempt each year.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["smoke","Should escalation (including to vaping) be triggered by a set number of failed quit attempts?","<b>No. Base it on the individual&rsquo;s smoking history, prior treatment experience, preferences and likelihood of benefit &mdash; not a fixed number of unsuccessful attempts.</b><br>What counts as an &lsquo;unsuccessful&rsquo; attempt is not clear-cut, and many people need multiple attempts.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["smoke","What is cytisinicline (cytisine), and what is its evidence?","<b>A plant-derived, nicotine-free partial agonist of the &alpha;4&beta;2 nicotinic acetylcholine receptor &mdash; varenicline was developed from studying it.</b><br>Reduces craving and withdrawal and the pleasure of smoking. Phase III <b>ORCA-2 and ORCA-3</b>: significantly higher abstinence than placebo, good tolerability. Used in parts of Europe for over 50 years.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["smoke","How does cytisine compare with NRT and varenicline, and why might it cause less nausea?","<b>NICE: more effective than NRT; no difference in effectiveness vs varenicline; reportedly better tolerated (less nausea).</b><br>Thought to be due to <b>lower binding affinity for 5-HT<sub>3A</sub> receptors</b>, whose activation is associated with nausea. Useful for patients wanting nicotine-free help.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["smoke","A patient on clozapine switches completely from smoking to vaping. Does the CYP1A2 interaction still matter?","<b>Yes &mdash; manage it like stopping smoking.</b> Vapes don&rsquo;t burn tobacco, so don&rsquo;t produce the polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons that induce CYP1A2. Expect changed metabolism of CYP1A2 substrates (e.g. <b>clozapine, olanzapine, caffeine</b>).<br>Tutorial 3 Case 1 (Jason, stable on clozapine) asks exactly this: &lsquo;What concerns arise when smoking patterns change?&rsquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["smoke","What is the critique (Simon Chapman) of claims that vaping is the most effective cessation tool?","<b>RCTs show somewhat higher quit rates than NRT or minimal support, but absolute differences are small.</b> Critics argue trial conditions (intensive support, free product, incentives, selective recruitment) differ from real life, population data are less convincing, and most former smokers historically quit <b>unassisted</b>.<br>The course presents this as a perspective, not settled doctrine.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["copd","How is a COPD exacerbation defined?","<b>Acute worsening of respiratory symptoms &mdash; breathlessness, cough and/or sputum &mdash; developing over 14 days or less.</b><br>May come with raised respiratory and heart rate; triggered by infection, air pollution or other irritants. Lung-function loss is often not fully recovered.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 6 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["copd","What is the strongest predictor of future COPD exacerbations, and what numbers back it up?","<b>Previous exacerbation history.</b><br>One moderate exacerbation: ~21% higher risk of future COPD hospitalisation. &ge;2 per year: about double the rate of exacerbation-free patients. Current drugs prevent only about one-quarter to one-third of exacerbations.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 6 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["copd","Why is a COPD exacerbation a cardiovascular event too?","<b>Even one moderate exacerbation can more than double the risk of myocardial infarction in the days immediately after &mdash; and CVD causes more than half of COPD-related deaths.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 6 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["copd","How do blood eosinophils guide ICS in COPD, and when should they be measured?","<b>&ge;300 cells/&micro;L: greatest ICS benefit. 100&ndash;300: may benefit depending on exacerbation history. &lt;100: little benefit and increased pneumonia risk.</b><br>Measure when <b>clinically stable</b> and <b>not on systemic corticosteroids</b> (they markedly suppress eosinophils). GOLD uses eosinophils alongside exacerbation history.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 6 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["reas","Name the five steps of the structured clinical reasoning process (RACGP 2012) used in the PHAR6202 Week 5 tutorial.","<b>1 Clinical history &rarr; 2 Working differential &rarr; 3 Physical examination &rarr; 4 Investigations &rarr; 5 Management plan.</b><br>History generates and tests hypotheses; exam is directed to support or refute them; investigations only where they add information. Iterative &mdash; new information can send you back.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 tutorial &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["reas","Tutorial case: Michael, 49, &lsquo;indigestion&rsquo;, wants stronger reflux treatment. Which new features should switch you from Type 1 to Type 2 thinking?","<b>Discomfort while walking uphill, pressure-like rather than burning, settles after stopping for a few minutes, nausea during an episode</b> &mdash; plus treated hypertension (perindopril) and ex-smoker.<br>The tutorial&rsquo;s point: the GORD first impression was reasonable but incomplete. The risk is <b>anchoring and premature closure</b> &mdash; not revising when the picture stops fitting.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 tutorial &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What does the Dr Jo Taylor intensive session say about interrupting the patient?","<b>Clinicians typically interrupt after 11&ndash;18 seconds; aim to avoid interrupting for 60 seconds.</b><br>Interrupting misses information, loses rapport, and loses context (how symptoms affect daily life, fears, expectations). Her framing of shared decision-making: like a game of tennis &mdash; taking turns, not coercion.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 tutorial &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Penicillin-allergic patient given amoxicillin by you: what opening line to the patient does the tutorial model?","<b>Patient:</b> &ldquo;Priya, I&rsquo;m sorry. I prescribed a medicine that you are allergic to. I&rsquo;d like to explain what we know, what we&rsquo;ve done, and answer your questions.&rdquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 tutorial &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","PHAR6302 Assessment 3, Part A &mdash; what is the ISBAR handover letter for, and what is it <i>not</i> for?","<b>To communicate a patient assessment and recommendations to the GP in a concise, clinically relevant and actionable manner.</b><br>It is <i>not</i> to document everything known about the patient &mdash; it must show effective prioritisation of information, written as a genuine clinical communication.<br>It should identify and communicate key patient issues, assessment findings, recommendations, and follow-up considerations.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Assessment 3 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Assessment 3 has a patient safety requirement. Which kinds of recommendation result in a fail grade regardless of the rest of the work?","<b>Any recommendation that could reasonably result in:</b><ul><li>patient harm</li><li>delayed escalation of serious illness</li><li>inappropriate prescribing outside the pharmacist&rsquo;s scope of practice</li><li>breaches of patient confidentiality</li><li>practice inconsistent with legal, regulatory or professional requirements</li></ul>Reasoning must be safe, evidence-based and consistent with current Australian guidelines, legislation, professional standards and pharmacist scope.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Assessment 3 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Assessment 3 &mdash; which patient may you write the handover about?","<b>One real, fully deidentified patient with asthma or COPD.</b><br>They should have presented with respiratory symptoms, suboptimal disease control, medication-related issues, adherence challenges, inhaler technique concerns, risk of exacerbation, or another clinical issue requiring communication with a GP.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Assessment 3 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","What does each SBAR element hold, as the course defines it?","<table><tr><th>S</th><td>Situation &mdash; what is happening?</td></tr><tr><th>B</th><td>Background &mdash; relevant history, medicines, comorbidities</td></tr><tr><th>A</th><td>Assessment &mdash; likely differentials, clinical judgement</td></tr><tr><th>R</th><td>Recommendation &mdash; what is needed next</td></tr></table>Note: the course materials define S, B, A and R only. The leading <b>I</b> of ISBAR is not defined anywhere in the course materials &mdash; do not fill it in from memory.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Per the course, what is SBAR used for &mdash; and where does it sit alongside OLDCARTS, VINDICATES and SOAP?","<b>Used for interprofessional consultation, GP referrals, and documenting reasoning in the patient record.</b><br>The Week 5 example (a patient with dizziness) combines them: OLDCARTS to explore the symptom, VINDICATES to consider causes, SOAP to organise reasoning, then <b>SBAR to communicate with the GP</b> if needed.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Mrs Santos example SBAR &mdash; what did the course put in the <b>Situation</b>?","<b>Why she presented now and the key current numbers.</b><br>Presented with her daughter requesting a BP check for morning dizziness; home BP approximately 150&ndash;160/95&ndash;100 mmHg; pharmacy readings 165/98 mmHg (sitting) and 162/96 mmHg (after 10 minutes); currently on perindopril 5 mg once daily with inconsistent adherence and a persistent cough.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Mrs Santos example SBAR &mdash; what kinds of information went in <b>Background</b>?","<b>Context the GP needs to interpret the situation.</b><ul><li>Communication needs: limited English, prefers simple language, daughter assists</li><li>Known hypertension on perindopril 5 mg daily</li><li>Missed/delayed doses; ACE inhibitor&ndash;associated cough contributing to non-adherence</li><li>Limited understanding of hypertension risks</li><li>Herbal tea twice daily</li><li>Home BP monitor checked &mdash; device and cuff appropriate</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Mrs Santos example SBAR &mdash; how does the <b>Assessment</b> differ from the Background?","<b>Background lists the facts; Assessment gives your clinical judgement on them.</b><br>Assessment: uncontrolled hypertension, consistent across home and pharmacy readings. Likely contributing factors: suboptimal adherence, ACE inhibitor cough reducing adherence, low health literacy. Herbal tea judged unlikely to be contributing. Morning dizziness may relate to elevated BP or medication timing/fasting state. Communication considerations (simplified speech, teach-back, daughter support) also recorded here.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Mrs Santos example SBAR &mdash; list the five headings the <b>Recommendation</b> was organised under.","<b>Medication adjustment &bull; Monitoring &bull; Adherence support &bull; Lifestyle advice &bull; Follow-up.</b><br>Course example: recommend switching perindopril to an ARB due to cough and adherence issues; daily home BP at the same time for 1 week; teach-back education; salt, diet and activity advice that is culturally appropriate; review in 1 week to assess BP and response.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","In the Week 1 CVD case, what does the course say you request before sending a summary of the pharmacy consultation to the patient&rsquo;s GP?","<b>Request the patient&rsquo;s consent to send it.</b><br>In the Week 1 CVD case (Melissa), the suggested communication uses SBAR and makes recommendations to the GP &mdash; there, for pathology tests the GP could arrange.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Assessment 3, Part B &mdash; what four questions must the critical reflection answer?","<ul><li>How did you determine what was important to include in the handover?</li><li>What was challenging about the clinical decision-making process?</li><li>What did you learn about communication with other healthcare professionals?</li><li>What knowledge, skills or areas of practice require further development?</li></ul>It is <b>not a case summary</b>. Frameworks suggested (not mandatory): Gibbs, Driscoll (What? So What? Now What?), Sch&ouml;n (reflection-in-action / on-action).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Assessment 3 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["asthma","A 28-year-old has an FEV<sub>1</sub> of 2.50 L, rising to 2.75 L after salbutamol. What does this show?",["No positive response &mdash; FEV<sub>1</sub> rose 250 mL but only 10%","A positive bronchodilator response, since FEV<sub>1</sub> rose by more than 200 mL","A positive response, since any rise &ge;10% counts","Asthma is excluded"],0,"<b>Both criteria are needed:</b> &ge;200 mL AND &ge;12%. 250 mL is only 10% of 2.50 L, so this does not meet the threshold. The tempting second option uses volume alone. A negative test does not exclude asthma either &mdash; consider repeat spirometry, FeNO or specialist referral.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","A patient with suspected asthma has inconclusive spirometry and a normal FeNO. What is the correct interpretation?",["Asthma is ruled out","A normal FeNO does not exclude asthma; further investigation may be needed","FeNO confirms COPD","Spirometry should not be repeated"],1,"A high FeNO supports asthma, but <b>a normal FeNO does not rule it out</b> (sensitivity only 0.61 at &ge;40 ppb). Repeat spirometry, peak flow monitoring, bronchial provocation testing or specialist referral may be needed.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","A patient scores 17 on the Asthma Control Test. How is this classified?",["Well controlled","Very poorly controlled","Cannot interpret &mdash; the ACT covers only one week","Poorly controlled"],3,"ACT 15&ndash;19 = <b>poorly controlled</b>; 20&ndash;25 well controlled; &lt;15 very poorly controlled. The ACT covers 4 weeks &mdash; it is the ACQ5 that covers the previous week.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","A patient reports no daytime symptoms or night waking this month, but had an ED presentation and an oral corticosteroid course 3 months ago. What is the best assessment?",["Well controlled and low risk &mdash; no change needed","Poor current control only","Good current control but elevated future risk","Low risk, since the exacerbation was more than 4 weeks ago"],2,"Current control and future risk are assessed <b>separately</b>. Previous exacerbations are the strongest predictor of future risk, so good symptoms do not mean low risk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","A patient with known asthma presents with sudden breathlessness and sharp right-sided chest pain. Reliever has not helped. Breath sounds are reduced on the right. Most likely concern?",["Moderate asthma exacerbation &mdash; step up to medium-dose MART","Inducible laryngeal obstruction","Pneumothorax","GORD"],2,"Sudden onset, unilateral pleuritic pain, asymmetrical breath sounds and poor bronchodilator response are classic red flags for <b>pneumothorax</b>. Escalating asthma therapy would miss it; ILO causes inspiratory noise and throat tightness, not unilateral signs.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","On auscultation of a patient with suspected asthma, which finding most strongly suggests an alternative or additional diagnosis?",["Widespread expiratory wheeze","No wheeze between episodes","Wheeze that improves after reliever","Crackles"],3,"<b>Crackles are not a feature of asthma.</b> Normal findings between episodes are common in asthma, and widespread expiratory wheeze when symptomatic may suggest asthma.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","A patient&rsquo;s personal best peak flow is 500 L/min. Today it is 230 L/min. Which zone?",["Red &mdash; medical emergency","Green","Yellow","Cannot tell without population reference values"],0,"230 &divide; 500 = 46%, which is <b>&lt;50% of personal best = red zone</b>: follow emergency instructions and seek urgent care. Yellow is 50&ndash;79%. Peak flow is read against personal best, not population values.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","An asthma patient on low-dose MART still has frequent symptoms. What is the FIRST step before stepping up?",["Step up to medium-dose MART immediately","Add montelukast","Check adherence, inhaler technique, triggers, smoking/vaping, comorbidities and alternative diagnoses","Refer to a respiratory physician for a biologic"],2,"Week 7: poor control is often due to adherence, technique, triggers or another diagnosis rather than inadequate pharmacotherapy. Check these before increasing intensity.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","Asthma has been well controlled for 3 months with low exacerbation risk. She is 10 weeks pregnant. Should you step down now?",["Yes &mdash; criteria are met","No &mdash; avoid stepping down during pregnancy","Yes, but stop ICS completely","No &mdash; step-down needs 12 months of control"],1,"Under the QLD asthma protocol a pregnant patient is <b>ineligible</b> for the Program &mdash; refer. Clinically, step-down criteria are met, but Week 7 lists <b>pregnancy</b> (with respiratory infections, travel, other high-risk periods) as times to avoid stepping down. ICS is never completely withdrawn.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","A patient on budesonide&ndash;formoterol AIR uses it 4 times a week, and occasionally before exercise. Under Week 7&rsquo;s earlier-review triggers, does reliever frequency alone mandate earlier review?",["No &mdash; the &gt;twice-weekly trigger excludes pre-exercise dosing and anti-inflammatory reliever regimens","Yes &mdash; any reliever use more than twice a week","Yes &mdash; any pre-exercise use requires review","No &mdash; only &ge;12 canisters a year matters"],0,"The trigger is a rapid-acting bronchodilator <b>more than twice a week, outside pre-exercise dosing or an anti-inflammatory reliever regimen</b>. Still assess the four control indicators &mdash; frequent use may reflect poor control.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","A patient&rsquo;s exacerbation has caused persistently worsening symptoms for 3 days with falling peak flow and increased reliever needs. They can speak in full sentences. How is it classified?",["Mild","Severe","Not an exacerbation &mdash; within normal variation","Moderate"],3,"<b>Moderate:</b> persistent worsening over two or more days, increasing reliever need, deteriorating lung function, needs a treatment change. Severe means no improvement despite repeated reliever doses and significant breathing difficulty (inability to finish a sentence in one breath needs urgent assessment).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["asthma","When reviewing an Aboriginal patient&rsquo;s asthma care, who determines whether the care was culturally safe?",["The treating pharmacist","The patient","The ACCHO","The GP"],1,"Week 7: in cultural safety, <b>the patient &mdash; not the clinician &mdash;</b> determines whether care has been culturally safe. ACCHOs and Aboriginal Health Workers are partners in collaborative care.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","During a diabetes review, a patient tells you she has been thinking about ending her life and has a plan. What is the most appropriate action?",["Give her a crisis line number and suggest she books a GP appointment this week","Complete a PHQ-9 and review the score at her next visit","Escalate urgently &mdash; contact her GP urgently, crisis services, ED or ambulance as appropriate","Avoid further questions about suicide so as not to increase her risk"],2,"<b>Active suicidal thoughts or intent is high concern and requires urgent assessment and escalation.</b> Simply advising her to seek help independently is explicitly what the module says not to do for a patient at significant risk. Asking directly does not increase risk, and a screening tool does not replace urgent action.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","A patient reports low mood, nervousness and fatigue, and you are unsure whether anxiety, depression or something else is driving it. Which tool best fits?",["PHQ-9","K10","GAD-7","DASS used to make a diagnosis"],1,"<b>K10</b> measures general psychological distress and is useful when it is not clear whether anxiety, depression or another issue is contributing. PHQ-9 is for suspected depression and GAD-7 for suspected anxiety. No screening tool establishes a diagnosis.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","A colleague says asking a distressed patient whether they have thought about suicide may &lsquo;put the idea in their head&rsquo;. Which response reflects the Week 8 content?",["Asking directly and respectfully does not increase the likelihood of self-harm","Only psychiatrists should ask about suicide","Remind the patient how much it would hurt their family instead","Ask only if the patient raises it first"],0,"Evidence shows asking directly and respectfully about suicidal thoughts <b>does not increase risk</b>. Guilt-inducing strategies (how much it would hurt family) are tempting but may stop the person talking openly to a helper in future.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","A patient with COPD has had persistent anxiety for months that now stops her doing daily activities and managing her inhalers. No safety concerns. What level of concern and action fit best?",["Lower &mdash; education and reassurance only","High &mdash; emergency department referral","Moderate &mdash; refer to GP for further assessment, discuss support services and set a follow-up plan","No concern &mdash; distress is not mental illness"],2,"Persistent symptoms affecting daily functioning and self-management match the module&rsquo;s <b>moderate</b> examples, whose actions include GP referral, discussing support services, team communication and a follow-up plan. Without safety concerns or psychosis this is not high concern; distress still warrants action even if not a mental illness.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","On the module&rsquo;s ranking, which antipsychotic carries the <b>highest</b> risk of weight gain?",["Aripiprazole","Haloperidol","Risperidone","Olanzapine"],3,"<b>Olanzapine</b> heads the list, followed by clozapine. Risperidone/paliperidone sits 7th of 17; aripiprazole and haloperidol are among the lowest (haloperidol last).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Which phrase uses person-centred language?",["Her urine screen was clean","He is a drug addict","She had a negative urine drug screen","He is non-compliant with his medicines"],2,"&lsquo;Negative/positive urine drug screen&rsquo; replaces &lsquo;clean/dirty&rsquo;. &lsquo;Drug addict&rsquo; becomes &lsquo;person living with opioid dependence&rsquo;; &lsquo;non-compliant&rsquo; becomes &lsquo;experiencing challenges with treatment adherence&rsquo;.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Before giving a K10, you explain why you are using it and how the results will be used. Which trauma-informed principle is this?",["Trustworthiness","Empowerment","Choice","Safety"],0,"<b>Trustworthiness</b> includes explaining the purpose of screening tools and why questions are asked. Choice is about asking permission and respecting refusal; safety is private space and explaining what will happen in the consultation; empowerment is building on strengths.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Patient: &lsquo;I feel exhausted all the time.&rsquo; Which response is a <b>reflection</b> in OARS?",["&lsquo;Have you tried going to bed earlier?&rsquo;","&lsquo;It sounds like your symptoms have been making daily activities much more difficult.&rsquo;","&lsquo;You&rsquo;ve already taken an important step by seeking help today.&rsquo;","&lsquo;Are you taking your medicines properly?&rsquo;"],1,"Reflective listening mirrors the patient&rsquo;s experience so they feel understood &mdash; this is the module&rsquo;s own example. &lsquo;You&rsquo;ve already taken an important step&hellip;&rsquo; is an <b>affirmation</b> &mdash; also OARS, but a different element. &lsquo;Have you tried going to bed earlier?&rsquo; is advice; &lsquo;Are you taking your medicines properly?&rsquo; is the closed question the module says to replace with an open one.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","While discussing COPD, a patient becomes tearful: &lsquo;I just don&rsquo;t seem to cope like I used to.&rsquo; What is the best first response?",["&lsquo;You shouldn&rsquo;t worry about that.&rsquo;","&lsquo;You need to see a psychologist.&rsquo;","Move quickly on to inhaler technique to keep to time","Pause, acknowledge the emotion, allow silence, and check whether they want to continue"],3,"Managing distress: pause, acknowledge emotions, allow silence, stay non-judgemental, check they want to continue. Telling them not to worry dismisses the concern; a directive referral before exploring tends to shut the conversation down.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","A patient&rsquo;s PHQ-9 score is high. Which is the correct use of this result?",["It confirms a diagnosis of depression","Interpret it alongside history, presentation, functioning and context","It replaces the need for a mental health history","Screening tools are only useful once, at diagnosis"],1,"Screening tools <b>do not establish a diagnosis</b> and should never be used in isolation or replace clinical judgement. They also help monitor change over time.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","An older patient with COPD has taken diazepam most evenings for several years. Which set of concerns does the module highlight for benzodiazepines?",["Dependence, sedation, falls risk, impaired concentration, cognitive impairment","Weight gain and dyslipidaemia only","Hyperglycaemia and QT prolongation only","No concern if the dose is stable"],0,"The module lists <b>dependence, sedation, falls, impaired concentration and cognitive impairment</b>, especially relevant in older adults with multiple conditions; long-term use may require regular review. Weight/metabolic effects are the module&rsquo;s antipsychotic concerns.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["mh","Which action best reflects culturally safe care for an Aboriginal patient, per Week 8?",["Focus straight on clinical issues to save the patient time","Assume family should be involved in every decision","Build rapport before focusing on clinical issues and respect the role of family and community where appropriate","Use the K10 in place of a conversation"],2,"Culturally safe care includes building rapport first, shared decision-making, recognising social determinants and intergenerational trauma, and respecting family and community <i>where appropriate</i> &mdash; while avoiding assumptions about any individual (which rules out B).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","You have told a GP you are concerned about a prescription. He dismisses it, the plan carries on, and you still feel it is deviating from safe practice. Which CUSS statement is next?",["&ldquo;I&rsquo;m concerned about this prescription.&rdquo;","&ldquo;We need to stop and reassess before continuing.&rdquo;","&ldquo;I&rsquo;m uncomfortable proceeding with this plan.&rdquo;","&ldquo;Can you tell me more about what&rsquo;s most concerning from your side?&rdquo;"],2,"You have already used <b>Concerned</b>, so the next step is <b>Uncomfortable</b> &mdash; a stronger statement that the situation is worsening or deviating from safe practice. &lsquo;Stop&rsquo; is the highest level, for when urgent action is required to prevent harm; jumping to it skips two steps. The last option is the Explore step of NURSE, not CUSS.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Which sequence is PACE?",["Probe, Alert, Challenge, Emergency","Pause, Ask, Clarify, Escalate","Probe, Ask, Confirm, Escalate","Prompt, Alert, Concern, Emergency"],0,"<b>Probe, Alert, Challenge, Emergency</b> &mdash; a stepwise escalation of concern comparable to CUSS (Concerned, Uncomfortable, Safety issue, Stop). The other options are invented.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","A practice nurse is upset that your new monitoring plan adds to her workload. You say: &ldquo;I can see why this change might be frustrating given your workload.&rdquo; Which NURSE step is this?",["Name","Respect","Support","Understand"],3,"<b>Understand</b> &mdash; showing you see <i>why</i> it feels difficult for them. Name would only acknowledge the emotion (&ldquo;it sounds like this has been challenging&rdquo;); Respect acknowledges their expertise or effort; Support offers to work through it together.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Which is the best I-message to raise a concern with a prescriber?",["&ldquo;You&rsquo;ve missed the patient&rsquo;s renal function in this regimen.&rdquo;","&ldquo;I&rsquo;m concerned about the risk of interactions with this regimen because of the patient&rsquo;s renal function. I&rsquo;d like us to review the options together.&rdquo;","&ldquo;This regimen is not appropriate and needs to change.&rdquo;","&ldquo;We need to stop and reassess before continuing.&rdquo;"],1,"It follows the I-message structure (I feel / when / because / I would like): owns the concern, gives the reason, and asks for joint review. Options 1 and 3 are accusatory &lsquo;you&rsquo;/judgement statements. Option 4 is the top CUSS step &mdash; an escalation, not an opening.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","A GP says: &ldquo;I&rsquo;d prefer to keep the current medication for now.&rdquo; In the Week 7 module&rsquo;s example sequence, what comes <i>after</i> raising your concern?",["Restate your recommendation more firmly","Escalate using CUSS","Explore the rationale behind the GP&rsquo;s decision","Document the disagreement and end the call"],2,"The module&rsquo;s example sequence is raise a concern (feedback) &rarr; <b>explore the rationale (understanding interests)</b> &rarr; suggest an alternative (negotiation) &rarr; shared plan (collaboration). Restating more firmly treats negotiation as a contest between opposing sides; the module favours a partnership approach. CUSS is described for escalating when concerns are not addressed, not as the next step here.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","A GP rings angry: a patient you prescribed for developed symptoms over the weekend and he believes your change caused them. It is not yet clear. What is the best first approach?",["Defend the decision &mdash; it was within guidelines, so it was correct","Apologise for causing harm and reverse the change","Avoid assumptions: gather more information from the patient and the GP, then reassess collaboratively","Tell the GP the symptoms are probably unrelated"],2,"The course: avoid assumptions, gather information, reassess &mdash; with the patient and the GP, not in isolation. Defending on process and conceding fault both assume the answer; outcomes alone do not determine decision quality, and it is not yet known whether the change was responsible.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Which list gives the stages of the Gibbs Reflective Cycle in order?",["Description, feelings, evaluation, analysis, conclusion, action planning","Description, analysis, feelings, evaluation, action planning, conclusion","Feelings, description, analysis, evaluation, conclusion, action planning","Description, evaluation, feelings, analysis, action planning, conclusion"],0,"<b>Description &rarr; feelings &rarr; evaluation &rarr; analysis &rarr; conclusion &rarr; action planning.</b> Feelings come straight after description; evaluation precedes analysis; action planning is last.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","A colleague gives you critical feedback on a prescribing decision in a team meeting. Which response best matches the course guidance?",["Explain your reasoning immediately so the record is corrected","Listen without interrupting, ask clarifying questions, and reflect before responding","Accept the feedback and change your practice straight away","Ask to discuss it privately later and move on"],1,"The course lists three behaviours: listen without interruption, ask clarifying questions, reflect before responding. Explaining immediately is the tempting option but interrupts and skips reflection; accepting without reflection is not the same as using feedback to refine reasoning.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 7 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","A patient dies after a tenfold dosing error on a ward. The health service requires a formal investigation for governance. Which approach fits best?",["Significant Event Analysis at the next team meeting","Sensemaking conversation alone","Root Cause Analysis","Individual retraining of the prescriber"],2,"<b>RCA</b> is the structured, formal method for serious adverse or sentinel events, especially when governance or regulatory requirements apply. SEA is the less formal, reflective tool for day-to-day learning &mdash; tempting, but not the fit for a death needing formal investigation. Sensemaking complements RCA rather than replacing it; retraining is a person-centred response.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","A community clinic team wants to reflect on a near miss where a dispensing pharmacist caught a prescription for a patient with a documented allergy. No harm occurred. Which approach is most suitable?",["Significant Event Analysis","Root Cause Analysis","London Protocol investigation","Disciplinary review"],0,"<b>SEA</b> suits day-to-day learning from near misses, complaints and good practice in primary care and clinical teams &mdash; this is the module&rsquo;s own worked example. RCA is reserved for high-impact events (serious harm, death, major system failure) needing formal investigation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","In a Root Cause Analysis, which step immediately follows &lsquo;Identify contributing factors&rsquo;?",["Gather information","Develop solutions","Identify root causes","Implement the action plan"],2,"Order: define problem &rarr; gather information &rarr; contributing factors &rarr; <b>root causes</b> &rarr; develop solutions &rarr; implement &rarr; monitor and evaluate &rarr; share learning. Jumping to solutions skips analysing which factors, if fixed, would reduce recurrence.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","Which is the correct order of the first four stages of SEA?",["Information gathering; awareness and prioritisation; analysis; facilitated team meeting","Awareness and prioritisation; information gathering; facilitated team meeting; analysis","Awareness and prioritisation; facilitated team meeting; information gathering; write it up","Facilitated team meeting; information gathering; analysis; agree and implement change"],1,"Stages: 1 awareness and prioritisation, 2 information gathering, 3 facilitated team-based meeting, 4 analysis, 5 agree, implement and monitor change, 6 write it up, 7 report, share and review. Facts are gathered and a timeline built <b>before</b> the team meets, and the formal analysis (stage 4) comes <b>after</b> the meeting &mdash; the tempting wrong order puts analysis first.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","After a delayed escalation, a reviewer says &lsquo;It was obvious the patient was deteriorating &mdash; why didn&rsquo;t the pharmacist act?&rsquo; Which approach most directly counters this reasoning?",["The 5 Whys","A fishbone diagram","Sensemaking conversation","Person-centred approach"],2,"The reviewer is showing <b>hindsight bias</b> &mdash; judging the decision by its outcome. Sensemaking explores what information was visible, what assumptions and pressures existed at the time, and is named as particularly useful for delayed escalation and diagnostic uncertainty. The 5 Whys and fishbone find causes but don&rsquo;t specifically address how the situation appeared at the time.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","Which statement about the London Protocol is correct?",["It seeks a single linear root cause","It is a person-centred model focused on individual competence","It is a systems-oriented RCA tailored to healthcare, focused on human factors","It replaces the need for team discussion of incidents"],2,"The London Protocol is a specific systems-oriented RCA for healthcare, examining task design, team communication, work environment, equipment and organisational influences. Seeking a linear root cause is the weakness of <b>traditional</b> RCA it is contrasted with.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","Which of the following would NOT be treated as a significant event suitable for SEA?",["A near miss with no patient harm","An example of excellent teamwork","Delayed follow-up of an abnormal result","None of these &mdash; all can be significant events"],3,"A significant event is anything any team member thinks worth reflecting on, including near misses and <b>positive</b> events. SEA is not only about adverse outcomes &mdash; the tempting wrong answer is the good-teamwork option.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["safe","A pharmacist prescriber involved in a near miss keeps replaying it, sleeps poorly and now double-checks every routine decision. What is the best interpretation and response?",["Evidence of incompetence; refer for retraining","A common second-victim response; seek support such as supervision, debriefing or employee support services","Best managed by not discussing the incident","Only relevant if the patient was harmed"],1,"These are common second-victim responses and the module says they do not mean the clinician is incompetent or uncaring. It recommends discussing the event with trusted colleagues or supervisors, debriefing, mentorship or supervision, and counselling or employee support services. Avoiding discussion appears in the module as one of the distress responses, not a remedy; and the module lists identifying a near miss among events that can affect wellbeing, so harm is not required.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 8 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","A 74-year-old reports getting dizzy and short of breath walking uphill. You hear a loud crescendo&ndash;decrescendo systolic murmur loudest at the 2nd intercostal space, right sternal border. Most appropriate action?",["Document HSDNM and review BP in 3 months","Reassure &mdash; murmurs are common in older adults","Refer for GP/cardiology assessment; an echocardiogram should be considered","Start an antihypertensive and review in 4&ndash;6 weeks"],2,"<b>Suspected aortic stenosis with exertional symptoms needs referral; echocardiography confirms diagnosis and severity.</b> Reassurance is the tempting wrong answer &mdash; exertional dizziness/breathlessness with a systolic murmur may mean significant valve disease (risk of collapse).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","Palpating the apex beat, you find it well lateral to the 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line. What does this most suggest?",["Cardiomegaly from left ventricular enlargement","A normal variant if the patient is lying at 45&deg;","Aortic stenosis","Radio-radial delay"],0,"<b>A laterally displaced apex beat may indicate cardiomegaly due to left ventricular enlargement</b>, commonly associated with heart failure, and should prompt further clinical assessment. Positioning at 45&deg; is the standard technique, not an explanation for displacement; a normal apex sits in the 5th ICS at or close to the midclavicular line.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","Which technique is correct when assessing the radial pulse?",["Use your thumb for better sensitivity","Count for 10 seconds and multiply by 6 if irregular","Palpate one wrist only; the other adds nothing","Use index and middle fingers and count a full 60 seconds, especially if irregular"],3,"<b>Index and middle fingers, full 60 seconds preferred &mdash; particularly if irregular.</b> The thumb has its own pulse. Palpating both radials together is how radio-radial delay is detected.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","During a cardiovascular exam you find a capillary refill time of 5 seconds. What does the course say?",["Normal &mdash; the cut-off is 6 seconds","Abnormal &mdash; an indication to seek urgent medical attention","Recheck at the next routine review","Only relevant if the patient has diabetes"],1,"<b>Normal CRT is less than 3 seconds (intensive handout); the Week 2 Feel page says an abnormal CRT is an indication to seek urgent medical attention.</b> Routine recheck under-responds.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","A 68-year-old has an irregularly irregular radial pulse at 112 beats/min and has never been told about a heart rhythm problem. Best response?",["Recheck in 3 months","Advise reducing caffeine only","Refer for further assessment &mdash; new-onset AF with a rate over 100","Document as sinus arrhythmia"],2,"<b>New-onset AF, symptomatic AF, or a ventricular rate greater than 100 beats/min should prompt assessment and referral</b> &mdash; this patient meets two. Considerations: rate control, rhythm control and stroke risk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["htn","Palpating both radial pulses together, you notice they are out of sync. Which is a recognised cause?",["Aortic stenosis","Coarctation of the aorta","Orthostatic hypotension","Atrial fibrillation"],1,"<b>Radio-radial delay suggests obstruction or narrowing of arterial flow to one upper limb: coarctation of the aorta, subclavian or brachiocephalic artery disease, or external compression of a major vessel.</b> The other options are not causes the course lists; an irregularly irregular pulse is the course&rsquo;s cue for AF.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["lipid","Michael, 35, has untreated LDL-C 6.8 mmol/L, corneal arcus (age 35) and a father who had an MI at 50. No personal CVD; no xanthomata. Dutch Lipid Clinic Network score and category?",["6 &mdash; probable FH","10 &mdash; definite FH","5 &mdash; possible FH","16 &mdash; definite FH"],1,"<b>LDL-C 6.5&ndash;8.4 = 5; arcus cornealis before 45 = 4; first-degree relative with premature coronary disease (man &lt;55) = 1. Total 10 (&gt;8) = definite FH.</b> 16 wrongly adds tendon xanthomata (6), which he does not have; 6 forgets the arcus; 5 counts the LDL-C only.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: Dutch Lipid Clinic Network score (PHAR6302) &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["smoke","A patient stable on clozapine stops smoking cigarettes and switches completely to nicotine vaping. What is correct?",["Manage as for stopping smoking &mdash; loss of CYP1A2 induction may change clozapine metabolism","No change: nicotine itself induces CYP1A2, so vaping maintains induction","Only relevant if the patient also stops caffeine","Relevant only with heat-not-burn products, not vapes"],0,"<b>Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons from burning tobacco cause the CYP1A2 induction; vapes don&rsquo;t burn tobacco.</b> So complete switching = management as if stopping smoking (clozapine, olanzapine, caffeine). The &lsquo;nicotine induces it&rsquo; distractor is the classic error.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["smoke","A patient wants nicotine-free help to quit and had nausea on varenicline. Which statement about cytisine (cytisinicline) is correct according to the course?",["It is a nicotine-containing lozenge","It is a full agonist at &alpha;4&beta;2 receptors","NICE rates it less effective than NRT","It is reportedly better tolerated than varenicline, with less nausea"],3,"<b>Cytisine is a nicotine-free &alpha;4&beta;2 partial agonist; NICE: more effective than NRT, similar to varenicline, better tolerated with less nausea</b> (lower 5-HT<sub>3A</sub> affinity). &lsquo;Full agonist&rsquo; is wrong &mdash; partial, like varenicline.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 5 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["copd","A stable COPD patient (not on oral steroids) has a blood eosinophil count of 80 cells/&micro;L. What does the evidence suggest about adding an ICS?",["Greatest benefit expected","Relatively little benefit and increased pneumonia risk","Benefit depends on exacerbation history","Count is invalid; repeat on prednisolone"],1,"<b>&lt;100 cells/&micro;L: little ICS benefit and higher pneumonia risk.</b> &lsquo;Depends on exacerbation history&rsquo; applies to 100&ndash;300. Systemic steroids suppress eosinophils &mdash; measure off them, not on them.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 6 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["copd","Which best describes a COPD exacerbation?",["Any symptom change lasting over 28 days","A fall in FEV<sub>1</sub> on routine spirometry without symptoms","Acute worsening of breathlessness, cough and/or sputum developing over 14 days or less","Any hospital admission in a patient with COPD"],2,"<b>Acute worsening of respiratory symptoms developing over 14 days or less</b>, possibly with raised RR and HR. It is symptom-defined, not a spirometry finding.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 6 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["wt","A patient on weekly semaglutide has an elective colonoscopy next week. Under the 2025 Australian ADS/ANZCA/GESA/NACOS recommendations, what applies?",["Continue it; 24-hour clear-fluid diet then standard 6-hour fast","Withhold for at least one dosing interval","Stop it permanently","Withhold for one week before the procedure"],0,"<b>In Australia GLP-1 RAs are NOT withheld for at least one dosing interval before elective colonoscopy: the 2025 ADS/ANZCA/GESA/NACOS recommendations advise against elective pre-procedural cessation &mdash; 24-hour clear fluids, then standard 6-hour fasting.</b> Withholding for a dosing interval is the tempting answer; the course notes only that the US position differs.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 4 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["reas","Michael, 49, asks for stronger reflux treatment. Which new detail should most strongly make you slow down and reconsider GORD?",["He has been eating poorly at work","He had reflux previously","An antacid seemed to help","The discomfort comes on walking uphill and settles after a few minutes of rest"],3,"<b>Exertional, pressure-like discomfort relieved by rest</b> doesn&rsquo;t fit GORD and raises a can&rsquo;t-miss cardiac cause &mdash; especially with treated hypertension and ex-smoking. The other options are the cues that built the initial GORD pattern (anchoring).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 tutorial &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","In an SBAR to a GP you write: &lsquo;Uncontrolled hypertension, consistent across home and in-pharmacy readings. Likely contributing factor: missed and delayed doses.&rsquo; Which element is this?",["Situation","Background","Assessment","Recommendation"],2,"<b>Assessment</b> &mdash; it is your clinical judgement (likely cause, interpretation), which is what the course defines A as. The raw readings themselves belong in <b>Situation</b>, and the fact of missed doses in <b>Background</b>; this sentence interprets them. Course example: Mrs Santos SBAR.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Which statement best describes what Assessment 3 Part A rewards?",["Documenting everything known about the patient so the GP has a complete record","Communicating the most clinically relevant information to the GP clearly, concisely and actionably","Reproducing the full consultation in chronological order under ISBAR headings","Summarising the guidelines relevant to asthma or COPD"],1,"The brief says the purpose is <b>not</b> to document everything known, but to communicate what is most clinically relevant in a clear, concise, actionable manner &mdash; showing prioritisation. Option A is the tempting one, and it is exactly what the brief rules out.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Assessment 3 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Which of these would result in a fail grade for Assessment 3 regardless of performance elsewhere?",["Writing 1,350 words in total","Not structuring the reflection around a named framework","A recommendation that could reasonably delay escalation of serious illness","Using GenAI to proofread the reflection, with acknowledgement"],2,"Delayed escalation of serious illness is named in the patient safety requirement as an automatic fail. Exceeding 1,320 words means text beyond the limit is not read or marked &mdash; costly, but not an automatic fail. A named framework is encouraged, not required. GenAI is permitted for editing and proofreading if acknowledged (not for clinical content or the ISBAR itself).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Assessment 3 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"],
["comm","Which of the following is NOT one of the questions the Assessment 3 critical reflection must address?",["How did you determine what was important to include in the handover?","What was challenging about the clinical decision-making process?","What was the patient&rsquo;s presenting history, in order?","What knowledge, skills or areas of practice require further development?"],2,"The brief states the reflection is <b>not a case summary</b>; recounting the presentation is description, not analysis. The four required questions are: what to include, what was challenging, what you learned about interprofessional communication, and what needs further development.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Assessment 3 &middot; Oct 2026 course export</div>"]
);


/* J. PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video, part 1 (diagnosis and assessment, GOLD 2026), Oct 2026. Append-only. */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["copd","How does GOLD define airflow obstruction on spirometry, and how does that differ from the QLD COPD protocol&rsquo;s definition?","<b>GOLD: post-bronchodilator FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC &lt;0.70</b> (a fixed cut-off).<br>QLD COPD protocol (Program eligibility): FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC &le;0.7 <i>and</i> FEV<sub>1</sub> &lt;80% predicted, post-bronchodilator.<br>Know which framework a question is asking about &mdash; for Program decisions, use the protocol.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","Why can the fixed FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC cut-off of 0.70 mislead, and in whom is it most likely to over-diagnose?","<b>It can under- or over-diagnose in younger or older people.</b> Above about 60 years it tends to <b>over-diagnose</b> airflow obstruction, because lung function declines physiologically with age.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","What are the two more physiological alternatives to the fixed 0.70 ratio for defining airflow obstruction?","<b>1. Lower limit of normal (LLN)</b> &mdash; the 5th percentile of a reference population; obstruction if FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC is below the LLN.<br><b>2. GLI z-scores</b> (Global Lung Function Initiative) &mdash; interpretable independent of age, sex, height and ethnicity; obstruction if the FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC z-score is below about &minus;1.6 (as stated in the tutorial).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","Give the GOLD grades of airflow obstruction severity.","Based on <b>post-bronchodilator FEV<sub>1</sub> % predicted</b>:<br><b>GOLD 1 (mild)</b> &ge;80%<br><b>GOLD 2 (moderate)</b> 50&ndash;79%<br><b>GOLD 3 (severe)</b> 30&ndash;49%<br><b>GOLD 4 (very severe)</b> &lt;30%<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","A patient&rsquo;s FEV<sub>1</sub> is 65% predicted. What severity is that under GOLD, and under the QLD COPD protocol?","<b>GOLD 2 (moderate)</b> under GOLD (50&ndash;79%), but <b>mild</b> under the QLD protocol bands (60&ndash;79%) &mdash; so still potentially manageable in the Program.<br>The two systems use different cut-offs and the same words. Never carry a severity label from one framework into the other.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","What is the FEV<sub>1</sub> % predicted (degree of airflow limitation) actually used for now?","<b>Prognosis and some therapeutic decisions</b> &mdash; especially specialised management such as endoscopic lung volume reduction &mdash; plus identifying a <b>rapid decline</b> in lung function, and prompting a search for an <b>alternative diagnosis</b> when symptoms don&rsquo;t match the degree of impairment.<br>Treatment choice itself is driven by symptoms and exacerbation risk (the ABE groups).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","Define the GOLD 2026 ABE groups.","<b>A</b> &mdash; no moderate or severe exacerbation in the past 12 months <b>and</b> low symptoms: mMRC 0&ndash;1 <b>and</b> CAT &lt;10.<br><b>B</b> &mdash; no moderate or severe exacerbation in the past 12 months, but higher symptoms: mMRC &ge;2 <b>or</b> CAT &ge;10.<br><b>E</b> &mdash; <b>one or more</b> moderate or severe exacerbations in the past 12 months, regardless of symptoms.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","What changed in the GOLD 2026 exacerbation-risk criterion, and why does it matter in Australia?","<b>Group E now starts at one moderate or severe exacerbation</b> in the previous year (previously two).<br>The tutorial notes this creates a slight difference from the Australian national guidelines &mdash; so check which guideline a question or protocol is using.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","Which symptom domains does the COPD Assessment Test (CAT) cover?","Cough, sputum, breathlessness, chest tightness, exercise limitation, confidence leaving the house despite COPD, sleep, and energy.<br>Score &lt;10 = low symptom burden; &ge;10 = higher burden.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","When should you suspect COPD, and how is it confirmed?","Suspect it in anyone with <b>breathlessness, chronic cough and/or sputum production</b> plus a <b>history of exposure to risk factors</b>.<br>Confirm with <b>post-bronchodilator spirometry</b> showing an obstructive pattern (reduced FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","Beyond smoking, what risk factors shape the development of COPD?","Genetic susceptibility and lifetime exposures, including <b>early-life factors</b> (prematurity, frequent childhood respiratory infections, asthma), <b>chronic air pollution</b>, <b>occupational hazards</b> and <b>socioeconomic factors</b>.<br>Smoking remains the most common single risk factor.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","What do we know about COPD burden in Australia, including for First Nations people?","BOLD study: moderate-or-worse COPD in about <b>7.5%</b> of people aged 40+, rising to <b>29%</b> at 75+.<br>First Nations people: prevalence about <b>2.3&times;</b> higher and mortality <b>2.7&times;</b> higher. Current management is extrapolated from non-First Nations populations, so applicability can&rsquo;t be assumed.<br>Globally COPD is the third leading cause of death (3.4 million deaths in 2023).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","Where does most of the morbidity and mortality of COPD come from?","<b>Acute exacerbations</b> account for the large bulk of it, and exacerbation history drives risk grouping (GOLD group E).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","Why is COPD described as heterogeneous?","Persistent airflow limitation and chronic inflammation cause <b>large- and small-airway dysfunction and destruction of lung parenchyma and vasculature</b> in highly variable combinations &mdash; so patients differ in clinical picture and in their response to treatment. COPD also commonly coexists with other chronic diseases.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["copd","A 64-year-old has post-bronchodilator FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC 0.62 and FEV<sub>1</sub> 55% predicted. What is the GOLD grade?",["GOLD 1 (mild)","GOLD 2 (moderate)","GOLD 3 (severe)","GOLD 4 (very severe)"],1,"GOLD 2 covers FEV<sub>1</sub> 50&ndash;79% predicted. Note that under the QLD protocol bands (mild 60&ndash;79%, moderate 40&ndash;59%) the same patient is <b>moderate</b> and must be referred &mdash; the two frameworks use different cut-offs.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","Under GOLD 2026, a patient with COPD had one exacerbation treated with oral prednisolone 8 months ago. CAT 7, mMRC 1. Which group?",["Group A","Group B","Group E","Cannot be classified without spirometry grade"],2,"From 2026, <b>one or more</b> moderate or severe exacerbations in the past 12 months places the patient in <b>group E</b>, regardless of a low symptom burden. Previously two were needed. (A moderate exacerbation is one needing oral corticosteroids and/or antibiotics, per GOLD.)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","No exacerbations in the past year, mMRC 2, CAT 8. GOLD 2026 group?",["Group A","Group B","Group E","Group C"],1,"With no moderate or severe exacerbation, the group depends on symptoms. <b>Either</b> mMRC &ge;2 <b>or</b> CAT &ge;10 makes it group B &mdash; here the mMRC of 2 is enough. There is no group C in the current ABE scheme.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","A 78-year-old ex-smoker has FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC 0.68, which is above the lower limit of normal for their age. What is the best interpretation?",["Confirmed COPD, because the ratio is below 0.70","Possible over-diagnosis by the fixed ratio; by LLN there is no airflow obstruction","Restrictive lung disease","Asthma, because the ratio is borderline"],1,"The fixed 0.70 cut-off tends to <b>over-diagnose</b> obstruction above about 60, because lung function declines with age. The LLN (5th percentile of a reference population) is the more physiological test &mdash; above it, obstruction is not present.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"],
["copd","Which of these is NOT a domain of the COPD Assessment Test (CAT)?",["Chest tightness","Confidence leaving the house","Weight loss","Energy"],2,"The CAT covers cough, sputum, breathlessness, chest tightness, exercise limitation, confidence leaving the house, sleep and energy. Weight loss is not a CAT item.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video (respiratory physician) &middot; Oct 2026 &middot; GOLD 2026</div>"]
);


/* K. PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video, part 2 (management, COPD-X 2026), Oct 2026. Append-only. */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["copd","What management applies to <b>every</b> patient with COPD, whatever their severity?","<b>Vaccinations up to date, smoking cessation, physical activity, nutrition advice, a written COPD action plan and self-management plan, inhaler technique, pulmonary rehabilitation referral (if symptomatic), optimising comorbidities, and regular review.</b><br>Free action plan templates are available from Lung Foundation Australia.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Which vaccinations does the tutorial recommend for people with COPD?","<b>Annual influenza</b> and <b>COVID-19 boosters</b> per national guidelines; <b>pneumococcal</b> (a new 21-valent conjugate vaccine is available in Australia); <b>RSV</b> (subsidised from age 75, otherwise self-funded); <b>shingles</b> (funded from 65); <b>diphtheria-tetanus-pertussis</b> (self-funded, but worth keeping up to date).<br><i>Funding as stated in the 2026 tutorial &mdash; check the current NIP.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","What practical smoking cessation steps does the tutorial suggest at a COPD consultation?","Ask and advise every smoker. Suggest <b>setting a quit date</b>, refer to <b>Quitline</b>, consider <b>NRT and/or varenicline or bupropion</b>, and refer to a dedicated smoking cessation clinic where available.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Outline the stepwise inhaler management of stable COPD in the 2026 national (COPD-X) guidelines, and where the QLD Program draws the line.","<b>COPD-X (as taught in the tutorial):</b><br>&bull; Symptomatic, spirometry-confirmed: <b>LAMA or LABA monotherapy</b>.<br>&bull; Suboptimal response (persistent symptoms and/or exacerbations): <b>LAMA/LABA</b>.<br>&bull; &ge;1 severe exacerbation (hospitalisation) <b>or</b> &ge;2 moderate in 12 months: <b>ICS/LABA/LAMA triple</b>.<br>Plus a SABA or SAMA reliever throughout.<br><b>QLD Program:</b> no improvement after one month, or worsening, on LAMA+LABA such that triple therapy would be needed &rarr; ineligible, <b>refer</b>. The exacerbation history that triggers triple therapy (hospitalisation, or &ge;2 exacerbations needing systemic steroids or antibiotics in 12 months) also makes the patient ineligible.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Apart from exacerbation history, when else is an inhaled corticosteroid indicated in COPD?","<b>Coexisting asthma or asthma features</b> &mdash; e.g. a substantial bronchodilator response (FEV<sub>1</sub> up by <b>&ge;15% and &ge;400 mL</b>) &mdash; or a blood eosinophil count <b>&gt;300 cells/&micro;L</b>.<br>If there is any coexisting or overlapping asthma, an ICS is an essential part of treatment.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Which inhaled treatment has been shown to reduce mortality in COPD?","<b>Single-inhaler triple therapy (ICS/LABA/LAMA)</b> &mdash; the IMPACT and ETHOS trials showed lower mortality than dual LABA/LAMA in the exacerbation-prone patients those trials enrolled. GOLD 2023 first recognised triple therapy as the only pharmacotherapy proven to reduce mortality.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","How should you choose an inhaler device for a COPD patient (assess, choose, train)?","Assess the inhalation:<br><b>Slow, steady breath over 3&ndash;5 s</b> &rarr; pressurised MDI (ideally with a spacer) or a soft mist inhaler.<br><b>Quick, deep breath over 2&ndash;3 s</b> &rarr; dry powder inhaler.<br>Then teach technique, and favour the same device type (or a single inhaler) across medicines.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","What are the advantages of using a spacer with a pMDI?","<b>Helps coordination, improves deposition deeper into the small airways, and reduces the risk of oral thrush.</b> Actuation aids can also help patients with poor hand strength or coordination.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Who should be referred to pulmonary rehabilitation, and what does it achieve?","<b>Every symptomatic COPD patient.</b> It is the most effective non-pharmacological management: improves quality of life and exercise capacity, reduces breathlessness and fatigue, reduces admissions, length of stay and readmission after an exacerbation, and <b>reduces mortality</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Name the specialised COPD treatments that usually need specialist assessment.","<b>Long-term oxygen</b> (improves survival in severe chronic resting hypoxaemia); <b>non-invasive ventilation</b> (severe chronic hypercapnia with a past hospitalisation for acute respiratory failure); <b>lung volume reduction</b> for severe emphysema (endobronchial valves or coils, bullectomy, surgery); <b>lung transplantation</b> in very select patients; and <b>supportive and palliative care</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","How is an acute exacerbation of COPD managed, according to the tutorial?","<b>Bronchodilator:</b> SABA and/or SAMA.<br><b>Systemic corticosteroid</b> for up to <b>5 days</b> &mdash; improves lung function and oxygenation and shortens recovery and length of stay.<br><b>Antibiotics</b> (5 days) if <b>purulent sputum</b>, positive sputum culture, or the patient needs non-invasive or mechanical ventilation.<br><i>QLD Program: the patient self-initiates the pharmacist-supplied prednisolone (up to 5 days, no repeats) per the action plan and arranges medical review; anyone needing antibiotics is referred and can stay in the Program.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Why do COPD exacerbations matter so much?","They may cause <b>permanent loss of lung function</b>; frequent severe exacerbations <b>increase mortality</b>; more exacerbations worsen health status; and patients who under-report them have poorer quality of life.<br>The strongest risk factor is a <b>previous exacerbation</b>. <b>Early treatment</b> reduces duration, severity, hospitalisation and lung function loss.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","What features suggest asthma&ndash;COPD overlap, and what does it mean for treatment?","Relatively <b>preserved DLCO</b> and raised <b>IgE or eosinophils</b>; these patients are exacerbation-prone and respond less well to standard treatment for either condition alone.<br>If there is any indication of coexisting asthma, an <b>ICS must be part of treatment</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Tutorial case: 61-year-old smoker on a LAMA plus PRN SABA still gets breathless walking up inclines. What was the next step, and what else belongs in his plan?","<b>Step up to dual LAMA/LABA bronchodilator therapy</b>, with ICS considered later depending on progress &mdash; though his <b>blood eosinophils were 0</b>, so little benefit from ICS would be expected (ICS benefit is minimal around 100 cells/&micro;L and greatest above 300).<br>Also: vaccinations, ongoing smoking cessation support, physical activity and pulmonary rehabilitation, mental health and comorbidity optimisation, a <b>bone mineral density scan</b> for osteoporosis, and <b>checking inhaler technique and adherence at every visit</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["copd","A patient with spirometry-confirmed COPD on a LAMA is still breathless on exertion but has had no exacerbations. Per the 2026 COPD-X stepwise approach, what is the next step?",["Add an ICS to the LAMA","Step up to LAMA/LABA dual bronchodilator","Start ICS/LABA/LAMA triple therapy","Start long-term oxygen"],1,"Persistent symptoms on monotherapy warrant <b>dual bronchodilation (LAMA/LABA)</b>. Triple therapy is for &ge;1 severe or &ge;2 moderate exacerbations in 12 months. ICS is not added for breathlessness alone.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","On LAMA/LABA, a patient has had two moderate exacerbations in the past 12 months. What does the stepwise approach recommend?",["Continue and review in 6 months","Switch to LABA monotherapy","Step up to ICS/LABA/LAMA triple therapy","Add a short-acting antimuscarinic regularly"],2,"<b>&ge;2 moderate</b> (or &ge;1 severe, hospitalised) exacerbations in 12 months despite LAMA/LABA is the trigger for <b>triple therapy</b>, which has also been shown to reduce mortality (IMPACT, ETHOS). <b>Exam trap:</b> under the QLD protocol this patient must be <b>referred</b> &mdash; triple therapy is outside the Program, and &ge;2 exacerbations in 12 months makes them ineligible.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","An older patient can only manage a slow, steady inhalation over about 4 seconds. Which device type is most appropriate?",["Dry powder inhaler","Pressurised MDI with a spacer, or a soft mist inhaler","Breath-actuated dry powder inhaler with a fast inhalation","Pressurised MDI without a spacer, fast inhalation"],1,"A <b>slow, steady 3&ndash;5 s inhalation</b> suits a <b>pMDI (with a spacer) or soft mist inhaler</b>. Dry powder inhalers need a quick, deep inhalation over 2&ndash;3 s.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Which finding is a stated indication for antibiotics in an acute COPD exacerbation?",["Increased breathlessness alone","Purulent sputum","Any exacerbation lasting more than 2 days","Blood eosinophils above 300"],1,"Antibiotics are indicated for <b>purulent sputum</b>, a positive sputum culture, or a need for non-invasive or mechanical ventilation, usually for 5 days. Raised eosinophils are not an antibiotic indication. Under the QLD Program, a patient needing antibiotics is referred.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Which bronchodilator response suggests asthma features and supports adding an ICS in COPD?",["FEV<sub>1</sub> rise of 5% and 100 mL","FEV<sub>1</sub> rise of &ge;12% only","FEV<sub>1</sub> rise of &ge;15% and &ge;400 mL","Any rise in FVC"],2,"The tutorial gives a <b>substantial</b> bronchodilator response &mdash; FEV<sub>1</sub> up by <b>&ge;15% and &ge;400 mL</b> &mdash; as an asthma feature supporting ICS. Eosinophils &gt;300 cells/&micro;L are another indicator.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["copd","Which COPD patients should be referred to pulmonary rehabilitation?",["Only those with severe (GOLD 3&ndash;4) disease","Only after a hospital admission","Every symptomatic patient","Only those who have stopped smoking"],2,"Pulmonary rehabilitation should be considered for <b>every symptomatic COPD patient</b>. It is the most effective non-pharmacological treatment and reduces admissions, readmissions and mortality.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 COPD pre-tutorial video 2 (respiratory physician) &middot; COPD-X 2026 &middot; Oct 2026</div>"]
);


/* L. PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work: PDL Error, uncertainty and patient safety. Oct 2026. Append-only. */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["safe","PDL&rsquo;s scope of practice equation for prescribing pharmacists?","<b>Education + Competence + Authorisation + Accountability = Scope of practice.</b><br>All four must be present before you take on a prescribing activity.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","Name the four stages of PDL&rsquo;s risk management cycle.","<b>Identify &rarr; Assess &rarr; Manage &rarr; Review</b> &mdash; a continuous cycle, not a linear checklist.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","What are PDL&rsquo;s five categories of risk in pharmacy practice?","<b>1. Regulatory and compliance</b> &middot; <b>2. Clinical</b> &middot; <b>3. Professional</b> &middot; <b>4. Ethical</b> &middot; <b>5. Operational</b>.<br>Risk is multi-dimensional &mdash; one situation often sits in several categories.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","Describe PDL&rsquo;s four risk ratings and what each requires.","<b>Critical (red)</b> &mdash; immediate action before proceeding further.<br><b>High (orange)</b> &mdash; could escalate to critical without timely intervention; take proactive steps to mitigate.<br><b>Moderate (yellow)</b> &mdash; generally acceptable; reflect on how to maintain it and upskill.<br><b>Low (green)</b> &mdash; a positive outcome; use it for continuous quality improvement.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","What two questions does PDL use to frame whether a risk needs managing?","<b>Is the risk foreseeable?</b> and <b>Is the risk preventable?</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","Which three areas of risk overlap in a prescribing pharmacist&rsquo;s work, according to PDL?","<b>The patient</b>, <b>your professional role</b> and <b>your practice setting</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","Name the four domains of the Quality Standards for Pharmacy Practice (QSPP).","<b>1. Clinical Governance and Leadership</b><br><b>2. Safely Delivering Medications and Pharmacy Services</b> (includes prescribing by pharmacists)<br><b>3. Partnering with Consumers and the Community</b><br><b>4. Business Governance and Operations</b><br>PDL describes QSPP as a guardrail: risk mitigation and a safety net for when practice falls below the expected standard.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","What rights does the Australian Charter of Healthcare Rights give patients?","<b>Access, Safety, Respect, Partnership, Information, Privacy, and Give feedback.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","Which frameworks did the PDL tutorial cite as supporting safe and effective prescribing practice?","Pharmacy Board of Australia (Ahpra) requirements, the PSA <b>Code of Ethics for pharmacists</b>, the <b>Quality Standards for Pharmacy Practice (QSPP)</b>, the <b>Australian Charter of Healthcare Rights</b>, and PDL&rsquo;s own Risk Assessment Tool and Scope of Practice Checklist.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","What practical benefits does PDL list for a proactive risk mindset?","Workflow efficiencies; fewer operational losses; better patient safety and outcomes; more informed decisions; consistent service across a team; protection from reputational and professional consequences; continuous quality improvement.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","Case (PDL tutorial): a pharmacist plans to launch a new prescribing service. Which governance questions should be settled first?","How enquiries are handled <b>when they&rsquo;re off duty</b>; <b>who controls the consultation records</b> (pharmacist or pharmacy); whether the premises allow a <b>safe, confidential</b> service; what to consider before adopting an <b>AI documentation tool</b>; and how to keep prescribing <b>within scope</b> on an ongoing basis.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["eth","Case (PDL tutorial): after you prescribe an antiviral for shingles, the patient says &ldquo;I guess I have to get it here.&rdquo; Months later a complaint alleges they felt pressured. What issues does this raise?","<b>Patient autonomy and informed choice</b> &mdash; what the patient was told about where they could have it dispensed; which professional standards guide prescribing-and-dispensing by the same pharmacist; and how you <b>demonstrate the decision served the patient&rsquo;s best interests</b>, not a real or perceived financial benefit.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","Case (PDL tutorial): an AI scribe records a previous drug reaction as a side effect rather than an allergy, and you prescribe from the same class. What questions does this raise?","<b>Who is ultimately responsible</b> for the record and the prescribing decision (the prescriber); what <b>privacy, consent and data security</b> steps were needed before using the tool; how patients should be <b>told AI is being used</b> to keep their trust; and what <b>governance processes</b> should be in place before implementation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","Case (PDL tutorial): you refer a patient with recurrent headaches to their GP; three months later they say they never went. What does this raise?","Your <b>duty of care after making a referral</b>; what <b>documentation</b> should be recorded; who could <b>scrutinise the decision</b> if the patient deteriorates; and what <b>level of follow-up</b> is appropriate when a patient doesn&rsquo;t act on a referral.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["safe","PDL&rsquo;s scope of practice equation is Education + Competence + Authorisation + ___ = Scope of practice. What completes it?",["Experience","Accountability","Insurance","Remuneration"],1,"<b>Accountability</b> completes the equation. Experience builds competence but is not a separate term.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","A risk is rated HIGH on the PDL Risk Assessment Tool. What does that mean?",["Stop and act immediately before proceeding","It could escalate to critical without timely intervention, so take proactive steps","It is generally acceptable; focus on upskilling","It is a positive outcome to share with the team"],1,"<b>High (orange)</b> may escalate to critical without timely intervention. <b>Critical (red)</b> requires immediate action before proceeding; moderate is generally acceptable; low is positive.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","Which is NOT one of PDL&rsquo;s five risk categories?",["Ethical","Operational","Financial","Clinical"],2,"PDL&rsquo;s five categories are <b>regulatory and compliance, clinical, professional, ethical and operational</b>. Financial is not listed as its own category.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","In which QSPP domain does prescribing by pharmacists sit?",["Clinical Governance and Leadership","Safely Delivering Medications and Pharmacy Services","Partnering with Consumers and the Community","Business Governance and Operations"],1,"Prescribing by pharmacists is item 2.2 in <b>Domain 2, Safely Delivering Medications and Pharmacy Services</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"],
["safe","An AI scribe mis-records an allergy as a side effect and you prescribe from the same class. Who is responsible for the record and the prescribing decision?",["The AI software vendor","The pharmacy owner","The prescribing pharmacist","Shared equally with the patient"],2,"The <b>prescriber</b> remains responsible for the record they sign and the decision they make. The PDL case then asks what consent, privacy and governance steps should come before adopting an AI tool.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Tutorial 4 pre-work, PDL (Pharmaceutical Defence Limited) &middot; Oct 2026</div>"]
);


/* M. NPCF 2025, PSA Code of Ethics 2026, PDL scope checklist + risk guide. Oct 2026. Append-only. */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["eth","Name the seven competency areas of the National Prescribing Competencies Framework (3rd ed., 2025).","<b>1.</b> Understand the person and their needs<br><b>2.</b> Understand the management options<br><b>3.</b> Explore, discuss and decide on a plan for medicines<br><b>4.</b> Prescribe medicines and communicate the agreed treatment decision<br><b>5.</b> Monitor and review the outcomes of treatment<br><b>6.</b> Prescribe safely and effectively<br><b>7.</b> Prescribe professionally<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: National Prescribing Competencies Framework, 3rd ed. (Sept 2025)</div>"],
["eth","Which NPCF competency says recognise when it is clinically appropriate <i>not</i> to prescribe?","<b>2.1</b> (area 2, understand the management options): recognise and communicate when it is clinically appropriate not to prescribe medicines. Area 2 also covers reviewing current medicines as a possible cause (2.2), non-drug options (2.3), cost and affordability (2.8), community impact (2.9) and referral when options are outside scope (2.10).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: National Prescribing Competencies Framework, 3rd ed. (Sept 2025)</div>"],
["eth","What does NPCF competency area 7 (prescribe professionally) require?","Comply with professional standards, codes and guidelines (7.1); use professional judgement when applying guidelines (7.2); keep <b>accurate, contemporaneous and complete records</b> (7.3); and <b>accept responsibility and accountability</b> for prescribing decisions (7.4).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: National Prescribing Competencies Framework, 3rd ed. (Sept 2025)</div>"],
["eth","Which NPCF competencies cover sharing the plan with the patient and other health professionals?","<b>4.5</b>: give accurate, complete information to other health professionals in a timely manner when prescribing or changing medicines. <b>4.6</b>: discuss and document the treatment plan with the person and make sure they understand it and how to use the medicine safely.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: National Prescribing Competencies Framework, 3rd ed. (Sept 2025)</div>"],
["eth","What does NPCF competency area 3 ask of the prescriber?","Shared decision-making: explore the person&rsquo;s preferences (3.1), set goals in partnership (3.2), discuss options and <b>allow time for an informed decision</b> (3.3), respond to concerns and expectations (3.4), develop the plan together (3.5), and agree a review plan (3.6).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: National Prescribing Competencies Framework, 3rd ed. (Sept 2025)</div>"],
["eth","Name the four ethical values of the PSA Code of Ethics for Pharmacists.","<b>Care, Integrity, Competency, Collaboration.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PSA Code of Ethics for Pharmacists (endorsed May 2026)</div>"],
["eth","State the four Care principles of the PSA Code of Ethics.","<b>1.</b> Make the health and wellbeing of the patient your first priority.<br><b>2.</b> Practise person-centred care.<br><b>3.</b> Support public health and environmental sustainability in partnership with patients and the community.<br><b>4.</b> Foster wellbeing in self and colleagues.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PSA Code of Ethics for Pharmacists (endorsed May 2026)</div>"],
["eth","State the three Integrity principles of the PSA Code of Ethics.","<b>1.</b> Act with honesty and integrity to maintain public trust and confidence in the profession.<br><b>2.</b> Only practise under conditions that uphold your professional independence, judgement and integrity.<br><b>3.</b> Respect and maintain professional boundaries to preserve trust and objectivity.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PSA Code of Ethics for Pharmacists (endorsed May 2026)</div>"],
["eth","State the Competency and Collaboration principles of the PSA Code of Ethics.","<b>Competency 1:</b> commit to continual development of self and the profession. <b>Competency 2:</b> practise within defined systems and frameworks.<br><b>Collaboration 1:</b> work respectfully with others to deliver person-centred care. <b>Collaboration 2:</b> collaborate to facilitate continuity of care.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PSA Code of Ethics for Pharmacists (endorsed May 2026)</div>"],
["safe","Under the PDL Scope of Practice Checklist, what counts as <b>authorisation</b> &mdash; and must all parts be met?","<b>Yes, ALL must be met:</b> national legislation authorising the service; state or territory legislation; and pharmacy premises meeting facility and resource requirements, including appropriately trained and qualified staff.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PDL Scope of Practice Checklist (2024) / PDL Risk Management Guide v1.0 (July 2025)</div>"],
["safe","What does <b>accountability</b> mean in the PDL scope of practice equation?","Strong clinical governance: leadership and healthy culture, continuous quality improvement (regular review of the service and SOPs), incident and near-miss reporting, monitoring clinical performance, a safe environment, working with stakeholders and the patient on shared decisions, team training, and good documentation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PDL Scope of Practice Checklist (2024) / PDL Risk Management Guide v1.0 (July 2025)</div>"],
["safe","Does completing the PDL Scope of Practice Checklist guarantee your indemnity cover?","<b>No.</b> It indicates whether you should start a new service; it does not guarantee professional indemnity cover or that the activity will always stay in scope. Changes in legislation or training requirements, or a break from practice, can change your scope. Keep a <b>written record</b> and set a review date.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PDL Scope of Practice Checklist (2024) / PDL Risk Management Guide v1.0 (July 2025)</div>"],
["safe","Why does documentation protect both patient and prescriber, according to PDL?","Good records support safer decisions, communication across transitions of care, continuity of care and partnership with patients &mdash; and act as <b>evidence of care</b> if there is an incident, regulatory notification or liability claim.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PDL Scope of Practice Checklist (2024) / PDL Risk Management Guide v1.0 (July 2025)</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["eth","A patient asks for an antibiotic for a viral sore throat. Which NPCF competency most directly applies?",["4.2 Prescribe in line with legislation","2.1 Recognise and communicate when it is clinically appropriate not to prescribe","5.4 Stop or modify existing medicines","6.4 Detect and report errors"],1,"<b>2.1</b> covers recognising and communicating when <b>not</b> prescribing is the right decision.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: National Prescribing Competencies Framework, 3rd ed. (Sept 2025)</div>"],
["eth","Which is NOT one of the four ethical values in the PSA Code of Ethics?",["Care","Integrity","Autonomy","Collaboration"],2,"The four values are <b>Care, Integrity, Competency and Collaboration</b>. Patient autonomy sits within person-centred care rather than as a separate value.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PSA Code of Ethics for Pharmacists (endorsed May 2026)</div>"],
["safe","Under the PDL checklist, a pharmacist has the training and confidence but the premises lack a private consultation room required for the service. Which element of scope is missing?",["Education","Competence","Authorisation","Accountability"],2,"Authorisation includes the premises meeting facility and resource requirements. All authorisation elements must be met.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PDL Scope of Practice Checklist (2024) / PDL Risk Management Guide v1.0 (July 2025)</div>"],
["eth","Which NPCF competency requires accurate, contemporaneous and complete records?",["1.5","4.6","6.3","7.3"],3,"<b>7.3</b>, in area 7 (prescribe professionally), alongside 7.4: accept responsibility and accountability for prescribing decisions.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: National Prescribing Competencies Framework, 3rd ed. (Sept 2025)</div>"]
);


/* ---------- N. University weekly check-ups as papers (14 Oct 2026) ----------
   Verbatim questions pasted by Uzair after completing them. Held out of the
   general pool via PAPERS / isUni. Appended at COURSE_MCQ[NFROM..+20]. */
var _N_FROM = COURSE_MCQ.length;
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["htn", "When should antihypertensive therapy be initiated in low CVD-risk patients?<br>According to the 2016 guidelines, treatment should be started in low absolute cardiovascular disease risk patients when it is persistently:", ["BP ≥ 180/100 mmHg", "BP ≥ 140/90 mmHg", "BP ≥ 160/100 mmHg", "BP ≥ 120/80 mmHg"], 2, "Low absolute risk: treat when persistently <b>≥160/100</b>. ≥140/90 is the <b>moderate</b>-risk threshold.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) / Heart Foundation Guideline for the diagnosis and management of hypertension in adults (2016)</div>"],
["htn", "When should therapy begin in patients at moderate absolute cardiovascular risk?<br>For people with moderate absolute CVD risk, the guideline advises initiating treatment when:", ["Systolic BP ≥ 140 mmHg and/or diastolic BP ≥ 90 mmHg", "Systolic BP ≥ 160 mmHg only", "Systolic BP ≥ 130 mmHg", "Systolic BP ≥ 120 mmHg"], 0, "Moderate absolute risk: treat when persistently <b>≥140 systolic and/or ≥90 diastolic</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) / Heart Foundation Guideline for the diagnosis and management of hypertension in adults (2016)</div>"],
["htn", "What is the general target BP for patients with uncomplicated hypertension?<br>The 2016 guidelines recommend treating uncomplicated hypertension to:", ["&lt; 150/95 mmHg", "Any BP tolerated by the patient", "&lt; 120/70 mmHg for all patients", "&lt; 140/90 mmHg (or lower if tolerated)"], 3, "Uncomplicated hypertension: target <b>&lt;140/90</b>, or lower if tolerated.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) / Heart Foundation Guideline for the diagnosis and management of hypertension in adults (2016)</div>"],
["htn", "What does the guideline recommend when clinic BP is ≥ 140/90 mmHg?<br>Which additional diagnostic measure should be offered?", ["Echocardiography", "Carotid Doppler ultrasound", "Ambulatory and/or home BP monitoring", "Routine 24-hour ECG"], 2, "Clinic BP ≥140/90 → offer <b>ambulatory and/or home BP monitoring</b> to confirm.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) / Heart Foundation Guideline for the diagnosis and management of hypertension in adults (2016)</div>"],
["htn", "Amelia, 52, non-smoker, no diabetes, no CKD, and no family history of premature CVD. Her absolute CVD risk is assessed as low. Multiple properly taken clinic BPs over the past two months have consistently been around 165/102 mmHg. She is otherwise well.<br>According to the 2016 guideline, what is the most appropriate next step?", ["No treatment unless end-organ damage is identified", "Begin antihypertensive medication because her BP level warrants therapy in low-risk patients", "Order routine ECG and defer BP decisions until results return", "Continue lifestyle management only and review in 6 months"], 1, "Low risk + persistent ≥160/100 → start treatment. No end-organ damage needs to be shown first.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) / Heart Foundation Guideline for the diagnosis and management of hypertension in adults (2016)</div>"],
["htn", "Michael, 68, has diabetes and stage 3 CKD. After initial treatment, his clinic BP averages 128/74 mmHg. His clinician is considering whether to intensify therapy to target &lt;120 mmHg systolic.<br>What does the 2016 guideline say about targeting &lt;120 mmHg systolic?", ["It should be applied universally to all hypertensive patients", "Only patients under 50 derive benefit", "It is unsafe in all patients and should not be attempted", "It may improve cardiovascular outcomes in selected high-risk populations but requires close monitoring for adverse effects"], 3, "&lt;120 systolic: selected high-risk patients only, with close monitoring for adverse effects.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) / Heart Foundation Guideline for the diagnosis and management of hypertension in adults (2016)</div>"],
["htn", "Linda, 49, presents with clinic BP readings between 142–146/92 mmHg on two separate visits. She reports she feels anxious during medical appointments. She has no major comorbidities.<br>What should be done next according to the 2016 guideline?", ["Offer ambulatory and/or home BP monitoring to confirm diagnosis", "Diagnose hypertension immediately based on clinic readings", "Repeat BP in one year", "Begin treatment without further testing"], 0, "Clinic ≥140/90 (and possible white-coat effect) → confirm with ambulatory and/or home monitoring.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) / Heart Foundation Guideline for the diagnosis and management of hypertension in adults (2016)</div>"],
["htn", "Carlos, 57, has newly diagnosed uncomplicated hypertension. After starting therapy, his BP has stabilised at 138/86 mmHg. He asks whether his BP is now at the guideline-recommended target.<br>Which target applies?", ["&lt; 160/100 mmHg", "&lt; 120/80 mmHg for all patients", "&lt; 140/90 mmHg (or lower if tolerated)", "&lt; 150/95 mmHg"], 2, "Uncomplicated: &lt;140/90 or lower if tolerated &mdash; 138/86 is at target.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 2 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) / Heart Foundation Guideline for the diagnosis and management of hypertension in adults (2016)</div>"],
["htn", "When blood pressure targets are not reached after initial monotherapy, what is the preferred next step according to Figure 6.2?", ["Stop drug therapy and intensify lifestyle measures only", "Maximise the dose of the first drug immediately", "Switch to a different drug class at maximum dose", "Add a second antihypertensive from a different pharmacological class"], 3, "Combining low&ndash;moderate doses from different classes improves efficacy and reduces adverse effects compared with high-dose monotherapy.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: University questions on HF 2016 Figure 6.2, sent 9 Sep 2026 &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["htn", "Why does Figure 6.2 recommend reviewing patients every 4–6 weeks during drug titration?", ["To ensure immediate blood pressure normalisation", "To align with dispensing intervals only", "Because maximum drug effect is often seen within 4–6 weeks", "Because adverse effects always appear within 7 days"], 2, "Understanding time-to-effect is essential for pharmacists to prevent inappropriate early escalation or discontinuation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: University questions on HF 2016 Figure 6.2, sent 9 Sep 2026 &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["htn", "True or false: lifestyle advice is only required if pharmacological therapy fails to control blood pressure.", ["True", "False"], 1, "False. Lifestyle advice is recommended at all stages of antihypertensive therapy, including when drug treatment is initiated and escalated.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: University questions on HF 2016 Figure 6.2, sent 9 Sep 2026 &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["lipid", "Which of the following findings would most strongly suggest familial hypercholesterolaemia?", ["LDL-C 6.8 mmol/L with a family history of premature cardiovascular disease", "HDL-C 1.5 mmol/L", "LDL-C 3.0 mmol/L", "Triglycerides 2.0 mmol/L"], 0, "Markedly raised LDL-C plus a family history of premature CVD.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["lipid", "A 58-year-old man has the following lipid panel:<br>Total cholesterol: 6.8 mmol/L<br>LDL-C: 4.9 mmol/L<br>HDL-C: 1.0 mmol/L<br>Triglycerides: 1.4 mmol/L<br>He has hypertension but no history of cardiovascular disease. His calculated 10-year cardiovascular risk is high.<br>Which is the most appropriate prescribing decision?", ["Initiate a high-intensity statin and encourage lifestyle modification", "Prescribe fenofibrate because HDL-C is low", "Reassure the patient and repeat lipids in 12 months", "Prescribe ezetimibe monotherapy as first-line treatment"], 0, "The patient has markedly elevated LDL-C and high cardiovascular risk. Guidelines recommend initiating a high-intensity statin as first-line therapy to reduce LDL-C and cardiovascular events. Lifestyle modifications should also be implemented. Ezetimibe is generally added if LDL targets are not achieved with statin therapy, and fibrates are not first-line for isolated LDL elevation.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["lipid", "A 67-year-old woman commenced atorvastatin 40 mg daily 3 months ago. Her lipid results (baseline → 3 months):<br>LDL-C 4.8 → 2.4 mmol/L<br>HDL-C 1.2 → 1.1 mmol/L<br>Triglycerides 1.8 → 1.6 mmol/L<br>What is the best interpretation and action?", ["Continue current therapy because LDL-C has decreased by approximately 50%", "Add a fibrate immediately to improve overall lipid control", "Discontinue atorvastatin because triglycerides remain elevated", "Treatment has failed because HDL-C decreased slightly"], 0, "The LDL-C has fallen by approximately 50%, indicating an excellent response to high-intensity statin therapy. Small changes in HDL-C are less clinically important than achieving LDL-C reduction. No additional therapy is indicated based on these results alone.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["bgl", "After initiating or changing diabetes pharmacotherapy, HbA1c should generally be reassessed after:", ["1 month", "12 months", "2 weeks", "3 months"], 3, "3 months.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["bgl", "A patient taking an SGLT2 inhibitor develops acute gastroenteritis with poor oral intake. What advice is most appropriate?", ["Continue as usual", "Stop all diabetes monitoring", "Double the dose", "Temporarily withhold the medicine and follow sick-day guidance"], 3, "Temporarily withhold and follow sick-day guidance.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["bgl", "A 60-year-old woman presents with:<br>Newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes<br>HbA1c 84 mmol/mol (9.8%)<br>Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction<br>eGFR 70 mL/min<br>Which strategy is most appropriate?", ["Metformin plus consideration of an SGLT2 inhibitor", "Delay treatment until repeat HbA1c is available", "Sulfonylurea monotherapy", "Lifestyle modification alone"], 0, "Significant hyperglycaemia and heart failure support early initiation of guideline-directed therapy, including an SGLT2 inhibitor because of cardiovascular benefits.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["bgl", "A patient has been taking metformin and a second antihyperglycaemic medicine for 3 months.<br>HbA1c changes from:<br>Baseline: 8.8%<br>Current: 8.5%<br>What is the most appropriate action?", ["Review adherence and consider changing the second agent because HbA1c reduction is less than 0.5%", "Continue unchanged indefinitely", "Immediately commence insulin", "Stop metformin"], 0, "A second agent producing less than a 0.5% HbA1c reduction after 3 months should prompt reassessment and consideration of alternative therapy.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["bgl", "Which patient requires the most urgent escalation of care?", ["HbA1c 7.2%, asymptomatic", "HbA1c 8.0%, hypertension", "HbA1c 12%, blood glucose 24 mmol/L with weight loss", "HbA1c 7.8%, BMI 32 kg/m²"], 2, "Severe hyperglycaemia with symptoms and weight loss may indicate metabolic decompensation and requires urgent management.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["bgl", "Which patient would benefit most from self-monitoring of blood glucose?", ["Patient with isolated dyslipidaemia", "Patient commencing treatment changes and intensive lifestyle modification", "Stable patient meeting all targets for several years", "Patient with normal glucose metabolism"], 1, "Self-monitoring is particularly useful during medication changes and significant lifestyle interventions.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"],
["bgl", "A patient says:<br>&rsquo;I know I should exercise more, but I don&rsquo;t think I&rsquo;ll stick with it.&rsquo;<br>Which response best reflects motivational interviewing?", ["&rsquo;Exercise is mandatory if you want better control.&rsquo;", "&rsquo;I&rsquo;ll refer you to an exercise physiologist.&rsquo;", "&rsquo;You need to exercise or your diabetes will worsen.&rsquo;", "&rsquo;Let&rsquo;s discuss what has worked for you previously and what might be achievable now.&rsquo;"], 3, "Motivational interviewing explores readiness, barriers, and opportunities for behaviour change.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6302 Week 3 Weekly Check Up (university, ungraded) &mdash; feedback text is the university&rsquo;s</div>"]
);
