S2_OSCE.push(
{"id": "vc1", "title": "CVD risk assessment: gathering the calculator inputs", "cond": "htn", "time": "8 min", "type": "consult", "brief": "Ms Helen Price, 52, asks for a heart health check. She has no known heart disease and takes no regular medicines. Take a focused history to assess her cardiovascular risk, measure her blood pressure (the examiner will give the readings), state her risk category once the examiner gives the calculator result, and explain it to her in plain language. Outline your next steps.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>Age/sex:</b> 52, female; periods irregular for a year (perimenopausal).</li><li><b>Identity (if asked):</b> not Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander; Australian-born, Anglo-Celtic background.</li><li><b>Smoking:</b> 8 cigarettes a day for 30 years; would like to cut down.</li><li><b>History:</b> pre-eclampsia in her first pregnancy. No diabetes, kidney disease, heart attack, stroke, angina, AF or heart failure. Not pregnant or planning pregnancy. No specialist care.</li><li><b>Medicines:</b> nil regular; no BP, cholesterol or blood-thinning medicines in the last 6 months. Occasional paracetamol. No allergies.</li><li><b>Family:</b> mother had a stroke at 72; father has high BP. No known familial high cholesterol.</li><li><b>Alarm symptoms:</b> none &mdash; no unexplained weight change, frequent headache or dizziness, chest pain or breathlessness.</li><li><b>Lifestyle:</b> 2 glasses of wine most nights; walks the dog twice a week; frequent takeaway.</li><li><b>Postcode:</b> gives it if asked.</li><li><b>BP (examiner):</b> 156/96, 150/94, 146/90 mmHg. BMI 29 kg/m&sup2;.</li><li><b>GP pathology 4 weeks ago (fasting):</b> TC 6.0, HDL-C 1.3, LDL-C 3.9, TG 1.7 mmol/L; HbA1c 5.6%; eGFR 88; uACR normal.</li><li><b>Calculator result (on request):</b> 6% over 5 years.</li><li><b>Her concern:</b> &ldquo;My dad has blood pressure tablets &mdash; does this mean I need them too?&rdquo;</li></ul>", "marks": ["Introduces self, confirms identity, explains the consultation and gains consent", "Confirms Program eligibility: aged 18–79, not pregnant or planning pregnancy, no ineligibility criteria", "SAFETY: Excludes established CVD and the conditions that make a patient high risk regardless of the calculator (moderate–severe CKD, familial hypercholesterolaemia)", "States she is in the calculator age band: 45–79 years (35–79 with diabetes, 30–79 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander)", "Asks Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander status and ethnic background rather than assuming", "Gathers every calculator input: age, sex, smoking, systolic BP, total cholesterol and HDL, diabetes status, CVD medicines in the last 6 months, AF, postcode", "Measures BP three times and averages the last two (148/92 mmHg)", "Judges mother stroke at 72 as NOT premature (female first-degree relative must be under 65)", "Elicits pre-eclampsia history: raises CVD risk but is not a calculator input; considered holistically", "SAFETY: Screens alarm symptoms (unexplained weight change, frequent headache and dizziness, chronic nausea, lethargy) and Grade 3 BP (≥180 systolic and/or ≥110 diastolic)", "States the category correctly: 6% = intermediate (5% to under 10% over 5 years)", "Explains risk as a frequency (about 6 in 100 people like her over 5 years) and checks understanding", "Links plan to category: intermediate risk with BP 148/92 = lifestyle, review and repeat BP in 2 months", "Confirms the abnormal lipids with a second test on a different day before any lipid medicine", "Offers smoking cessation advice, support and pharmacotherapy; plans AUSDRISK and CVD risk reassessment in 2 years", "Documents in the CVD Risk Management Plan and sends a copy to her usual GP"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Open:</b> &ldquo;Hi, I&rsquo;m [name], a pharmacist prescriber. Can I confirm your name and date of birth? I&rsquo;d like to ask some questions and check your blood pressure so we can estimate your chance of a heart attack or stroke. Is that OK?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Eligibility and high-risk screen:</b> &ldquo;Have you had a heart attack, stroke, angina, heart failure or an irregular heartbeat? Any kidney disease, or very high cholesterol that runs in the family? Are you pregnant or planning to be?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Identity:</b> &ldquo;We ask everyone &mdash; are you of Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander origin? What is your cultural background?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Inputs (say aloud):</b> age, sex, smoking, systolic BP, total cholesterol and HDL, diabetes, BP/cholesterol/blood-thinning medicines in the last 6 months, AF, postcode.</li><li><b>Family:</b> &ldquo;Mum&rsquo;s stroke at 72 isn&rsquo;t premature &mdash; that means a female relative under 65 or male under 55.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Pregnancy:</b> pre-eclampsia noted &mdash; raises risk; not entered in the calculator.</li><li><b>Alarm symptoms:</b> all negative.</li><li><b>BP:</b> three readings, average the last two = 148/92. Not Grade 3.</li><li><b>Reason aloud:</b> &ldquo;52, no diabetes, not First Nations &mdash; calculator applies from 45. 6% is intermediate.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Explain:</b> &ldquo;Imagine 100 people like you. Over the next 5 years about 6 would have a heart attack or stroke. Smoking, blood pressure and cholesterol are all things we can change.&rdquo; Teach-back: &ldquo;How would you explain that number to your family?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Her concern:</b> &ldquo;Not automatically. At your risk and this BP, the first step is lifestyle changes, then I recheck your BP in 2 months.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Plan:</b> smoking cessation support including medicines; diet, activity, alcohol; repeat fasting lipids on another day before any lipid medicine, then discuss benefits and harms; AUSDRISK today; reassess CVD risk in 2 years.</li><li><b>Safety-net:</b> seek medical care promptly for acute chest pain, severe headache, visual disturbance or new neurological symptoms.</li><li><b>Document:</b> risk %, category, BP, targets and plan in the CVD Risk Management Plan; copy to her GP.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD CVD Hypertension protocol V2 (F, Table 2, Appendix 1-2); QLD Lipid protocol Table 1; eTG ASCVD risk estimation; NACCHO-RACGP National Guide ch 16; AusCVDRisk results page; PHAR6202 Intensive CVD workshop; PHAR6202 Wk 9 Focused CV Assessment"},
{"id": "vc2", "title": "Aboriginal patient: risk reclassification and culturally safe referral", "cond": "htn", "time": "8 min", "type": "consult", "brief": "Mr Shane Kennedy, 34, has come in because his blood pressure was high at a workplace screening. He has no regular GP. Assess his cardiovascular risk (the examiner will give the calculator result when you ask), decide whether reclassification applies, state what that means for his management, and arrange appropriate follow-up.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>Identity:</b> only if asked &mdash; &ldquo;Yeah, I&rsquo;m Aboriginal.&rdquo; Says a previous clinic never asked.</li><li><b>Family:</b> father had a heart attack at 49; mother has type 2 diabetes.</li><li><b>History:</b> no known diabetes, kidney disease or heart disease; no regular medicines; no NSAIDs or steroids. Non-smoker. No alarm symptoms.</li><li><b>Lifestyle:</b> truck driver, long shifts, takeaway on the road, little exercise.</li><li><b>BP:</b> 152/96, 146/92, 148/92 mmHg. BMI 31 kg/m&sup2;.</li><li><b>Point-of-care tests today:</b> TC 5.4, HDL-C 1.1 mmol/L; HbA1c 5.8%. No eGFR or uACR on record.</li><li><b>Calculator result (on request):</b> 4% over 5 years.</li><li><b>Concerns:</b> &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t want to end up like Dad.&rdquo; Wants his partner involved in decisions. Went to the local Aboriginal community controlled health service as a kid and would go back if it fits around shifts.</li><li><b>Examiner question at the end:</b> &ldquo;What would change if he were 28?&rdquo;</li></ul>", "marks": ["Asks about Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander origin with a neutral question asked of everyone, not based on appearance", "Asks whether he would like his partner or family involved in decisions", "Asks permission before any touch (BP cuff) and explains each step", "Confirms Program eligibility (18–79, no exclusions) and screens for secondary causes and alarm symptoms", "States the age thresholds: AusCVDRisk from 30 for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people (45 general, 35 with diabetes)", "Identifies father MI at 49 as premature CVD (male first-degree relative under 55)", "Explains reclassification aloud: 4% sits just below the 5% threshold; Aboriginal identity and premature family history support moving him up to intermediate", "Uses hypertension Table 2 (applies from 30 for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people), not Table 3", "Applies the intermediate rule correctly: 147/92 would normally mean lifestyle and repeat in 2 months, BUT family history of premature CVD means pharmacotherapy is recommended initially, review 4–6 weeks", "SAFETY: Gets eGFR, electrolytes and uACR before starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB (and because eGFR under 60 or uACR ≥3 would make him ineligible)", "Orders annual HbA1c or FBG, eGFR and uACR; notes he is considered high risk of type 2 diabetes", "Plans CVD risk reassessment every year (eTG, First Nations)", "Offers culturally safe options (local ACCHO, Aboriginal Health Worker, annual health check) as choices, not instructions", "Enrols with concurrent GP referral (Entry point 1), with his consent, and sends the CVD Risk Management Plan", "Explains risk without causing shame or fear and checks understanding with teach-back", "Answers the examiner: at 28 the calculator is not validated; Table 3 applies (lifestyle, repeat BP in 3 months, consider ambulatory monitoring)"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Open:</b> introduce, consent. &ldquo;Is it OK if I put the cuff on your arm?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Identity:</b> &ldquo;We ask everyone this &mdash; are you of Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander origin?&rdquo; &rarr; yes. &ldquo;Thanks for telling me. Would you like anyone else involved in decisions, today or later?&rdquo;</li><li><b>History:</b> family (father MI at 49), diabetes, kidney disease, medicines including NSAIDs and steroids, smoking, alcohol, activity, alarm symptoms.</li><li><b>BP:</b> average of last two = 147/92.</li><li><b>Reason aloud (age):</b> &ldquo;He&rsquo;s 34. For Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people the calculator applies from 30.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Reason aloud (reclassify):</b> &ldquo;4% is low but just under the 5% line. Two factors apply: Aboriginal identity, considering his circumstances and community CVD prevalence, and premature family history &mdash; father under 55. I&rsquo;d reclassify to intermediate.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Reason aloud (BP):</b> &ldquo;Table 2 applies from 30 for First Nations. Intermediate, 147/92: normally lifestyle and repeat in 2 months, but with premature family history pharmacotherapy is recommended initially, review 4&ndash;6 weeks.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Safety:</b> &ldquo;I need kidney blood and urine tests first &mdash; they decide eligibility and are needed before an ACE inhibitor or ARB.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Explain:</b> &ldquo;The calculator said 4 in 100 over 5 years, but it may not fully capture risk for Aboriginal people, or when Dad had heart trouble young. So I&rsquo;m treating your risk as higher. BP tablets plus changes that fit your shifts can bring it down.&rdquo; Teach-back.</li><li><b>Options, his choice:</b> local ACCHO, an Aboriginal Health Worker, an annual health check, partner involvement. &ldquo;What would suit you?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Tests:</b> yearly HbA1c (high diabetes risk), eGFR and uACR; CVD risk reassessed yearly.</li><li><b>Refer and document:</b> enrol with concurrent GP referral, with consent; CVD Risk Management Plan to the GP he chooses; review 4&ndash;6 weeks after starting.</li><li><b>If 28:</b> calculator not validated under 30; Table 3 &mdash; lifestyle, repeat BP in 3 months, consider ambulatory monitoring.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD CVD Hypertension protocol V2 (eligibility, D, F, Tables 1-4, Appendix 2); eTG ASCVD risk estimation; NACCHO-RACGP National Guide (intro, ch 16 Box 1-2); PHAR6302 Wk 3 Culturally Safe Cardiometabolic Care; PHAR6202 Wk 4 Shared Decision Making"},
{"id": "vc3", "title": "Hesitant about a statin: shared decision with an Aboriginal patient", "cond": "lipid", "time": "8 min", "type": "comm", "brief": "Lorraine Walker, 58, is an Aboriginal woman enrolled in the CVD Risk Reduction Program for hypertension. Her CVD risk is high (calculated 11%) and repeat fasting lipids confirm LDL-C 3.9 mmol/L. Her daughter has come with her. At the last visit she said she was not keen on cholesterol tablets. Discuss lipid management with her and reach a shared plan.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>How to address her:</b> if asked, prefers &ldquo;Aunty Lorraine&rdquo;. Happy for her daughter Kylie to stay if asked; uncomfortable if it is assumed.</li><li><b>Results:</b> TC 6.1, HDL-C 1.3, LDL-C 3.9, TG 1.9 mmol/L (fasting, confirmed on repeat). LFT and CK not yet done. eGFR 78. BP at target on amlodipine.</li><li><b>Ideas:</b> &ldquo;My brother went on those cholesterol tablets and got sore legs, so he stopped.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Concerns:</b> &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t want to be rattling with tablets.&rdquo; Worried about cost. A clinic once rushed her and lectured her about her weight; she stopped going.</li><li><b>Expectations:</b> wants to talk it over with her husband before starting anything.</li><li><b>What matters:</b> looking after her grandchildren and being around for them.</li><li><b>Background:</b> no muscle or liver disease, no hypothyroidism; no amiodarone, diltiazem or clarithromycin.</li><li><b>If given real options and time:</b> agrees to baseline bloods today and a follow-up with her husband; open to starting then.</li></ul>", "marks": ["Asks how she would like to be addressed and whether she would like her daughter present, rather than assuming", "Allows time for yarning and relationship-building before the medicine discussion", "ASK: finds out what she already knows and what matters to her before giving information", "Explores the brother experience with curiosity, not dismissal", "States her targets: LDL-C at least 50% reduction and under 1.8 mmol/L whichever is lowest (here under 1.8); non-HDL-C under 2.6; TG under 1.7", "Explains that at high risk lipid-lowering medicine is recommended for all, in addition to lifestyle", "TELL: explains benefits and harms in plain language, without fear, urgency or shame", "Gives accurate muscle information: symptoms commonly reported but the causal link is overestimated; more than 90% who stopped could tolerate a statin again", "Offers genuine options: protocol default (high dose of a high-potency statin), a slower start if tolerability is the worry, lifestyle first, or time to decide", "Supports collective decision-making: welcomes her wish to talk with her husband and family", "CHECK: confirms understanding with teach-back", "EXPLORE: addresses barriers including cost, tablet burden and past healthcare experiences without becoming defensive", "SAFETY: arranges baseline LFT and CK before any statin is started", "SAFETY: safety-nets muscle symptoms: come back to discuss rather than stop alone; CK checked if symptoms develop", "Offers ACCHO or Aboriginal Health Worker support and written information", "Agrees a concrete follow-up; documents options, preferences and decision; updates the GP"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Open:</b> &ldquo;Hello, I&rsquo;m [name]. How would you like me to call you?&rdquo; &rarr; Aunty Lorraine. &ldquo;Would you like your daughter to stay while we talk?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Yarn first:</b> &ldquo;Before any numbers &mdash; how have things been for you and the family?&rdquo; Listen; don&rsquo;t rush.</li><li><b>ASK:</b> &ldquo;What do you already know about cholesterol tablets? What worries you about them?&rdquo; &rarr; brother&rsquo;s sore legs. &ldquo;That sounds like it stayed with you. Tell me more?&rdquo;</li><li><b>TELL:</b> &ldquo;Your heart check put you in the higher-risk group. For that group a cholesterol tablet is recommended as well as healthy changes. The aim is your LDL, the bad cholesterol, under 1.8.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Muscle worry:</b> &ldquo;Sore muscles get blamed on these tablets more often than they cause them. And most people who stop because of aches can take one again, often a different one or a lower dose.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Options:</b> &ldquo;We could start the usual stronger dose; start lower and build up slowly; focus on food and activity first and look again; or take time to decide with your husband. Any of these is OK to choose.&rdquo; To examiner: &ldquo;Protocol default at high risk is a high dose of a high-potency statin; eTG notes a slower start is better tolerated in some.&rdquo;</li><li><b>CHECK:</b> &ldquo;So I know I&rsquo;ve explained it well &mdash; how would you explain this to your husband?&rdquo;</li><li><b>EXPLORE:</b> cost, tablet load, the rushed clinic &mdash; acknowledge, don&rsquo;t defend.</li><li><b>AGREE:</b> baseline liver tests and CK today so she is ready either way; follow-up booked, husband welcome.</li><li><b>SUPPORT:</b> written information; offer the local ACCHO or an Aboriginal Health Worker; GP or ACCHO can check the PBS co-payment measure.</li><li><b>Safety-net (if she starts):</b> &ldquo;If your muscles ache, come and see me before stopping &mdash; there are other options.&rdquo; Lipids rechecked at 6 weeks.</li><li><b>Document:</b> options offered, her preferences, decision to defer, tests ordered, review date; update GP.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD Lipid protocol V2 (Table 1-3, F, I); eTG Lipid modification (statin regimens, adverse effects); eTG ASCVD risk estimation Table 3.2; PHAR6202 Wk 4 Shared Decision Making; PHAR6302 Wk 9 Culturally Safe Care; PHAR6202 Intensive Sept slide deck; NACCHO-RACGP National Guide"},
{"id": "vc4", "title": "Focused cardiovascular examination: ankle swelling on amlodipine", "cond": "pa", "time": "8 min", "type": "exam", "brief": "Mr Graham Lee, 67, is enrolled in the CVD Risk Reduction Program for hypertension and takes amlodipine 10 mg daily. He reports that both ankles have been puffy for 3 weeks. A brief history has been taken. Perform a focused cardiovascular examination, verbalising each step and finding to the examiner, then state your interpretation and plan. The examiner will give findings as you go and then ask what would change your plan.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>History given:</b> bilateral ankle puffiness for 3 weeks, starting about a month after amlodipine was increased to 10 mg. No breathlessness, no orthopnoea (one pillow), weight stable, walking tolerance unchanged; no chest pain, palpitations, dizziness or fainting.</li><li><b>General:</b> comfortable at rest; no cyanosis, breathlessness or pallor; no aids.</li><li><b>Hands:</b> warm and pink; no tar staining, xanthomata or clubbing; capillary refill under 2 seconds.</li><li><b>Pulse/BP:</b> radial 74, regular; no radio-radial delay; BP 134/82.</li><li><b>Face:</b> no conjunctival pallor, corneal arcus or xanthelasma; no central cyanosis.</li><li><b>Chest:</b> no scars, deformity or visible pulsations; apex beat 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line.</li><li><b>Auscultation:</b> heart sounds dual, no murmurs; lung bases clear.</li><li><b>Legs:</b> pitting oedema to both ankles only, not to mid-shin.</li><li><b>Examiner what-ifs:</b> &ldquo;What if the pulse were irregularly irregular?&rdquo; &ldquo;A loud systolic murmur in the aortic area?&rdquo; &ldquo;Orthopnoea, 4 kg weight gain and bibasal crackles?&rdquo;</li></ul>", "marks": ["Washes hands, introduces self, explains the examination in plain language and gains consent", "Positions at 45 degrees, exposes adequately and asks about pain", "General inspection: cyanosis, breathlessness, pallor, aids or equipment", "Hands: colour, tar staining, xanthomata, clubbing, temperature; capillary refill (normal under 3 seconds)", "Radial pulse rate and rhythm; radio-radial delay; BP", "Face: conjunctival pallor, corneal arcus, xanthelasma; central cyanosis", "Chest inspection; palpates apex beat (5th intercostal space, midclavicular line) and states that a laterally displaced apex may indicate cardiomegaly", "Auscultates with the diaphragm starting at the aortic area (2nd ICS right sternal border), then pulmonic (2nd ICS left), tricuspid (4/5th ICS lower left sternal border), mitral (5th ICS midclavicular line); reports heart sounds dual no murmurs", "Auscultates lung bases for inspiratory crackles", "Tests pitting oedema (firm pressure on medial shin about 3 seconds) and states its extent", "Verbalises pertinent negatives against heart failure: no orthopnoea, stable weight, apex not displaced, clear bases", "Interprets as likely amlodipine vasodilatory oedema; proposes dose reduction or a change of class, review 4–6 weeks after the change, and informs the GP", "SAFETY: Irregularly irregular pulse = possible AF; AF is outside the Program; refers for medical assessment", "SAFETY: New murmur (e.g. possible aortic stenosis) = refer for medical review; echocardiography confirms", "SAFETY: Orthopnoea + weight gain + bibasal crackles = heart failure more likely, refer; abnormal CRT, chest pain, syncope, severe hypotension or SpO2 under 92% = ABCDE and urgent escalation", "Closes: thanks the patient, explains findings, documents examination and plan"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Prepare:</b> wash hands. &ldquo;I&rsquo;d like to examine your heart and circulation &mdash; hands, face, chest and legs, your pulse, and listening with a stethoscope. Is that OK? Any pain anywhere?&rdquo; Sit him at 45&deg;, expose the chest.</li><li><b>General:</b> &ldquo;Comfortable at rest; no cyanosis, breathlessness or pallor.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Hands:</b> &ldquo;Warm and pink, no tar staining, xanthomata or clubbing. Capillary refill under 2 seconds &mdash; normal is under 3.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Pulse/BP:</b> &ldquo;Radial 74, regular, no radio-radial delay. BP 134/82.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Face:</b> &ldquo;No pallor, arcus or xanthelasma; no central cyanosis.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Chest:</b> &ldquo;No scars or visible pulsations. Apex beat 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line &mdash; not displaced, so no sign of an enlarged heart.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Auscultate (diaphragm):</b> &ldquo;Starting at the aortic area, 2nd intercostal space right sternal border; pulmonic, 2nd left; tricuspid, 4th&ndash;5th lower left sternal border; mitral, 5th midclavicular. Heart sounds dual, no murmurs.&rdquo; Lung bases: &ldquo;No crackles.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Legs:</b> press the medial shin about 3 seconds: &ldquo;Pitting oedema to both ankles, not to mid-shin.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Interpret:</b> &ldquo;Pertinent negatives for heart failure &mdash; no orthopnoea, weight stable, apex not displaced, bases clear. Bilateral ankle oedema after the dose increase fits amlodipine&rsquo;s vasodilatory effect.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Plan:</b> &ldquo;Discuss reducing the amlodipine dose or changing class, review BP 4&ndash;6 weeks after the change, and update his GP.&rdquo;</li><li><b>What-ifs:</b> irregularly irregular pulse &rarr; possible AF, outside the Program, refer for medical assessment. Loud aortic-area systolic murmur &rarr; possible aortic stenosis, refer; echocardiogram confirms. Orthopnoea + weight gain + bibasal crackles &rarr; heart failure more likely, refer. Prolonged CRT, chest pain, syncope, severe hypotension or SpO2 &lt;92% &rarr; ABCDE, urgent escalation.</li><li><b>Close:</b> thank him, explain findings, document.</li></ul>", "src": "Cardiovascular Exam Handout (D Kesby); PHAR6302 Wk 2 Physical Assessment Listen and Feel; PHAR6302 Wk 2 General Assessment Look; PHAR6202 Wk 9 Focused CV Assessment and Recognising Clinical Deterioration; QLD CVD Hypertension protocol V2 (eligibility, Table 4, M); eTG Hypertension"},
{"id": "vc5", "title": "ACE inhibitor follow-up: interpreting creatinine and potassium", "cond": "htn", "time": "8 min", "type": "interpret", "brief": "Mr David Brooks, 61, is enrolled in the CVD Risk Reduction Program at intermediate CVD risk. Twelve days ago you started perindopril arginine 5 mg daily for BP 164/98 mmHg. He returns with his repeat bloods. Review him, interpret the results aloud using the protocol rule, and state your plan. The examiner will then change one result.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>Baseline:</b> creatinine 84 &micro;mol/L, eGFR 86, K+ 4.3 mmol/L.</li><li><b>Day 12:</b> creatinine 99 &micro;mol/L, eGFR 72, K+ 4.8 mmol/L, Na+ 139 mmol/L.</li><li><b>BP today:</b> 146/90 mmHg (average of last two).</li><li><b>Symptoms:</b> mild light-headedness on standing for the first 2 days, now gone. No swelling of lips, face or tongue. No vomiting, diarrhoea or reduced fluid intake.</li><li><b>Adherence:</b> takes it every morning from a pill box.</li><li><b>Other medicines:</b> none. Asks: &ldquo;My knee&rsquo;s playing up &mdash; can I grab some Nurofen?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Examiner change:</b> &ldquo;Now the day-12 creatinine is 110 &micro;mol/L and K+ is 5.5 mmol/L. What do you do?&rdquo;</li></ul>", "marks": ["Confirms identity and rechecks Program eligibility at this visit", "Asks about adverse effects: light-headedness or dizziness (hypotension) and swelling of lips, face or tongue (angioedema)", "Asks about dehydration (vomiting, diarrhoea, poor intake) and all medicines including NSAIDs and over-the-counter products", "Checks adherence", "Calculates the creatinine rise as a percentage of baseline: (99 − 84) / 84 ≈ 18%", "Checks K+ 4.8 against the reference interval 3.5–5.2 mmol/L", "Quotes Table 4 exactly: a small rise in serum creatinine (up to 25%) OR serum potassium (within the normal range) should not necessarily prompt dose reduction or cessation", "Considers clinical context (isolated change, no symptoms) before deciding to continue", "Continues the same dose; makes no change at day 12 because response is judged at 4–6 weeks", "States the next step if above 140/90 at 4–6 weeks: add a low dose of a second agent from a different class, never an ARB with the ACE inhibitor outside specialist supervision", "SAFETY: Advises against NSAIDs (a secondary cause of raised BP; triple whammy kidney injury risk if a diuretic is added)", "SAFETY: Safety-nets angioedema: swelling of lips or airway needs urgent medical help", "SAFETY: Variation: creatinine 110 (≈31% rise) and K+ 5.5 (above 5.2) are outside the Table 4 reassurance; does not just continue; refers to the usual GP with clinical handover as an abnormal result the Program cannot manage", "States the referral rule: still above target 4–6 weeks after two first-line agents at maximum tolerated doses", "Documents results, percentage change, interpretation and plan; updates the CVD Risk Management Plan and the usual GP"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Open:</b> confirm identity. &ldquo;How have you been since starting the new tablet? Any new conditions or medicines since last time?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Adverse effects:</b> &ldquo;Any dizziness when you stand? Any swelling of your lips, face or tongue?&rdquo; &rarr; brief light-headedness, resolved; no swelling.</li><li><b>Context:</b> &ldquo;Any vomiting, diarrhoea or not drinking much? Anything from the supermarket or pharmacy?&rdquo; Adherence check.</li><li><b>Interpret aloud:</b> &ldquo;Creatinine 84 to 99 &mdash; up 15, about 18% of baseline. Potassium 4.8, inside 3.5 to 5.2. Table 4 says <i>a small rise in serum creatinine (up to 25%) or serum potassium (within the normal range) should not necessarily prompt dose reduction or cessation</i>. Both are inside that, it&rsquo;s isolated, and he&rsquo;s well. Continue the same dose.&rdquo;</li><li><b>BP:</b> &ldquo;146/90 is above his 140/90 target, but it&rsquo;s day 12. Response is judged at 4&ndash;6 weeks, so no change today.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Next steps stated:</b> &ldquo;If still above target at 4&ndash;6 weeks, add a low dose of a second agent from a different class &mdash; not an ARB with the ACE inhibitor. If above target 4&ndash;6 weeks after two first-line agents at maximum tolerated doses, refer.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Nurofen:</b> &ldquo;I&rsquo;d avoid it. Anti-inflammatories can push blood pressure up, and if we later add a fluid tablet, that mix with your BP tablet can injure the kidneys. Let&rsquo;s talk about other options for your knee.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Safety-net:</b> &ldquo;If your lips, face or throat swell, get urgent medical help.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Variation:</b> &ldquo;110 is up 26 &mdash; about 31%, over 25%. Potassium 5.5 is above 5.2. Neither part of the Table 4 reassurance applies. I wouldn&rsquo;t just carry on: this is an abnormal result the Program can&rsquo;t manage, so I&rsquo;d contact his GP with a full clinical handover and decide about the ACE inhibitor together.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Document:</b> results, % change, interpretation, plan, review date; update the CVD Risk Management Plan and send it to the GP.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD CVD Hypertension protocol V2 (eligibility, Table 2, Table 4, I, M); eTG Hypertension (ACE inhibitors, assessing and adjusting therapy); PHAR6302 Wk 2 General Assessment Look"},
{"id": "vc6", "title": "Lipid panel: targets, starting and escalating a statin", "cond": "lipid", "time": "8 min", "type": "interpret", "brief": "Mr Rob Feeney, 58, has high CVD risk (calculated 11%) and no diabetes. Part A: interpret his confirmed fasting lipid panel and start therapy within the Program. Part B: the examiner gives his 6-week result; interpret it and decide the next step. State monitoring and referral points as you go.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>Part A (confirmed on a repeat fasting test):</b> TC 6.6, LDL-C 4.4, HDL-C 1.0, TG 2.4 mmol/L (non-HDL-C 5.6). Baseline LFT and CK normal. eGFR 82. No hypothyroidism, liver, kidney or muscle disease. No amiodarone, diltiazem or clarithromycin.</li><li><b>Part B (6 weeks on atorvastatin 40 mg):</b> TC 3.6, LDL-C 2.0, HDL-C 1.1, TG 1.6 mmol/L (non-HDL-C 2.5). Takes it every night. No muscle aches.</li><li><b>Examiner variation:</b> &ldquo;What if he reported aching in both thighs that began 5 weeks after starting?&rdquo;</li></ul>", "marks": ["Checks severe hyperlipidaemia exclusions: TC ≥7.5, LDL-C ≥5.0 or TG ≥6 mmol/L (none met)", "Checks secondary causes (hypothyroidism, cholestasis, nephrotic syndrome) and that the abnormal result was confirmed on a repeat fasting test", "States high-risk targets from Table 3: LDL-C at least 50% reduction and under 1.8 mmol/L whichever is lowest; non-HDL-C under 2.6; TG under 1.7", "Works out his LDL-C target aloud: 50% of 4.4 = 2.2, so under 1.8 is lower and applies", "States that at high risk pharmacotherapy is recommended for all, alongside lifestyle", "Chooses a high dose of a high-potency statin (high risk and LDL-C over 4.0), e.g. atorvastatin 40–80 mg or rosuvastatin 20–40 mg (CrCl 30 mL/min or more)", "SAFETY: Baseline LFT and CK before initiating", "SAFETY: Screens factors that raise statin adverse-effect risk (muscle, liver or kidney disease; interacting drugs such as amiodarone, diltiazem, clarithromycin)", "Prescribes enough supply (including repeats) to reach the next review; lipids rechecked at 6 weeks", "Part B: identifies LDL-C 2.0 as a 50% or greater reduction but NOT at target because under 1.8 is lower; non-HDL-C and TG at target", "Reviews adherence and asks about muscle symptoms before escalating to a higher dose", "Escalates within scope: atorvastatin to 80 mg (maximum), then add ezetimibe if still above target; no more than two agents", "Rechecks lipids 6 weeks after the change", "SAFETY: Refers if targets are not reached 6 months after commencing therapy", "SAFETY: Muscle variation: checks CK, excludes predisposing factors, avoids stopping statins altogether (alternative statin, lower dose or intermittent dosing); refers if a raised CK cannot be managed in the Program", "Documents targets and plan in the CVD Risk Management Plan and sends a copy to the usual GP"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Part A, eligibility aloud:</b> &ldquo;TC 6.6, LDL 4.4, TG 2.4 &mdash; under the severe thresholds of 7.5, 5.0 and 6, so he stays in the Program. Secondary causes excluded; confirmed on a repeat fasting test.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Targets:</b> &ldquo;High risk: LDL at least a 50% cut and under 1.8, whichever is lower. Half of 4.4 is 2.2, so his target is under 1.8. Non-HDL under 2.6 (now 5.6); triglycerides under 1.7 (now 2.4).&rdquo;</li><li><b>Decision:</b> &ldquo;High risk, so medicine is recommended alongside lifestyle. High risk and LDL over 4.0 &mdash; a high dose of a high-potency statin: atorvastatin 40 mg daily.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Safety checks:</b> baseline LFT and CK normal; no muscle, liver or kidney disease; no amiodarone, diltiazem or clarithromycin.</li><li><b>To Rob:</b> &ldquo;This tablet lowers your bad cholesterol to cut your chance of a heart attack or stroke. One a day. If your muscles ache, come and talk to me rather than stopping.&rdquo; Teach-back. Enough repeats to reach review.</li><li><b>Monitoring:</b> lipids at 6 weeks; LFT and CK again only if clinically indicated &mdash; CK not needed unless muscle symptoms.</li><li><b>Part B, interpret:</b> &ldquo;LDL 4.4 to 2.0 &mdash; about a 55% cut, but not under 1.8, and the lower figure is the target, so not at target. Non-HDL 2.5 and TG 1.6 are at target.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Before escalating:</b> &ldquo;Are you taking it every day? Any muscle aches?&rdquo; &rarr; yes; none.</li><li><b>Escalate:</b> &ldquo;Increase atorvastatin to 80 mg, the maximum. If LDL is still above target on the maximum tolerated dose, add ezetimibe &mdash; two agents is my limit. Recheck lipids 6 weeks after each change.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Referral point:</b> &ldquo;If targets aren&rsquo;t reached 6 months after starting, refer to his GP for ongoing management.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Variation:</b> &ldquo;Bilateral, aching, large muscles, onset at 4&ndash;6 weeks &mdash; fits statin-related symptoms. Check CK; look for predisposing factors such as hypothyroidism, alcohol or strenuous exercise. Avoid stopping statins completely: another statin, a lower dose or intermittent dosing. A raised CK the Program can&rsquo;t manage &rarr; refer.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Document:</b> baseline, targets, drug and dose, monitoring dates; CVD Risk Management Plan to GP.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD Lipid protocol V2 (eligibility, D, F, G, Tables 1-3); eTG Lipid modification (statin regimens, adverse effects, statin-associated muscle symptoms)"},
{"id": "vr1", "title": "Focused respiratory examination: worse breathing in COPD", "cond": "pa", "time": "8 min", "type": "exam", "brief": "Mrs Rosa Bianchi, 70, is enrolled in the COPD Monitoring Program (mild COPD, on a LAMA). Her breathing has been worse for 4 days. The history has already been taken. Task: perform a focused respiratory examination on the simulated patient, verbalising each step. The examiner will give you findings as you go. In the last 2 minutes, explain to the patient what the findings mean and what happens next.", "patient": "<p><b>Known from history:</b> 4 days of increasing breathlessness, productive cough with yellow sputum, fever and chills. No chest pain, no haemoptysis, no calf pain.</p><p><b>Findings (give only when the candidate performs that step):</b></p><ul><li><b>Vitals:</b> BP 118/74 mmHg; HR 108 bpm; RR 26 breaths/min; SpO<sub>2</sub> 92% on room air; temperature 38.5&deg;C.</li><li><b>Inspection:</b> looks unwell and tired; speaks in full sentences with effort; intermittent accessory muscle use; no cyanosis; no finger clubbing; no pedal or sacral oedema.</li><li><b>Palpation:</b> no chest wall tenderness; chest expansion reduced over the left lower chest.</li><li><b>Percussion:</b> dull at the left base; resonant elsewhere.</li><li><b>Auscultation:</b> coarse crackles and bronchial breath sounds at the left base; reduced air entry at the left base; no wheeze.</li></ul><p><b>Patient prompt (at the end):</b> &ldquo;Can&rsquo;t you just give me the steroid tablets from my action plan?&rdquo;</p>", "marks": ["SAFETY: Performs hand hygiene, introduces self, explains the examination in plain language and obtains consent before touching", "Offers a chaperone, protects privacy and dignity, positions the patient at about 45°", "Counts respiratory rate over a full 60 seconds and measures SpO₂, HR, BP and temperature", "Interprets vitals aloud: RR 26 (normal about 12–16), HR over 100, SpO₂ 92%, fever", "General inspection: speech in full sentences, work of breathing, accessory muscles, cyanosis, clubbing, chest shape", "Checks for pedal or sacral oedema in a breathless patient", "Palpation: accessory muscles and chest expansion, comparing left with right", "Percussion: compares side to side at each level and names the note (dull = fluid or consolidation)", "Auscultation: anterior from the supraclavicular fossae and posterior in a stepladder pattern, comparing sides at each level", "Names each sound heard and what it suggests (crackles, bronchial breathing, reduced air entry, wheeze)", "Synthesises: fever, tachycardia and focal dullness with crackles point to pneumonia rather than an uncomplicated COPD exacerbation", "SAFETY: Identifies red flags — SpO₂ at 92%, RR 26, HR over 100, suspected pneumonia — and switches to an ABCDE approach", "SAFETY: Escalates to emergency care (ambulance) per the COPD protocol rather than self-management with prednisolone", "Explains the decision honestly and answers the request for steroid tablets", "Documents findings, red flags, decision and handover; informs the usual healthcare provider"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Open:</b> hand hygiene. &ldquo;Hello Mrs Bianchi, I&rsquo;m the pharmacist. I&rsquo;d like to examine your chest &mdash; look, feel, tap and listen. Is that OK? Would you like someone with you?&rdquo; Sit her at about 45&deg;; keep her covered where possible.</li><li><b>Vitals:</b> count RR for a full 60 seconds without drawing attention to it. &ldquo;RR 26 &mdash; normal is about 12&ndash;16. HR 108, over 100. SpO<sub>2</sub> 92% on room air. Temperature 38.5 &mdash; fever.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Look:</b> &ldquo;Full sentences but working hard, some accessory muscle use. No cyanosis. No clubbing &mdash; not typical of uncomplicated COPD anyway. No ankle or sacral oedema.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Feel:</b> accessory muscles, then expansion side to side. &ldquo;Expansion reduced on the left lower chest.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Percuss:</b> compare each level. &ldquo;Dull at the left base &mdash; dullness suggests fluid or consolidation. Hyperresonance would make me think pneumothorax.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Listen:</b> front from the supraclavicular fossae down; back in a stepladder pattern; compare sides at each level. &ldquo;Coarse crackles and bronchial breath sounds with reduced air entry at the left base. No wheeze.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Reasoning aloud:</b> &ldquo;New crackles localised to one lobe in COPD suggest pneumonia rather than a simple flare. With fever, HR over 100 and fast breathing, this fits pneumonia.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Red flags (ABCDE):</b> &ldquo;SpO<sub>2</sub> at 92%, RR 26, suspected pneumonia &mdash; the COPD protocol says emergency care for oxygen falling towards 92% and for suspected pneumonia.&rdquo;</li><li><b>To the patient:</b> &ldquo;Your chest findings suggest an infection in the lower left lung, and your oxygen is lower than it should be. The steroid tablets are for flare-ups you manage at home &mdash; this needs hospital assessment today. I&rsquo;m calling an ambulance now and I&rsquo;ll stay with you.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Document and hand over:</b> complaint, examination findings, red flags, decision, ambulance called; send a summary to her GP.</li></ul>", "src": "PHAR6302 Wk 6 Look/Feel/Listen pages; PHAR6302 Wk 2 Vitals (preparation); PHAR6202 Wk 9 Respiratory Assessment, Recognising Clinical Deterioration; QLD COPD protocol V2"},
{"id": "vr2", "title": "Spirometry interpretation and Program eligibility", "cond": "pa", "time": "8 min", "type": "interpret", "brief": "The examiner will hand you three spirometry reports from your pharmacy. For each patient, state the pattern (obstructive, restrictive or normal), whether bronchodilator reversibility criteria are met, and what the result means for eligibility for the Asthma or COPD Program. Reason aloud. A: Ms Lena Ho, 36, intermittent wheeze and night cough, not on any inhaled corticosteroid. B: Mrs June Taylor, 63, Aboriginal woman, ex-smoker, breathless on hills. C: Mr Sam Okafor, 55, never smoked, a year of slowly worsening breathlessness and dry cough.", "patient": "<p><b>All tests:</b> at least 3 acceptable manoeuvres; salbutamol 400 micrograms given; post-test 10&ndash;15 minutes later.</p><p><b>A &mdash; Ms Ho:</b> Pre: FEV<sub>1</sub> 2.45 L (72% predicted), FVC 3.70 L, FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC 0.66. Post: FEV<sub>1</sub> 2.90 L (85% predicted), FVC 3.85 L, FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC 0.75. Change in FEV<sub>1</sub>: +450 mL (+18%).</p><p><b>B &mdash; Mrs Taylor:</b> Reference set GLI-2012 &lsquo;other/mixed&rsquo;. Pre: FEV<sub>1</sub> 1.85 L (63% predicted), FVC 2.95 L, FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC 0.63. Post: FEV<sub>1</sub> 1.94 L (66% predicted), FVC 3.03 L, FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC 0.64. Change: +90 mL (+5%). No exacerbation in the past 3 months.</p><p><b>C &mdash; Mr Okafor:</b> Pre: FEV<sub>1</sub> 2.05 L (64% predicted), FVC 2.35 L (60% predicted), FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC 0.87. Post: FEV<sub>1</sub> 2.09 L. Change: +40 mL (+2%). Flow&ndash;volume loop: small volume.</p><p><b>Examiner follow-up question:</b> &ldquo;What if Mrs Taylor&rsquo;s post-bronchodilator FEV<sub>1</sub> had been 55% predicted?&rdquo;</p>", "marks": ["Checks test quality first — at least 3 acceptable manoeuvres, bronchodilator given, post-test at 10–15 minutes", "Defines FEV₁, FVC and the FEV₁/FVC ratio correctly", "A: identifies a reduced ratio (expiratory airflow limitation) and calculates the FEV₁ change (+450 mL, +18%)", "A: states the positive bronchodilator response criterion — increase of ≥200 mL AND ≥12%", "A: notes ≥15% and more than 400 mL increases confidence of asthma, but spirometry alone does not confirm it", "A: FEV₁ 72% predicted is above the <60% exclusion — eligible for the Asthma Program if no other exclusion applies", "B: uses the post-bronchodilator values — FEV₁/FVC ≤0.7 with FEV₁ <80% predicted = persistent airflow limitation", "B: grades severity as mild (FEV₁ 60–79%) and notes the ratio is not near 0.7, so no repeat is needed", "B: cultural safety — confirms GLI-2012 other/mixed reference values were used for an Aboriginal patient", "B: eligible for the COPD Program (aged ≥35, mild) subject to the other exclusion criteria", "C: identifies a restrictive pattern — low FVC with a normal or increased FEV₁/FVC ratio — with examples (ILD, chest wall disease, obesity)", "SAFETY: C is ineligible for both Programs (restrictive pattern) — refers to the GP with a comprehensive clinical handover", "SAFETY: Follow-up: FEV₁ 55% = moderate COPD and below the 60% threshold — ineligible, refer", "Explains a result to the patient in plain language and documents results for the usual healthcare provider"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Quality:</b> &ldquo;All three have at least three acceptable manoeuvres, 400 micrograms salbutamol and a post-test at 10&ndash;15 minutes, so I can interpret them.&rdquo;</li><li><b>A:</b> &ldquo;Pre-bronchodilator ratio 0.66 &mdash; reduced, so expiratory airflow limitation. FEV<sub>1</sub> rose 450 mL, 18%: meets &ge;200 mL <i>and</i> &ge;12%, and &ge;15% with more than 400 mL increases confidence of asthma. It fits her history, but spirometry alone doesn&rsquo;t confirm asthma. FEV<sub>1</sub> 72% predicted &mdash; above the &lt;60% exclusion. She can enter the Asthma Program if nothing else excludes her.&rdquo;</li><li><b>B:</b> &ldquo;GLI-2012 &lsquo;other/mixed&rsquo; values were used, which is right for an Aboriginal patient. Post-bronchodilator ratio 0.64 and FEV<sub>1</sub> 66% &mdash; &le;0.7 with FEV<sub>1</sub> &lt;80%: persistent airflow limitation. Change 90 mL, 5% &mdash; not a significant response. Mild COPD, 60&ndash;79%. The ratio isn&rsquo;t near 0.7, so no repeat. She&rsquo;s 63 &mdash; eligible for the COPD Program if no exclusion applies.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Follow-up:</b> &ldquo;At 55% that&rsquo;s moderate COPD and below 60% &mdash; ineligible; I&rsquo;d refer to her GP with a handover.&rdquo;</li><li><b>C:</b> &ldquo;FVC low at 60% with a ratio of 0.87 and a small-volume loop &mdash; low FVC with a normal or increased ratio is a restrictive pattern, for example interstitial lung disease, chest wall disease or obesity. No meaningful bronchodilator response. A restrictive pattern makes him ineligible for both Programs. I&rsquo;d refer him to his GP with a comprehensive handover.&rdquo;</li><li><b>To a patient (A):</b> &ldquo;Your airways were narrower than expected and opened up well after the reliever &mdash; that pattern fits asthma, together with your symptoms.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Document:</b> values, reference set, interpretation, eligibility decision; send results to each GP.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD Asthma protocol V2 (Eligibility, Appendix 5); QLD COPD protocol V2 (flowchart G, Appendix 3–4); eTG Pulmonary function testing (Table 9.27)"},
{"id": "vr3", "title": "Asthma review: reliever overuse on as-needed budesonide-formoterol", "cond": "asthma", "time": "8 min", "type": "consult", "brief": "Ms Priya Nair, 41, has asthma and is enrolled in the Improved Asthma Symptom Control Program. She uses budesonide-formoterol 200/6 (Turbuhaler) as needed only. She is here for her scheduled review and says she has been using it a lot more lately. Task: review her asthma, decide on management within the Program, and explain your plan to her.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>Reliever:</b> budesonide-formoterol on 4&ndash;5 days a week for the last 6 weeks, 1&ndash;2 inhalations each time.</li><li><b>Other inhalers (only if asked):</b> bought salbutamol over the counter twice in the last 2 months &mdash; &ldquo;the other one tastes funny&rdquo;. 2 canisters this year.</li><li><b>Control:</b> wakes coughing about 1 night a week; has stopped her gym classes. ACT score if done: 16.</li><li><b>Exacerbations:</b> one 8 months ago &mdash; GP gave prednisolone. Never in ED, hospital or ICU for asthma.</li><li><b>Triggers:</b> worse since winter colds; no new pets; symptoms the same on days off work.</li><li><b>Alarm screen:</b> no haemoptysis, chest pain, weight change, night sweats, fever, orthopnoea or oedema.</li><li><b>Background:</b> not pregnant or planning; no food allergy or anaphylaxis; no diabetes or heart disease; no specialist care; no competitive sport. Spirometry 14 months ago confirmed asthma, FEV<sub>1</sub> 82% predicted.</li><li><b>Technique (when asked to show):</b> breathes in slowly and gently through the Turbuhaler.</li><li><b>Concern:</b> &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t want to be on steroids every day.&rdquo;</li></ul>", "marks": ["Introduces self, confirms identity, explains the purpose of the review and gains consent", "SAFETY: Re-checks eligibility — ICU ever, ED or hospital in the past month, ≥2 admissions or ≥3 ED visits in a year, alarm symptoms, pregnancy", "Assesses control over the past 4 weeks: daytime symptoms, reliever days, night waking, activity limitation (or ACT)", "Classifies control as poor and says why (any one feature is enough; ACT ≤19)", "Asks about other inhalers, finds the OTC salbutamol and counts canisters (≥12 a year = treat and refer)", "Identifies risk factors for severe attacks — previous severe exacerbation, frequent SABA use", "Checks inhaler technique by demonstration BEFORE stepping up, and corrects it with a placebo device", "Checks adherence, triggers and a workplace pattern (suspected occupational asthma excludes) before stepping up", "States the rule: on as-needed ICS-formoterol only, reliever need ≥3 times a week means add maintenance (switch to MART)", "Prescribes MART: budesonide-formoterol 200/6 one inhalation twice daily plus one as needed, maximum 2400/72 micrograms a day including maintenance", "Advises one inhaler for relief (the ICS-formoterol) and explains why SABA alone is not used", "Shared decision-making: addresses her steroid concern — ICS is indicated for every adult with asthma", "SAFETY: Updates the written Asthma Action Plan — maximum doses, prednisolone up to 5 days with no repeats and when to start it, doctor review after starting, ambulance signs", "Arranges review within 2–3 months of the step-up and gives reasons to return sooner", "Documents and sends the updated Action Plan and medicine change to her usual GP"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Open:</b> confirm identity, purpose, consent.</li><li><b>Eligibility re-check:</b> &ldquo;Any ED or hospital visits for asthma in the last month? Ever in intensive care? Coughing blood, chest pain, weight change, fevers, night sweats, swelling? Pregnant or planning?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Control (last 4 weeks):</b> &ldquo;How many days a week do you have symptoms or use your inhaler? Wake at night? Stopped doing anything?&rdquo; &rarr; 4&ndash;5 days, night waking, stopped gym, ACT 16 = <b>poor control</b>.</li><li><b>Risk:</b> &ldquo;Any other puffers?&rdquo; OTC salbutamol, 2 canisters this year &mdash; under the 12-canister trigger, but frequent SABA use plus last year&rsquo;s prednisolone raises her risk of a severe attack.</li><li><b>Before stepping up:</b> &ldquo;Can you show me how you use it?&rdquo; Slow, gentle breath &mdash; correct with a placebo and the device checklist. Check adherence, triggers, and whether symptoms ease on days off work (occupational asthma would exclude her).</li><li><b>Reasoning aloud:</b> &ldquo;On as-needed budesonide-formoterol alone, needing it 3 or more times a week means adding maintenance &mdash; switch to maintenance-and-reliever therapy.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Plan:</b> budesonide-formoterol 200/6, 1 inhalation twice daily, plus 1 inhalation when needed; maximum 2400/72 micrograms a day including maintenance (12 inhalations). Same inhaler for relief &mdash; stop the salbutamol.</li><li><b>Shared decision:</b> &ldquo;I hear you about daily steroids. Every adult with asthma needs an inhaled steroid. This low dose, in the inhaler you already use, lowers your risk of a bad attack.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Action Plan (written, updated):</b> maximum dose at one time and per 24 h. Prednisolone up to 5 days, no repeats (eTG adult dose 37.5&ndash;50 mg once daily) &mdash; only if symptoms keep coming back within 4 hours or don&rsquo;t settle after the reliever <i>and</i> a doctor can&rsquo;t see her straight away; see a doctor as soon as possible after starting. Call 000 for severe breathing difficulty, rapid worsening, reliever not working, trouble speaking sentences, blue lips or drowsiness.</li><li><b>Review:</b> within 2&ndash;3 months of the step-up; sooner if worse, side effects or device trouble; and after any flare.</li><li><b>Communicate:</b> Action Plan, change and next review date to her GP; document.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD Asthma protocol V2 (eligibility, adjusting pharmacotherapy, self-management, review, Appendix 6); eTG Maintenance management of asthma (Step 3 MART); eTG Acute asthma (corticosteroids)"},
{"id": "vr4", "title": "COPD Program enrolment: still breathless on a LAMA", "cond": "copd", "time": "8 min", "type": "consult", "brief": "Mr Graham Pike, 67, ex-smoker, has had COPD for 3 years and would like to join the COPD Monitoring Program. He uses tiotropium 2.5 microgram Respimat, 2 inhalations once daily, and an ipratropium puffer for relief several times a day. He is still short of breath walking to the shops. Task: confirm eligibility, assess his symptoms, and agree a management plan with him.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>Spirometry letter (8 months ago):</b> post-bronchodilator FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC 0.63; FEV<sub>1</sub> 68% predicted; no significant bronchodilator response.</li><li><b>CAAT today:</b> 17. <b>Breathlessness:</b> walks slower than people his age on the flat because of breathlessness.</li><li><b>Exacerbations:</b> one in the last 12 months &mdash; GP gave prednisolone only, no antibiotics. Never hospitalised for COPD.</li><li><b>Ipratropium:</b> 2 puffs 3&ndash;4 times a day. <b>Tiotropium:</b> every morning; technique correct when demonstrated.</li><li><b>Screen:</b> no heart failure, AF, angina, stroke, DVT or other heart disease; BP today 134/82; no diabetes; no mental health condition; no specialist in the last 12 months; no haemoptysis, chest pain, weight change, night sweats, fever or ankle swelling.</li><li><b>Vaccines:</b> influenza vaccine last year, not this year; unsure about pneumococcal; nothing else he recalls.</li><li><b>Rehab:</b> never offered. &ldquo;Isn&rsquo;t that a gym thing for younger people?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Social:</b> quit smoking 5 years ago; lives with his wife.</li></ul>", "marks": ["Introduces self, explains the Program and gains consent to enrol", "SAFETY: Checks eligibility — aged ≥35, never hospitalised for COPD, never an exacerbation needing OCS plus antibiotics, fewer than 2 treated exacerbations in 12 months", "SAFETY: Screens exclusions — complex cardiovascular disease (incl. BP ≥180 and/or ≥110), alarm symptoms, poorly controlled mental health, specialist care, diabetes status", "Uses spirometry from the last 12 months and confirms FEV₁ ≥60% predicted (68% = mild)", "Interprets CAAT 17 as moderate impact (10–20) and knows 21 or more means refer to a medical practitioner", "Uses the mMRC dyspnoea scale and records the grade", "Checks inhaler technique and adherence before stepping up", "SAFETY: Identifies ipratropium is contraindicated with a LAMA; stops it and prescribes salbutamol via pMDI with spacer as reliever", "Steps from LAMA monotherapy to LAMA+LABA, e.g. tiotropium+olodaterol 2.5/2.5 micrograms Respimat, 2 inhalations once daily, replacing tiotropium alone", "Minimises devices (same Respimat) and demonstrates with a placebo device", "States the referral trigger: not stabilised or worse after 1 month on dual therapy (adding ICS is outside the Program)", "Reviews vaccination — influenza, pneumococcal, RSV, COVID-19, pertussis, herpes zoster — against the Immunisation Handbook", "Recommends pulmonary rehabilitation and answers his concern (benefit regardless of severity)", "Provides a COPD Action Plan and lifestyle prescription; considers lung cancer screening referral if eligible", "Arranges review at 3 months and sends the plan to his GP"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Open:</b> explain the Program, consent to enrol, he can opt out at any time.</li><li><b>Eligibility:</b> 67, so &ge;35. &ldquo;Ever been in hospital for your lungs? Ever needed steroid tablets <i>and</i> antibiotics for a flare? How many flares needed tablets in the last year?&rdquo; One, prednisolone only &mdash; still eligible. Screen heart disease (heart failure, AF, angina, stroke, BP &ge;180 systolic and/or &ge;110 diastolic), alarm symptoms, mental health, diabetes, specialist care.</li><li><b>Spirometry (within 12 months):</b> ratio 0.63, FEV<sub>1</sub> 68% &mdash; mild, above the 60% cut-off.</li><li><b>Symptoms:</b> &ldquo;CAAT 17 is moderate impact; 21 or more and I&rsquo;d refer you to your doctor.&rdquo; Record mMRC grade.</li><li><b>Technique and adherence:</b> watch the Respimat &mdash; correct, taken daily.</li><li><b>Reasoning aloud:</b> &ldquo;Still symptomatic on a LAMA alone with good technique and adherence &mdash; Step 3, LAMA plus LABA. And ipratropium is contraindicated with a LAMA.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Plan:</b> stop ipratropium; salbutamol 100&ndash;200 micrograms via pMDI with spacer when needed. Replace tiotropium with tiotropium+olodaterol 2.5/2.5 micrograms Respimat, 2 inhalations once daily &mdash; same device, one fewer inhaler. Demonstrate with a placebo.</li><li><b>Safety-net:</b> &ldquo;If you&rsquo;re not settled, or worse, after a month on it, I&rsquo;ll refer you to your GP &mdash; the next step isn&rsquo;t one I can start.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Vaccines:</b> influenza due; check pneumococcal, RSV, COVID-19, pertussis and shingles against the Immunisation Handbook.</li><li><b>Pulmonary rehab:</b> &ldquo;It improves exercise capacity and quality of life whatever your severity. Programs usually run at least 8 weeks, 2&ndash;3 sessions a week.&rdquo; Refer via Lung Foundation Find a Service.</li><li><b>Also:</b> COPD Action Plan and lifestyle prescription; lung cancer screening if eligible.</li><li><b>Close:</b> review in 3 months (CAAT change of 2 is meaningful); send plan to GP; document.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD COPD protocol V2 (eligibility, CAAT/mMRC, H non-pharmacological, stepwise pharmacotherapy, M review); eTG Stable COPD (LAMA/LABA, Table 9.15, rehab, vaccines); LFA Stepwise Management of Stable COPD (2026)"},
{"id": "vr5", "title": "COPD flare-up: self-management with the action plan", "cond": "copd", "time": "8 min", "type": "consult", "brief": "Mr Tony Russo, 64, is enrolled in the COPD Monitoring Program (mild COPD, on umeclidinium-vilanterol once daily and salbutamol as needed). Since yesterday he has been more breathless than usual and is using his reliever more. He asks what he should do, and whether you can give him antibiotics just in case. Task: assess him, decide whether he can self-manage, and go through his COPD Action Plan, including when to start prednisolone and when to call an ambulance.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>Symptoms:</b> more breathless for 1 day; coughing more; sputum usual amount, white, unchanged. No fever, shivers, chest pain, palpitations or leg swelling. Can walk around the house, eat and sleep.</li><li><b>Reliever:</b> salbutamol 2&ndash;4 puffs; relief lasts about 5&ndash;6 hours.</li><li><b>Observations (when measured):</b> RR 20; HR 88; SpO<sub>2</sub> 95% on room air; temperature 36.8&deg;C; BP 132/80; speaks in full sentences; not drowsy or confused; no cyanosis.</li><li><b>History:</b> no exacerbations in the last 12 months; never hospitalised. No prednisolone at home yet.</li><li><b>Request:</b> &ldquo;My brother has amoxicillin in his rescue pack. Can you give me some just in case?&rdquo;</li></ul>", "marks": ["Introduces self, confirms identity and gains consent", "SAFETY: Screens ambulance criteria first — severe breathlessness, chest pain or palpitations, cannot manage at home, drowsiness or confusion, cyanosis, SpO₂ falling towards 92%, leg swelling", "Measures RR, HR, SpO₂, temperature and BP and interprets them as stable", "Screens for bacterial infection — sputum volume and purulence, fast breathing at rest, HR over 100, rigors — and notes sputum colour alone is unreliable", "Asks about reliever use, how long relief lasts, exacerbations in the past 12 months and hospitalisation", "Reliever plan: salbutamol up to 8 puffs one at a time via spacer, repeated as required; no ipratropium while on a LAMA", "Writes the maximum dose at one time and per 24 hours on the Action Plan", "States the prednisolone trigger: symptoms persist or recur within 4 hours after reliever use", "Supplies prednisolone for up to 5 days, no repeats: 30–50 mg once daily for 5 days, no taper needed", "SAFETY: Tells him to arrange medical practitioner review as soon as possible after starting prednisolone", "SAFETY: Declines antibiotics — not prescribable in the Program; refers to a doctor if signs of bacterial infection develop", "States ambulance criteria apply even if he has already started prednisolone", "Knows the eligibility consequence: an exacerbation needing OCS plus antibiotics, or ≥2 treated exacerbations in 12 months, means referral out of the Program", "Arranges return for Action Plan review after the flare, encourages pulmonary rehabilitation, documents and updates the GP"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Open:</b> identity, consent. &ldquo;Tell me what&rsquo;s changed since yesterday.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Danger first:</b> &ldquo;Any chest pain, fluttering or racing heart? Swollen legs? Feeling drowsy or muddled? Can you get between rooms, eat and sleep?&rdquo; Obs: RR 20, HR 88, SpO<sub>2</sub> 95%, 36.8&deg;C, full sentences &mdash; no ambulance criteria.</li><li><b>Infection screen:</b> sputum volume and thickness, shivers, fast breathing at rest, HR over 100 &mdash; none. &ldquo;Sputum colour on its own isn&rsquo;t a reliable sign of infection.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Reasoning aloud:</b> &ldquo;Mild flare, stable observations, no signs of bacterial infection &mdash; safe to self-manage with his action plan.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Reliever:</b> salbutamol up to 8 puffs, one at a time, through the spacer, repeated as needed; maximum at one time and per 24 hours written on the plan. No ipratropium &mdash; it clashes with his LAMA.</li><li><b>Prednisolone:</b> supply up to 5 days, no repeats: 30&ndash;50 mg once daily for 5 days; no tapering needed. &ldquo;Start only if your symptoms persist or come back within 4 hours of using your reliever &mdash; and then book a doctor as soon as possible.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Antibiotics:</b> &ldquo;I can&rsquo;t prescribe antibiotics in this program, and they only help when a bacterial infection triggers the flare. If you get more or thicker sputum, shivers, or a fast heart rate, see your doctor.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Call 000 &mdash; even if you&rsquo;ve started the tablets &mdash; if:</b> breathing becomes severe; chest pain, fluttering or a racing or slow heartbeat; you can&rsquo;t manage at home; you&rsquo;re drowsy or confused; lips turn blue; oxygen drops towards 92%; your legs swell; you can&rsquo;t keep the tablets down; infection or pneumonia is suspected.</li><li><b>Eligibility:</b> &ldquo;If a flare ever needs steroids <i>and</i> antibiotics, or you have two treated flares in a year, I&rsquo;ll hand your care back to your GP.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Close:</b> teach-back; come back after this flare to review the plan; pulmonary rehab after an exacerbation; document; update GP.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD COPD protocol V2 (self-management of exacerbations, ambulance list, eligibility, review); eTG COPD exacerbations (bronchodilator, systemic corticosteroids); LFA My COPD Action Plan (Apr 2026)"},
{"id": "vr6", "title": "Acute asthma at the pharmacy counter", "cond": "asthma", "time": "8 min", "type": "consult", "brief": "Ms Kate Brennan, 28, comes to the counter wheezing. She says her asthma is bad and she left her puffer at home. You are the pharmacist on duty. The examiner will give you her observations and how she responds to treatment. Task: assess severity, manage her, and plan follow-up.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>On arrival:</b> walked in; speaks whole sentences in one breath; alert; no cyanosis; no accessory muscle use. RR 24; HR 110; SpO<sub>2</sub> 95% on room air.</li><li><b>Quick history (if asked):</b> asthma since her teens; salbutamol only, no preventer; cold for 3 days. No allergen exposure, no lip or tongue swelling, no rash.</li><li><b>After the first round of treatment (4 minutes):</b> no real change.</li><li><b>After the second round:</b> now speaking only a few words at a time; neck muscles working; SpO<sub>2</sub> 91%; still alert.</li><li><b>Paramedics arrive at 7 minutes.</b></li><li><b>Examiner final question:</b> &ldquo;She comes back a week later after being discharged from the emergency department and asks to join the Asthma Program. What do you do?&rdquo;</li></ul>", "marks": ["Rapid first look before a long history: can she speak whole sentences, work of breathing, alertness, cyanosis", "Measures SpO₂, RR and HR and classifies initial severity as mild to moderate (whole sentences, SpO₂ above 94%)", "Briefly considers anaphylaxis as a differential (allergen exposure, swelling, rash)", "Starts first aid promptly: salbutamol 100 micrograms, 4 puffs one at a time via spacer, 4 breaths after each puff", "Repeats after 4 minutes and reassesses after each round", "SAFETY: Calls an ambulance (000) when there is still no response — advises it is a severe asthma attack", "SAFETY: Recognises severe features — cannot complete sentences, accessory muscle use, SpO₂ below 92%", "Continues 4 puffs (up to 8 in an adult) every 4 minutes while waiting, and stays with her", "Communicates calmly and explains each step to the patient", "Hands over to paramedics: timeline, doses and times given, serial observations, relevant history", "Knows the alternative if salbutamol is unavailable: budesonide-formoterol 2 actuations, then 1 every 4 minutes, maximum 6 doses", "Knows that if she had responded but needed reliever more than 3–4 hourly, same-day medical review is required", "SAFETY: Follow-up — an ED visit in the past month makes her ineligible for the Asthma Program; refers to her GP with a handover", "Explains that SABA alone is not appropriate and she needs ICS-containing treatment and a written action plan", "Documents the event (times, doses, observations, ambulance call) and informs her GP"], "model": "<ul><li><b>First look (seconds):</b> whole sentences, alert, no cyanosis; SpO<sub>2</sub> 95%, RR 24, HR 110. &ldquo;Mild to moderate for now.&rdquo; Quick check: &ldquo;Any new food, sting or medicine? Lips or tongue swelling? Rash?&rdquo; &mdash; anaphylaxis is a key differential.</li><li><b>First aid now:</b> &ldquo;Kate, I&rsquo;m going to give you salbutamol through a spacer &mdash; slow breaths for me.&rdquo; Salbutamol 100 micrograms, 4 puffs, one at a time, 4 breaths after each. Wait 4 minutes; reassess.</li><li><b>Round 2:</b> no change &rarr; repeat 4 puffs; reassess.</li><li><b>Reasoning aloud:</b> &ldquo;She can&rsquo;t finish sentences now, she&rsquo;s using accessory muscles and SpO<sub>2</sub> is 91% &mdash; under 92%. Severe, and not responding.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Escalate:</b> call 000 (or have a colleague call) &mdash; &ldquo;severe asthma attack&rdquo;. Continue 4 puffs (up to 8 in an adult) every 4 minutes. Stay with her; watch for drowsiness, exhaustion, cyanosis.</li><li><b>If no salbutamol:</b> budesonide-formoterol 2 actuations, 1 more after 4 minutes, then ambulance and 1 every 4 minutes, maximum 6 doses.</li><li><b>Handover to paramedics:</b> 3-day cold, no preventer, times and doses given, serial observations.</li><li><b>Had she settled:</b> if she needed her reliever more than 3&ndash;4 hourly, same-day medical review.</li><li><b>One week later:</b> &ldquo;Because you went to the emergency department in the last month, I can&rsquo;t manage you in the Asthma Program right now. A reliever on its own isn&rsquo;t safe long-term &mdash; you need an inhaled-steroid-containing inhaler and a written action plan. Please see your GP this week; I&rsquo;ll send them a summary.&rdquo; Check her spacer technique as usual pharmacy care.</li><li><b>Document:</b> times, doses, observations, ambulance call and handover; inform her GP.</li></ul>", "src": "eTG Acute asthma (first aid 4×4×4, Table 9.10 severity, follow-up); QLD Asthma protocol V2 (eligibility, ambulance criteria, key considerations); PHAR6202 Wk 9 Respiratory Assessment and Recognising Clinical Deterioration (red flags)"},
{"id": "vr7", "title": "Smoking cessation in a patient with COPD", "cond": "smoke", "time": "8 min", "type": "consult", "brief": "Mr Darren Walker, 56, an Aboriginal man, is enrolled in the COPD Monitoring Program (mild COPD, on tiotropium and salbutamol). At his review he says he wants to quit smoking for good this time. Task: assess his smoking and nicotine dependence, decide what you can provide under the Queensland Smoking Cessation Clinical Practice Guideline, agree a plan with him, and arrange follow-up.", "patient": "<ul><li><b>Smoking:</b> 25 cigarettes a day since age 15. First cigarette within 5 minutes of waking. Strong cravings; irritable and restless when he tries to stop.</li><li><b>Past attempt:</b> last year used a 14 mg patch on its own for 3 weeks; cravings won; relapsed. Never tried varenicline.</li><li><b>Medicines:</b> tiotropium, salbutamol. Nothing else, no supplements.</li><li><b>Health:</b> no psychiatric history, depression or self-harm; 2&ndash;3 beers at weekends; no skin reactions to medicines. Recent bloods: kidney function normal (CrCl about 85 mL/min). BP today 136/84.</li><li><b>Home:</b> his partner also smokes; grandchildren visit often &mdash; &ldquo;I want to be around for them.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Preference:</b> &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t want patches stuck on me all day. A mate used a tablet.&rdquo;</li><li><b>If offered:</b> would like his Aboriginal Health Worker at the local health service kept in the loop.</li></ul>", "marks": ["Builds rapport, asks permission to discuss smoking, listens to his reasons — culturally safe and non-judgemental", "Takes a smoking history: amount, age started, pack-years, time to first cigarette, cravings and withdrawal, past attempts and treatments", "Assesses dependence with HSI or FTND items and states he is highly dependent (smokes within 30 minutes of waking)", "Identifies why the last attempt failed — low-dose patch monotherapy (underdosing)", "SAFETY: Screens for medicines that interact with smoking (tobacco smoke induces CYP1A2) — an interacting medicine would require referral", "Screens psychiatric history, renal function and pregnancy status as relevant, and measures BP", "SAFETY: Applies the CPG: COPD is a chronic disease — treat and concurrently refer to the GP and Quitline", "Offers first-line options of similar effectiveness (varenicline or combination NRT) and lets him choose", "Prescribes varenicline for CrCl ≥30: 0.5 mg daily for 3 days, 0.5 mg twice daily for 4 days, then 1 mg twice daily to complete 12 weeks", "Sets a quit date within the first 1–2 weeks of treatment", "Counsels adverse effects: nausea (take with food), insomnia (second dose mid-afternoon), withdrawal symptoms, mood changes — seek care", "Arranges follow-up at 2 weeks (progress, adverse effects, BP, support) and at 12 weeks; mentions an optional second 12-week course", "Discusses passive smoking at home as a COPD risk factor", "Involves his Aboriginal Health Worker with consent and shares the plan with his GP", "Considers referral for lung cancer screening if eligible; documents the plan"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Open:</b> &ldquo;Thanks for bringing this up, Darren. Is it OK if we talk about your smoking? What makes now the right time?&rdquo; Listen &mdash; the grandkids. &ldquo;Would you like your Aboriginal Health Worker involved?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Ask and assess:</b> 25 a day since 15 &mdash; record pack-years; first cigarette within 5 minutes of waking; strong cravings; last try a 14 mg patch alone, relapsed at 3 weeks.</li><li><b>Dependence:</b> HSI items (time to first cigarette, cigarettes per day). &ldquo;You&rsquo;re highly dependent &mdash; people who smoke within 30 minutes of waking need strong treatment.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Reasoning aloud:</b> &ldquo;Last time was underdosing &mdash; a lower-dose patch on its own. Varenicline and combination NRT work about equally well.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Safety screen:</b> medicines &mdash; tobacco smoke induces CYP1A2; an interacting medicine would mean referral (none). Psychiatric history (none). Kidney function: CrCl about 85. BP 136/84.</li><li><b>Scope:</b> &ldquo;Because you have COPD, I&rsquo;ll start treatment and also refer you to your GP and Quitline for extra support.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Options, his choice:</b> (1) nicotine 21 mg/24 h patch plus 4 mg lozenge or gum 1&ndash;2 hourly as needed (maximum 16 in 24 h), at least 12 weeks; or (2) varenicline. He chooses varenicline.</li><li><b>Prescribe:</b> varenicline 0.5 mg daily for 3 days, 0.5 mg twice daily for 4 days, then 1 mg twice daily to complete 12 weeks. &ldquo;Most people stop smoking naturally in the first 1&ndash;2 weeks &mdash; let&rsquo;s pick a quit date in that window.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Counsel:</b> nausea &mdash; take with food; trouble sleeping &mdash; second dose mid-afternoon; withdrawal symptoms in the first week &mdash; stop and seek advice; mood changes &mdash; seek medical care.</li><li><b>Home:</b> passive smoke also harms his lungs &mdash; talk about a smoke-free home.</li><li><b>Follow-up:</b> 2 weeks (progress, side effects, BP, support) and 12 weeks; a second 12-week course can be considered. &ldquo;Slips are common &mdash; come back, don&rsquo;t be discouraged.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Close:</b> lung cancer screening if eligible; with consent, share the plan with his GP and Aboriginal Health Worker; document.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD Smoking Cessation CPG (1 Jul 2025); eTG Varenicline; eTG Nicotine replacement therapy; PHAR6302 Wk 5 Patient History (FTND/HSI); RACGP Supporting smoking cessation (CYP1A2); QLD COPD protocol V2; PHAR6302 Wk 6 COPD in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people"},
{"id": "vx1", "title": "Sick-day counselling: metformin + SGLT2 inhibitor with gastroenteritis", "cond": "bgl", "time": "8 min", "type": "consult", "brief": "Mrs Leila Haddad, 59, has type 2 diabetes. You prescribe her metformin and empagliflozin under the Blood Glucose Program; her GP prescribes perindopril. She has had vomiting and diarrhoea since last night and asks whether she should keep taking her tablets. Assess her and counsel her on sick-day management. A simulated patient plays Mrs Haddad.", "patient": "<b>If asked:</b><ul><li>Started about 18 hours ago. Vomited 3 times (last 4 hours ago), diarrhoea 5 times. Partner had the same 2 days ago.</li><li>Keeping down small sips of water. Eating very little. Mild crampy discomfort only &mdash; no severe abdominal pain, no blood, no fever.</li><li>Feels &ldquo;washed out&rdquo; and a bit tired. Alert, speaking normally, not breathless.</li><li>Took metformin, empagliflozin and perindopril this morning.</li><li>Home blood glucose this morning 9.6 mmol/L. Has a blood ketone meter (given when the SGLT2 inhibitor was started): 0.3 mmol/L this morning.</li><li>No written sick-day plan.</li></ul><b>Concern (volunteer):</b> &ldquo;My doctor told me never to stop my diabetes tablets.&rdquo;<br><b>Teach-back (if asked):</b> first answer is &ldquo;So I stop the diabetes ones until I feel better?&rdquo; &mdash; she has missed perindopril and the restart rule unless the candidate corrects it.", "marks": ["Creates privacy, confirms identity, and takes a focused history (onset, vomiting/diarrhoea frequency, fluids kept down, abdominal pain, blood, fever) before advising", "Takes a complete medicine list, including the GP-prescribed perindopril, and what was taken today", "Asks for her blood glucose AND blood ketone readings, and whether she has a sick-day plan", "SAFETY: Advises her to withhold empagliflozin now — acute illness with vomiting/diarrhoea and low intake raises ketoacidosis risk", "SAFETY: Advises her to withhold metformin while she has vomiting, diarrhoea or is not eating much", "Identifies perindopril as a SADMANS medicine and addresses it (withhold while dehydration risk persists) — and says the GP will be told because it is the GP’s prescription", "Gives the restart rule: resume once eating and drinking normally again", "Fluids: increase fluid intake; non-glucose fluids if BGL >15 mmol/L; oral rehydration solution if BGL <15 mmol/L", "Monitoring: BGL every 2–4 hours; check blood ketones (not urine) if BGL stays ≥15 mmol/L on two readings 2 hours apart, or if she feels worse", "SAFETY: Explains euglycaemic ketoacidosis in plain words — she can be seriously unwell with a near-normal glucose; warning signs are abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, tiredness, breathlessness, fruity breath, confusion", "SAFETY: Clear escalation — unable to keep fluids down, not well enough to follow the plan, signs of dehydration, ketoacidosis symptoms, or ketones above the urgent threshold → seek medical care / attend hospital today", "Uses genuine teach-back on the conditional instructions (not “does that make sense?”) and corrects the gap", "Provides written information (NDSS sick-day factsheet) and states she will notify the GP so a sick-day plan can be developed/updated", "Acknowledges her belief about never stopping tablets and explains why sick days are the exception — empathic, jargon-free"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Open and assess first:</b> &ldquo;Before I answer, can I ask a few quick questions about how unwell you are?&rdquo; &mdash; vomiting/diarrhoea count, fluids, pain, blood, fever, what she took today, BGL and ketones.</li><li><b>Reason aloud:</b> &ldquo;She is on an SGLT2 inhibitor and metformin with a dehydrating illness. Glucose 9.6 does not reassure me on its own &mdash; ketones are the safety check.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Stop for now:</b> &ldquo;While you are vomiting or not eating much, please stop your empagliflozin and your metformin. Your perindopril is on the same &lsquo;sick-day&rsquo; list &mdash; hold it too while you are losing fluids, and I will let Dr X know because she prescribes it.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Restart:</b> &ldquo;Start them again once you are eating and drinking normally.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Fluids:</b> &ldquo;Keep sipping. If your sugar is over 15, use water or sugar-free fluids; under 15, an oral rehydration drink is fine.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Monitor:</b> &ldquo;Check your sugar every 2 to 4 hours. Use the blood ketone meter &mdash; not urine strips &mdash; if your sugar stays at 15 or more on two checks two hours apart, or if you feel worse.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Explain the hidden risk:</b> &ldquo;With this tablet, ketones can build up even when your sugar looks normal. Tummy pain, more vomiting, unusual tiredness, fast breathing, fruity breath or feeling confused are warning signs.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Escalate (clear thresholds):</b> &ldquo;If you cannot keep fluids down or feel too unwell to follow this plan, go to hospital. If your ketone reading is above 1.5, you need urgent medical review today &mdash; do not wait for the morning.&rdquo; (Ketone figures from NSW ACI hospital guidance: normal &le;0.6; &ge;1.0 test for acidosis if unwell; &gt;1.5 urgent clinical review.)</li><li><b>Teach-back:</b> &ldquo;If you woke up tomorrow still vomiting, what would you do with each tablet?&rdquo; &mdash; correct the perindopril and restart gaps.</li><li><b>Close:</b> NDSS sick-day factsheet; notify GP of withheld medicines so a sick-day plan can be developed; document advice.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD Blood Glucose protocol (Sick day management); RACGP Diabetes sick-day management Table 1; PHAR6202 Diabetes participant pack (SADMANS, blood ketones, teach-back); KHA CKD Handbook 5th ed (SADMANS); NSW ACI SGLT2i in acute care (ketone thresholds — hospital guidance); PHAR6202 Wk 9 GI assessment"},
{"id": "vx2", "title": "Pathology after starting perindopril: continue, adjust or refer?", "cond": "htn", "time": "8 min", "type": "interpret", "brief": "Mr Graham Pike, 61, started perindopril under the Hypertension Program two weeks ago. His follow-up bloods are back. Interpret the results aloud, decide what to do with the perindopril, and explain your decision to Mr Pike, who is worried his kidneys are being damaged. The examiner may change the results afterwards and ask what you would do then.", "patient": "<table><tr><th></th><th>Before starting</th><th>Day 14</th></tr><tr><td>Creatinine</td><td>80 &micro;mol/L</td><td>96 &micro;mol/L</td></tr><tr><td>eGFR</td><td>86 mL/min/1.73m2</td><td>70 mL/min/1.73m2</td></tr><tr><td>Potassium</td><td>4.4 mmol/L</td><td>4.9 mmol/L</td></tr><tr><td>Sodium</td><td>139 mmol/L</td><td>138 mmol/L</td></tr></table><b>If asked:</b> no dizziness, no illness, no vomiting or diarrhoea, no new medicines; takes ibuprofen &ldquo;now and then&rdquo; for his back (none this fortnight). BP today 146/90 mmHg.<br><b>Concern:</b> &ldquo;I read online that these pills wreck your kidneys. My numbers went up &mdash; should I stop?&rdquo;<br><b>Examiner probe (after the decision):</b> &ldquo;Suppose the day-14 creatinine was 104 &micro;mol/L and potassium 5.4 mmol/L. What now?&rdquo;", "marks": ["Confirms which result is baseline and which is follow-up, and that bloods were due before starting and 1–2 weeks after", "Calculates the creatinine rise correctly: 80 → 96 µmol/L = 20%", "Compares potassium against the reference interval 3.5–5.2 mmol/L — 4.9 is within range", "SAFETY: States the QLD Table 4 rule accurately — a small rise in creatinine (up to 25%) OR potassium within the normal range should not necessarily prompt dose reduction or cessation (does not misquote as 30%)", "Notes eGFR remains ≥60 (normal per the protocol) and the fall is less than 25% within two weeks (KHA: ACE inhibitor should be continued)", "Decision verbalised: continue perindopril at the same dose — reasoning stated aloud, not just the answer", "Checks for confounders before concluding — dehydrating illness, NSAID use, new medicines", "Explains to the patient in plain language: an expected, reversible drop in kidney blood flow when this medicine starts — not damage", "Addresses his wish to stop with shared decision-making rather than instruction", "Gives sick-day advice: perindopril is a SADMANS medicine — withhold during a dehydrating illness, restart when eating and drinking normally; avoid self-medicating with NSAIDs", "Plans follow-up: BP review 4–6 weeks after starting; update the GP after this occasion of care", "SAFETY: Probe — recognises a 30% creatinine rise and potassium 5.4 mmol/L fall outside the Table 4 allowance; does not continue unchanged; contacts the GP / refers (abnormal pathology not manageable in the Program)", "SAFETY: Probe — knows the KHA rule that a GFR fall >25% below baseline means the ACE inhibitor should be ceased and nephrology referral considered, and that K+ >6.5 mmol/L needs the Emergency Department", "Documents results, interpretation and plan"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Orient:</b> &ldquo;These are his baseline bloods and the 2-week check &mdash; the protocol asks for kidney function and electrolytes before starting and 1&ndash;2 weeks later.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Calculate aloud:</b> &ldquo;Creatinine 80 to 96 is a 20% rise. Potassium 4.9 is inside 3.5&ndash;5.2. eGFR 70 is still &ge;60, and the fall from 86 is under 25%.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Apply the rule exactly:</b> &ldquo;Table 4 says a small rise in creatinine, up to 25%, <i>or</i> potassium within the normal range, should not necessarily prompt dose reduction or cessation. Both are within that here.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Check confounders:</b> any vomiting, diarrhoea, ibuprofen or new medicines this fortnight?</li><li><b>Decide:</b> &ldquo;Continue perindopril at the same dose. Recheck BP at 4&ndash;6 weeks.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Explain to Mr Pike:</b> &ldquo;When this tablet starts, it slightly reduces blood flow through the kidney filters, so this number rises a little. That is expected and reversible &mdash; it is not damage. Your result is inside the safe range we watch for, so stopping would lose the benefit for your blood pressure.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Safety-net:</b> &ldquo;If you get vomiting or diarrhoea and cannot keep fluids up, hold the perindopril until you are eating and drinking normally. Please check with me before taking ibuprofen.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Probe answer:</b> &ldquo;104 is a 30% rise, and 5.4 is above the 5.2 upper limit, so this is outside the allowance. I would not continue unchanged on my own authority &mdash; I would contact the GP today with the results; this is abnormal pathology I cannot manage in the Program. KHA says if GFR falls more than 25% below baseline the ACE inhibitor should be ceased and nephrology referral considered. A potassium above 6.5 would mean the Emergency Department.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Close:</b> document, send update to GP.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD Hypertension protocol V2 (Table 4; I: Monitoring; M; eligibility; uACR and eGFR); KHA CKD Management in Primary Care 5th ed (RAS inhibitors clinical tip; Hyperkalaemia; SADMANS)"},
{"id": "vx3", "title": "Written record: starting a statin in the Lipid Modification Program", "cond": "comm", "time": "8 min", "type": "doc", "brief": "Mr Arjun Mehta, 64, was enrolled in the Lipid Modification Program today. He has no previous diagnosis of dyslipidaemia. After shared decision-making he has agreed to start a statin. Using the information sheet, write the clinical record entry and CVD Risk Management Plan for this consultation, including what you will send to his GP. The examiner marks the written entry.", "patient": "<b>Information sheet:</b><ul><li>64 years, non-smoker. BP today 134/82 mmHg.</li><li>AusCVDRisk calculated today: <b>high</b>.</li><li>Fasting lipids (last week): total cholesterol 6.6, LDL-C 4.4, HDL-C 1.0, triglycerides 2.0, non-HDL-C 5.6 mmol/L.</li><li>Baseline liver function and CK (last week): within reference range.</li><li>No regular medicines; occasional paracetamol. No known drug allergies or previous adverse drug reactions.</li><li>Verbal consent to Program participation given today.</li><li>Agreed: high-dose, high-potency statin (candidate names agent and dose per Therapeutic Guidelines), plus diet and physical activity advice.</li><li>GP: Dr Ruth Abbott. Review appointment booked.</li></ul>", "marks": ["Date, time, patient identifiers and prescriber details recorded; entry is legible, structured and contemporaneous", "Documents informed consent for Program participation", "Documents eligibility check, including that TC <7.5 and LDL-C <5.0 mmol/L (not severe hyperlipidaemia — which would need referral)", "Records allergies, intolerances and previous adverse drug reactions (none known) explicitly — not left blank", "Records AusCVDRisk category (high) and the lipid, LFT and CK baseline results", "Assessment links the decision to the protocol: high CVD risk → pharmacotherapy recommended for all, plus lifestyle; high risk / LDL-C >4.0 → high dose of a high-potency statin", "Medicine fully specified: name, strength, dose, frequency, quantity/repeats", "SAFETY: Individualised targets written and the calculation shown — LDL-C: 50% of 4.4 = 2.2, so <1.8 mmol/L is the lower and applies; non-HDL-C <2.6; triglycerides <1.7 mmol/L", "Advice documented: how to take the medicine, non-pharmacological measures, managing adverse effects, when to seek care, when to return", "Monitoring plan: repeat lipids at 6 weeks; LFT/CK re-tested if clinically indicated", "Review date (6 weeks) recorded, and the 6-month rule (refer if targets not reached) noted", "Collaborative care: copy of the CVD Risk Management Plan to the GP with history and pathology, new pharmacotherapy, advice/referrals and next review date", "States concurrent referral to the GP for further review and collaborative care (entry point 1)", "No unexplained abbreviations; objective, non-judgemental wording"], "model": "<p><b>Record entry (example):</b></p><ul><li><b>Date/time; pharmacist prescriber name and signature.</b> Arjun Mehta, DOB, address.</li><li><b>Consent:</b> informed consent to Lipid Modification Program participation obtained and documented today.</li><li><b>Eligibility:</b> no ineligibility criteria. TC 6.6 (&lt;7.5), LDL-C 4.4 (&lt;5.0) &mdash; not severe hyperlipidaemia. No previous dyslipidaemia diagnosis &rarr; entry point 1.</li><li><b>Allergies/ADRs:</b> nil known. <b>Medicines:</b> paracetamol PRN.</li><li><b>Assessment:</b> AusCVDRisk high. Lipids: TC 6.6, LDL-C 4.4, HDL-C 1.0, TG 2.0, non-HDL-C 5.6 mmol/L. LFT and CK within range (baseline).</li><li><b>Plan:</b> high CVD risk &rarr; pharmacotherapy recommended plus lifestyle. High risk and LDL-C &gt;4.0 &rarr; high-dose high-potency statin: [agent, strength, dose, frequency, quantity, repeats &mdash; per TG Cardiovascular]. Diet and physical activity advice given.</li><li><b>Targets (calculation shown):</b> LDL-C &mdash; 50% reduction from 4.4 = 2.2; &lt;1.8 is lower, so target <b>&lt;1.8 mmol/L</b>. Non-HDL-C &lt;2.6. TG &lt;1.7 mmol/L.</li><li><b>Counselling:</b> medicine use; managing adverse effects; when to seek care; when to return; written information provided.</li><li><b>Monitoring:</b> repeat lipids at 6 weeks; LFT/CK if clinically indicated. If targets not reached 6 months after starting &rarr; refer.</li><li><b>Review:</b> [date] (6 weeks).</li><li><b>Collaborative care:</b> concurrent referral to Dr Abbott for further review. Copy of CVD Risk Management Plan sent with history, pathology, new statin, advice given and next review date.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD Lipid Modification protocol V2 (entry points; Table 1; Pharmacotherapy options; G; Tables 2–3; I; J; K); National Prescribing Competencies Framework (6.3d, 4.6, calculations)"},
{"id": "vx4", "title": "ISBAR phone handover: asthma beyond the Program", "cond": "comm", "time": "8 min", "type": "comm", "brief": "You manage Ms Hana Kowalski, 38, in the Improved Asthma Symptom Control Program. Today she reports poor control despite medium-dose ICS-formoterol maintenance-and-reliever therapy, with good inhaler technique and adherence. You have decided she needs referral. Phone her GP, played by the examiner, and hand over using ISBAR. The GP is busy.", "patient": "<b>Candidate notes (from today):</b><ul><li>Last 4 weeks: daytime symptoms 5 days/week; waking with symptoms 2 nights/week; has stopped her morning walks. ACT score 14.</li><li>Regimen: medium-dose ICS-formoterol MART for 4 months. Technique demonstrated correctly; device intact; dispensing history matches daily use.</li><li>Spirometry 3 months ago: FEV1 78% predicted, reversible airflow limitation.</li><li>No ED visits or hospitalisations; no oral corticosteroids this year.</li><li>No alarm features (no haemoptysis, chest pain, fever, night sweats, weight change, orthopnoea, oedema). Speaking in full sentences today.</li><li>New kitten at home for 2 months. Non-smoker.</li></ul><b>GP lines:</b> opens with &ldquo;I have two minutes &mdash; what is it?&rdquo;. Midway: &ldquo;Can you not just step her up yourself?&rdquo;. At the end asks: &ldquo;So what exactly do you want from me?&rdquo;", "marks": ["I — states own name, role (pharmacist prescriber) and pharmacy, confirms who they are speaking to, identifies the patient", "S — one clear sentence: poor asthma control despite Level 3 therapy, needs GP review because it is outside the Program", "B — relevant history only: current regimen, no exacerbations/ED, spirometry FEV1 78%, new kitten", "A — gives own clinical judgement using the 4-week criteria: daytime symptoms >2 days/week, night waking, activity limitation; ACT 14 (≤15 = very poor control)", "A — states that technique and adherence were checked and are good, so this is not a fixable technique problem", "SAFETY: Explains she now needs treatment consistent with Level 4, which makes her ineligible for the Program — does not step up herself", "R — specific, actionable request with a timeframe: GP review as soon as possible to consider stepping up beyond Level 3", "R — states what has already been done within scope: technique and adherence review, trigger discussion, Asthma Action Plan copy to follow", "SAFETY: Confirms the patient was told when to seek emergency care (e.g. unable to complete a sentence in one breath)", "Handles the “step her up yourself” pushback assertively and respectfully (I-message / graded assertiveness) without capitulating", "Prioritises — concise, leads with what changes the GP’s decision, within the time the GP gives", "Closes the loop: who does what, by when; confirms written handover (Asthma Action Plan, spirometry, symptom control) will be sent", "Professional, collaborative tone throughout", "States the call will be documented"], "model": "<ul><li><b>I</b> (course materials define S, B, A and R only &mdash; the opening simply identifies who is calling about whom): &ldquo;Dr Lee, it is [name], pharmacist prescriber at [pharmacy]. I am calling about Hana Kowalski, 38, date of birth [x].&rdquo;</li><li><b>S:</b> &ldquo;Her asthma is poorly controlled despite medium-dose ICS-formoterol MART, and I need you to review her because the next step is outside my Program.&rdquo;</li><li><b>B:</b> &ldquo;On MART four months. No ED visits or oral steroids this year. Spirometry three months ago: FEV1 78% predicted, reversible. New kitten at home for two months.&rdquo;</li><li><b>A:</b> &ldquo;Over the last four weeks she has had daytime symptoms five days a week, night waking twice a week and has stopped her walks; ACT 14, which indicates very poor control. Her technique and adherence are good, so I do not think this is a device problem. She now needs Level 4 treatment, which makes her ineligible for the Program.&rdquo;</li><li><b>R:</b> &ldquo;Could you see her as soon as possible to consider stepping her up? I have not changed her treatment. I have discussed the kitten as a possible trigger and told her that if she cannot finish a sentence in one breath she needs emergency care.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Pushback:</b> &ldquo;I understand you are stretched. I am uncomfortable stepping her up because Level 4 is outside the protocol I prescribe under &mdash; I would like her to be seen by you.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Close the loop:</b> &ldquo;So you will see her this week, and I will send her Asthma Action Plan, spirometry and symptom-control summary today. Anything else you need from me?&rdquo;</li><li>Document the call and the agreed plan.</li></ul>", "src": "QLD Improved Asthma Symptom Control protocol V2 (Eligibility; A; Appendix 6; J; K; L); PHAR6302 Assessment 3 brief; PHAR6202 Wk 5 SBAR; PHAR6202 Wk 7 assertive communication (I-messages, CUSS)"},
{"id": "vx5", "title": "Administering an IM influenza vaccine — and managing anaphylaxis", "cond": "inj", "time": "8 min", "type": "skill", "brief": "Ms Grace Moore, 52, has come in for her 2026 influenza vaccine. Complete the pre-vaccination assessment and consent, select and justify the needle, site and technique, and talk the examiner through administration on the manikin arm. The examiner will then tell you what happens next. Finish by stating what you would document.", "patient": "<b>If asked:</b><ul><li>Well today; no change in health. Weight 98 kg, BMI 37.</li><li>Egg allergy &mdash; hives as a child. Has never had anaphylaxis. No previous reaction to an influenza vaccine or its components.</li><li>Fainted once during a blood test. Anxious about needles. Prefers the left arm.</li></ul><b>Examiner, about 5 minutes after the injection:</b> &ldquo;She says her throat feels tight and itchy. There are hives across her chest, her voice is hoarse and you can hear a wheeze.&rdquo;", "marks": ["Pre-administration checks: appropriate today, change in health, contraindications/precautions, correct medicine, storage, previous adverse effects", "States the only absolute contraindications are anaphylaxis after a previous influenza vaccine dose or a vaccine component — egg allergy is NOT a contraindication", "Informed consent covers purpose, benefits, common adverse effects, post-vaccination monitoring and follow-up; invites questions; confirms she may decline; consent documented", "Addresses fainting history: seated and positioned properly; advises her to lie down if she feels giddy or light-headed", "Hand hygiene, aseptic technique, PPE; confirms emergency equipment and adrenaline are ready before starting", "Needle: 22–25 gauge, 38 mm (woman >90 kg / high BMI) — justified; not the 25 mm default", "Site and technique: deltoid, 90°, flattening technique, sleeve fully off; inspects site; landmarks correctly to avoid too-high injection (SIRVA)", "Does not aspirate; injects smoothly; withdraws in one motion; light pressure about 30 seconds; does not massage", "Disposes of the needle immediately into a sharps container without recapping", "SAFETY: Recognises anaphylaxis (airway/breathing signs plus hives) and distinguishes it from a vasovagal episode", "SAFETY: Activates emergency procedures and gives adrenaline 1:1000 by deep IM injection, mid-outer thigh, at 0.01 mL/kg up to a maximum 0.5 mL — states 0.5 mL for 98 kg; 1 mL syringe", "SAFETY: Repeats adrenaline every 5 minutes until clinical improvement; does not rely on antihistamine or hydrocortisone; does not use 1:10,000", "Arranges urgent medical care and stays with her", "Documentation: medicine name, brand, dose, route, site, batch number, expiry, date and time, pharmacist details; AIR reporting; adverse event recorded and communicated to the GP; formal ADR report"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Assess:</b> &ldquo;Are you well today? Any reaction to a flu vaccine before?&rdquo; &mdash; check medicine, storage, previous adverse effects.</li><li><b>Contraindications:</b> &ldquo;The only absolute contraindications are anaphylaxis after a previous flu vaccine or one of its components. Egg allergy, even anaphylaxis, is not one &mdash; you can safely have it.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Consent:</b> purpose, benefit, common side effects (redness, tenderness or swelling at the site), monitoring afterwards, follow-up; &ldquo;Any questions? You are free to say no.&rdquo; Document consent.</li><li><b>Fainting history:</b> &ldquo;Sit back and rest your forearm on your thigh. If you feel light-headed at any point, tell me and we will lie you down.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Prepare:</b> hand hygiene, aseptic technique, PPE; adrenaline and emergency kit within reach.</li><li><b>Needle and site:</b> &ldquo;22&ndash;25 gauge, 38 mm, because she is over 90 kg &mdash; a 25 mm needle may only reach fat. Left deltoid, sleeve right off, flattening technique, 90&deg;. Landmark carefully &mdash; too high risks the shoulder joint (SIRVA).&rdquo;</li><li><b>Inject:</b> no aspiration; smooth plunger; withdraw in one motion; light pressure about 30 seconds; no rubbing. Needle straight into sharps, not recapped.</li><li><b>Reaction:</b> &ldquo;Throat tightness, hoarse voice, wheeze and hives &mdash; this is anaphylaxis, not a faint. I am activating our emergency procedure and calling for help.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Adrenaline:</b> &ldquo;Adrenaline 1:1000, 0.01 mL per kg &mdash; 98 kg gives 0.98, so the maximum 0.5 mL &mdash; deep IM into the mid-outer thigh with a 1 mL syringe. Repeat every 5 minutes until she improves. Antihistamines or hydrocortisone are not the treatment.&rdquo; Arrange urgent medical care; stay with her.</li><li><b>Document:</b> vaccine name, brand, dose, route, site, batch, expiry, date and time, my details; report to AIR; record the reaction, inform the GP, submit an ADR report.</li><li><b>Observation period:</b> course materials do not state the routine time &mdash; follow the Immunisation Handbook &lsquo;After vaccination&rsquo; and local policy.</li></ul>", "src": "PHAR6302 Wk 10: Preparing for Safe Administration; Needle/Site Selection (incl. Immunisation Handbook needle table); Monitoring and Managing Adverse Events; Documentation; Australian Immunisation Handbook — Influenza (contraindications, egg allergy); PDL Risk Management Guide (formal ADR reporting)"},
{"id": "vx6", "title": "Recognising deterioration: chest infection with abnormal observations", "cond": "pa", "time": "8 min", "type": "interpret", "brief": "Mrs Joan Ellery, 71, asks you for a strong antibiotic for a chest infection she has had for three days. Her daughter says she seems muddled today. You have taken her observations; the examiner will give them when asked. Verbalise your reasoning and decide what happens next.", "patient": "<b>History (if asked):</b> productive cough 3 days, fevers, breathlessness much worse since this morning. Hypertension (perindopril). Daughter: &ldquo;She is not making sense today &mdash; she is normally sharp.&rdquo; Speaks in short phrases, stops to breathe.<br><b>Observations (on request):</b><ul><li>Respiratory rate 32/min</li><li>SpO2 89% on room air</li><li>Heart rate 118 bpm</li><li>BP 94/58 mmHg</li><li>Temperature 38.7 &deg;C</li><li>Blood glucose not known</li></ul><b>Pressure line:</b> &ldquo;I just want tablets so I can go home and sleep it off.&rdquo;", "marks": ["Asks for observations early rather than proceeding to the antibiotic request", "Identifies SpO2 89% as a red flag (below 92%)", "Identifies respiratory rate 32/min as a red flag (above 30/min)", "Identifies inability to speak in full sentences and new confusion (altered conscious state) as red flags", "Recognises fever + tachycardia + low BP + confusion as possible sepsis — an ABCDE trigger; notes low BP in context without inventing a numeric threshold", "Verbalises the shift: the question is not the diagnosis but whether she is deteriorating and needs urgent medical assessment", "Works through ABCDE aloud: airway, breathing (RR, SpO2, work of breathing), circulation (HR, BP, perfusion), disability (confusion, glucose where appropriate), exposure (fever, rash)", "States this is outside pharmacist-led management", "SAFETY: Escalates immediately — calls an ambulance (000); does not send her home, to the GP later, or let her drive", "SAFETY: Does not prescribe or supply an antibiotic as a substitute for escalation", "Explains to patient and daughter calmly and honestly why she needs urgent hospital assessment", "Stays with the patient and re-checks observations while waiting", "Hands over to paramedics in a structured way (SBAR) and informs the GP", "Documents findings, red flags, clinical concerns and actions"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Pause the request:</b> &ldquo;Before we talk about antibiotics, I need to check how you are breathing.&rdquo; Ask for observations.</li><li><b>Step 5 &mdash; red flags, aloud:</b> &ldquo;Oxygen 89% &mdash; below 92%. Breathing rate 32 &mdash; above 30. She cannot finish a sentence and is newly confused. Fever, heart rate 118, low blood pressure: this could be sepsis.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Reframe:</b> &ldquo;The question is not what the diagnosis is &mdash; it is whether she is deteriorating and needs urgent medical assessment. She is.&rdquo;</li><li><b>ABCDE quickly:</b> A &mdash; talking, no stridor. B &mdash; RR 32, SpO2 89%, working hard. C &mdash; HR 118, BP 94/58, check perfusion. D &mdash; confused; glucose if available. E &mdash; fever 38.7, check for rash.</li><li><b>Scope:</b> &ldquo;This is outside what I can manage in a pharmacy.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Escalate:</b> &ldquo;I am calling an ambulance now.&rdquo; No antibiotic supply; she does not go home or drive.</li><li><b>Explain:</b> &ldquo;Joan, your oxygen is low and you are breathing very fast. That needs a hospital team today &mdash; tablets from me would not be safe. I will stay with you until the ambulance comes.&rdquo;</li><li><b>While waiting:</b> stay; repeat observations; note any change.</li><li><b>Handover (SBAR) to paramedics:</b> S &mdash; 71-year-old, 3-day productive cough, worse breathlessness and new confusion today. B &mdash; hypertension on perindopril. A &mdash; RR 32, SpO2 89% RA, HR 118, BP 94/58, T 38.7; concern for sepsis/respiratory compromise. R &mdash; urgent hospital assessment.</li><li><b>After:</b> notify GP; document findings, concerns and actions.</li></ul>", "src": "PHAR6202 Wk 9: Recognising Clinical Deterioration (ABCDE, red flags); Respiratory Assessment (urgent escalation features, escalation options); Linking History, Assessment and Reasoning (documentation); PHAR6302 Wk 9 Preventing Harm (000); PHAR6202 Wk 5 SBAR"},
{"id": "vx7", "title": "Distress disclosed at a LAIB appointment: ask, assess, escalate", "cond": "mh", "time": "8 min", "type": "consult", "brief": "Mr Daniel Reeves, 34, receives monthly long-acting injectable buprenorphine at your pharmacy and is due today. While you prepare, he says his partner has left, he has been drinking more, and adds: I do not really see the point any more. Respond and decide what happens next. A simulated patient plays Mr Reeves.", "patient": "<b>Reveals if asked sensitively:</b><ul><li>Partner left 3 weeks ago. Drinking most nights (about 6 cans). Sleeping badly, stopped seeing friends, not working.</li><li>Not intoxicated now: alert, clear speech, last drink last night.</li><li><b>Only if asked directly about suicide:</b> &ldquo;Yeah&hellip; I have thought about ending it this week. There are sleeping tablets at home that she left behind. I have not done anything.&rdquo;</li><li>No illicit opioid use; worried he might &ldquo;go back to using&rdquo;.</li><li>Has a GP he trusts. His mother lives nearby and he would let her be called.</li></ul><b>If the candidate suggests help:</b> &ldquo;I will just go home and ring someone tomorrow.&rdquo;", "marks": ["Moves to a private space, explains what will happen, uses respectful person-first language", "Responds to the disclosure with empathy rather than moving on to the injection", "Explores warning signs: hopelessness, social withdrawal, increased alcohol use, loss of engagement", "SAFETY: Asks directly and respectfully about suicidal thoughts — does not avoid the question", "Explores plan and means (sleeping tablets at home) and current intoxication", "Avoids guilt-inducing statements (e.g. how family would feel)", "SAFETY: Classifies this as HIGH concern (active suicidal thoughts) requiring urgent assessment and escalation", "SAFETY: Does not simply advise him to seek help independently — rejects the go-home-and-ring-tomorrow plan", "SAFETY: Escalates now with him: urgent GP contact / local mental health crisis service (Mental Health Line) / ED referral / ambulance if needed", "Involves family or carers with his agreement (mother)", "Addresses overdose risk: alcohol and sedatives with opioids; offers take-home naloxone and overdose recognition education for him and his mother", "Notifies his OAT prescriber; avoids an unplanned gap in treatment, which itself raises overdose risk", "Uses trauma-informed principles: choice, collaboration, confidentiality, no judgement", "Documents clinical findings, risk assessment, actions and follow-up"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Pause and make space:</b> &ldquo;Daniel, that sounds really hard. Before we do your injection, can we talk somewhere private for a few minutes?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Explore:</b> &ldquo;How have you been sleeping? Seeing anyone? How much are you drinking at the moment?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Ask directly:</b> &ldquo;When you say you do not see the point &mdash; are you having thoughts of ending your life?&rdquo; (Asking directly does not increase risk.)</li><li><b>Plan and means:</b> &ldquo;Have you thought about how? Is there anything at home you might use?&rdquo; Check he is not intoxicated now.</li><li><b>Reason aloud:</b> &ldquo;Active suicidal thoughts with access to tablets, plus heavier drinking &mdash; this is high concern and needs urgent assessment today.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Respond to &lsquo;I will ring tomorrow&rsquo;:</b> &ldquo;I am glad you told me. I am not comfortable with you going home alone today. Can we call your GP together now? If we cannot reach them, I will contact the mental health crisis line with you, or we go to the emergency department.&rdquo; Ambulance if he cannot be kept safe.</li><li><b>Support network:</b> &ldquo;Would it help if we rang your mum to be with you?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Overdose risk:</b> &ldquo;Alcohol or sleeping tablets on top of buprenorphine raise the risk of overdose. I would like you and your mum to have naloxone and know how to use it.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Treatment continuity:</b> inform his OAT prescriber; plan so his treatment is not interrupted, because a gap also raises overdose risk.</li><li><b>Avoid:</b> &ldquo;Think how your family would feel&rdquo; &mdash; guilt shuts the conversation down.</li><li><b>Close:</b> document findings, risk level, who was contacted and the follow-up plan.</li></ul>", "src": "PHAR6302 Wk 8: Recognising Risk and Knowing When to Escalate; Mental Health Stigma and Opioid Dependence; Trauma-Informed and Culturally Safe Care. PHAR6302 Wk 9: Safety, Risk Recognition and Escalation; Preventing Harm and Improving Outcomes; Person-Centred, Trauma-Informed Care"}
);
var OSCE_GUIDE = "<div class='box blu'><b>Assessment 3 &mdash; Multi-station OSCE.</b> 50%, <b>must pass</b> (minimum standard set by standard-setting). Thu 26 or Fri 27 Nov 2026, on campus, one day. Station timings, rubric and preparatory guidance are released on Canvas before the exam. Supplementary OSCE possible; pass caps the course mark at 50.</div>\n<h3>What examiners mark</h3>\n<p class='small'>Not only the decision: <b>the reasoning used to reach it, and how you communicate it</b>. Say your thinking out loud.</p>\n<ul><li>Gather relevant information efficiently and accurately</li><li>Apply clinical reasoning appropriately</li><li><b>Recognise red flags and patient-safety concerns</b></li><li>Select suitable management and prescribing options</li><li>Communicate clearly and respectfully</li><li>Professional behaviour</li><li>Document accurately when required</li><li><b>Work within the pharmacist prescriber&rsquo;s scope</b></li></ul>\n<h3>What a station can ask you to do</h3>\n<p class='small'>Focused consultation &middot; history &middot; perform or interpret physical assessment &middot; prioritise problems &middot; differentials &middot; reasoning &middot; management plan &middot; safe prescribing &middot; deprescribing &middot; referral/escalation &middot; talk to patients <i>and</i> health professionals &middot; documentation &middot; culturally responsive, person-centred care &middot; legal/ethical principles. Stations may use simulated patients, health professionals or written materials.</p>\n<h3>The 8-minute skeleton (every consult station)</h3>\n<ol><li><b>Open:</b> hands, introduce, confirm identity, purpose of the service and your scope, <b>consent</b>, privacy.</li><li><b>Agenda:</b> their concern in their words; ICE (ideas, concerns, expectations).</li><li><b>Focused history:</b> presenting problem &rarr; red flags early &rarr; PMH, medicines (incl. OTC/complementary), allergies, social/smoking/alcohol, family history.</li><li><b>Eligibility:</b> state out loud whether the patient is inside the Program and why.</li><li><b>Examine / measure</b> with a question in mind &mdash; say what you&rsquo;re looking for.</li><li><b>Reason aloud:</b> &ldquo;Most likely&hellip; must not miss&hellip; safe in my scope?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Plan with the patient:</b> options, shared decision, non-drug + drug, dose, how to take it.</li><li><b>Safety-net:</b> what to watch for, when/where to seek help (ambulance vs GP), when to come back.</li><li><b>Close:</b> teach-back, written info, review date, <b>document and communicate</b> to the usual GP.</li></ol>\n<div class='box red'><b>A high score with a missed red flag can still fail.</b> In every station: eligibility &rarr; red flags &rarr; scope &rarr; referral. A safe referral beats an inappropriate prescription.</div>\n<h3>Your likely high-yield areas</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Know cold</th><th>Practise here</th></tr>\n<tr><td>CVD risk assessment</td><td>Who to assess, calculator inputs (clinic BP), AusCVDRisk bands (low &lt;5%, intermediate 5 to &lt;10%, high &ge;10%), reclassification factors, QLD thresholds/targets</td><td>Stations vc1, vc2, vc5, vc6, o1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Physical examination</td><td>CV and respiratory sequence; inspection &rarr; palpation &rarr; percussion &rarr; auscultation; what each finding supports; red flags (SpO&#8322; &lt;92%, RR &gt;30, silent chest, cyanosis, can&rsquo;t complete sentences)</td><td>vc4, vr1, vx6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Asthma</td><td>ICS for everyone (never SABA alone), AIR/MART, control vs risk (ACT bands), technique before step-up, prednisolone self-start rule, ambulance criteria</td><td>vr3, vr6, o2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>COPD</td><td>Spirometry (post-BD FEV&#8321;/FVC &lt;0.7; QLD FEV&#8321; &ge;60%), steps 1&ndash;3, no ipratropium with a LAMA, triple = refer, prednisolone up to 5 days, antibiotics = refer, rehab, vaccines</td><td>vr1, vr2, vr4, vr5, o4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cultural safety</td><td>Ask, don&rsquo;t assume; rapport first; family/kin involvement; ACCHO; AusCVDRisk/assessment from age 30 for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people (QLD); cultural safety is judged by the patient</td><td>vc2, vc3, e6</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Phrase bank (say these)</h3>\n<ul><li>&ldquo;Before we start, I&rsquo;d like to check that it&rsquo;s okay to ask some personal questions and take your blood pressure.&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;Some people I see identify as Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander &mdash; do you?&rdquo; (ask everyone, not on appearance)</li><li>&ldquo;What I&rsquo;m checking for here is&hellip;&rdquo; (during the exam)</li><li>&ldquo;The most likely explanation is&hellip;, but I need to rule out&hellip;&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;This is outside what I can safely manage, so I&rsquo;m going to refer you to&hellip; today, and here&rsquo;s why.&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;If you notice&hellip;, call 000 / go to ED &mdash; don&rsquo;t wait for your appointment.&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;So I know I&rsquo;ve explained it well, can you tell me how you&rsquo;ll take it?&rdquo;</li></ul>\n<h3>On the day</h3>\n<ul><li>No electronic devices, smart watches, <b>calculators</b> or your own equipment &mdash; everything is provided. Practise mental arithmetic for % changes (e.g. creatinine rise, LDL reduction).</li><li>Arrive early with student ID; late arrivals may not be admitted.</li><li>AI tools not permitted.</li></ul>\n<h3>Countdown plan</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Now &rarr; November intensive (Callaghan: Sat 31 Oct &amp; Sun 1 Nov; Gosford stream Thu&ndash;Fri):</b> one station a day out loud + Practise &rarr; Review my mistakes. Learn the exam sequences (Study &rarr; Physical assessment).</li><li><b>Intensive:</b> respiratory assessment, spirometry, pathology, injectables, travel health, Advanced OSCE prep &mdash; bring questions; send me any mock stations or rubric afterwards.</li><li><b>November:</b> run the <b>4-station circuit</b> every second day; record yourself once a week; fix every safety miss before moving on.</li><li><b>Final week:</b> circuits only + red-flag lists + phrase bank.</li></ul><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Assessment 3 &mdash; Multi-station OSCE brief (Canvas, Oct 2026); QLD protocols; PHAR6202 Week 9; app briefs</div>";
if (typeof LINKS !== 'undefined') for (var _li = 0; _li < LINKS.length; _li++) if (LINKS[_li].id === 'lifestyle') LINKS[_li].items.push({n:'NSW Get Healthy Service (free phone health coaching)',u:'https://www.nsw.gov.au/health-and-wellbeing/healthy-living/gethealthyservice',w:'Free NSW coaching for healthy eating, activity, weight and alcohol &mdash; a referral option to name in lifestyle plans.'});

/* ===== X: eTG Acute asthma (20 Oct 2026) — 18 cards, 7 MCQ, all quotes machine-checked (drafts/acute_asthma.py) ===== */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["asthma", "What is the National Asthma Council <b>4&times;4&times;4</b> first aid plan (salbutamol pMDI)?", "Salbutamol 100 microgram/actuation: <b>4 puffs</b>, one at a time, via spacer if available (<b>4 breaths</b> after each puff); <b>repeat after 4 minutes</b> if needed. If still no response, <b>call an ambulance</b> and keep giving 4 puffs (up to 8 in an adult) every 4 minutes.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "First aid for acute asthma when salbutamol is not available: what are the terbutaline and budesonide+formoterol options?", "<b>Terbutaline</b> (&ge;6 years) 500 microgram: 2 inhalations one at a time, then 1 more after 4 minutes if needed; if no response, call an ambulance and continue 1 every 4 minutes.<br><b>Budesonide+formoterol</b> (&ge;12 years): 2 inhalations one at a time, then 1 more after 4 minutes if needed; if no response, call an ambulance and continue 1 every 4 minutes, <b>up to a maximum total of 6 doses</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "After first aid, a patient responds but needs their reliever <b>more than every 3&ndash;4 hours</b>. What does eTG advise?", "A <b>same-day medical review</b>. If they did not respond rapidly and significantly to the first treatment, call an ambulance and say it is a <b>&lsquo;severe asthma attack&rsquo;</b>. If symptoms are escalating, they may need the emergency department.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Initial rapid severity assessment: what makes acute asthma <b>mild to moderate</b> (all must apply)?", "<b>All</b> of: can walk and speak whole sentences in one breath, and SpO<sub>2</sub> <b>&gt;94%</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Initial rapid severity assessment: features of <b>severe</b> acute asthma (any one)?", "<b>Any</b> of: unable to complete sentences in one breath &middot; increased work of breathing with accessory muscle use (tracheal tug, intercostal/subcostal recession, marked abdominal breathing) &middot; obvious respiratory distress &middot; SpO<sub>2</sub> <b>90&ndash;94%</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Initial rapid severity assessment: features of <b>life-threatening</b> acute asthma (any one)?", "<div class='box red'><b>Any</b> of: reduced consciousness &middot; collapse &middot; soft or absent breath sounds &middot; poor respiratory effort &middot; exhaustion &middot; cyanosis &middot; SpO<sub>2</sub> <b>&lt;90%</b>.</div><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Why is <b>wheeze</b> an unreliable guide to acute asthma severity? What are the two most important severity indicators?", "In severe asthma wheeze may be essentially absent (<b>&lsquo;silent chest&rsquo;</b>) and only appear as obstruction is relieved. Cyanosis is only visible with marked hypoxaemia, and its absence does not exclude life-threatening asthma. The most important indicators are <b>general appearance / mental state</b> and <b>work of breathing</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Name the eTG risk factors for <b>potentially fatal asthma</b> (Figure 9.7).", "Previous ventilation/ICU admission &middot; hospital admission in the last year &middot; repeated ED visits in the last year &middot; frequent SABA use (eg <b>&gt;1 canister/month</b>) &middot; needing &ge;3 classes of maintenance medicine &middot; poor lung function &middot; &lsquo;brittle asthma&rsquo; &middot; confirmed food allergy &middot; current/recent oral corticosteroid &middot; rural or remote location.<br>Often coexist with psychosocial factors: poor adherence or follow-up, depression, substance misuse incl. smoking, denial, social isolation, learning difficulties, financial/domestic problems.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Acute respiratory symptoms plus urticaria, flushing or angioedema. What must you think of, and what is first-line?", "<div class='box red'><b>Anaphylaxis.</b> Sudden-onset shortness of breath with typical skin features is diagnostic. Also consider it with cardiovascular (dizziness, hypotension) or GI (vomiting, diarrhoea) symptoms, even without skin signs. <b>IM adrenaline</b> is first-line; if anaphylaxis is suspected or can&rsquo;t be excluded, give it empirically.</div><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "eTG acute asthma: who needs an <b>oral corticosteroid</b>, how soon, and what is the adult regimen?", "All acute asthma in adults and children &ge;6 years <b>except the mildest</b> (mild symptoms that respond quickly and completely to bronchodilator). Start <b>within the first hour</b>.<br>Adult: <b>prednisolone 37.5&ndash;50 mg</b> orally once daily for a total of <b>5&ndash;10 days</b>, OR dexamethasone 16 mg, repeated once the next day.<div class='small muted'>Contrast: the COPD exacerbation regimen (pred 30&ndash;50 mg &times; 5 days) is a different condition. Check the asthma pack for what the Program lets you prescribe.</div><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Children 1&ndash;5 years with acute wheeze: when are oral corticosteroids recommended, and at what dose?", "For all <b>severe</b> acute wheezing; with only mild to moderate wheeze they may be of no benefit and can be withheld. Dose: <b>prednisolone 1 mg/kg (up to 50 mg)</b> once daily for a total of <b>3 days</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Mild to moderate acute asthma: initial salbutamol dose (pMDI + spacer), and what if there is no rapid response in primary care?", "Salbutamol 100 microgram, 1 puff at a time via spacer: adult/child &ge;6 years <b>4&ndash;12 puffs</b>, child 1&ndash;5 years 2&ndash;6 puffs, repeat as required. Monitor for <b>at least an hour</b> after symptoms resolve. If they worsen or lack a rapid, marked response, escalate to severe-asthma treatment and <b>arrange transfer to hospital</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Severe acute asthma (adult): bronchodilator regimen and oxygen target?", "<b>Salbutamol 12 puffs + ipratropium 8 puffs</b> (21 microgram) via pMDI + spacer, every <b>20 minutes</b> for the first hour. Oxygen if SpO<sub>2</sub> <b>&lt;92%</b>, titrated to <b>92&ndash;96%</b>. Oral corticosteroid within the first hour. In primary care, arrange hospital transfer.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Do antibiotics or oral beta2 agonists have a role in acute asthma?", "<b>No</b>, unless there is strong evidence of lung infection (eg radiographic pneumonia). Oral beta2 agonist therapy also has no role.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Repeated high-dose salbutamol: which adverse effects should you anticipate?", "<b>Hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia</b>; cardiovascular effects (myocardial ischaemia, QT prolongation and arrhythmia); and paradoxical worsening of respiratory failure (lactic/metabolic acidosis).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "After an acute episode resolves, what five things does eTG say to review before discharge (Figure 9.8)?", "1. Inhaler technique 2. Adherence 3. Triggers and avoidance 4. Preventer therapy and dose 5. Written asthma action plan, with education.<br>Don&rsquo;t discharge while SABA is still needed <b>more often than every 4 hours</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "An adult needed ED care for acute asthma and is not on an ICS. What does eTG say, and when is follow-up due?", "<b>Start a regular ICS</b> to reduce the risk of another episode; every adult or adolescent after acute severe asthma needs one. Follow up with the GP or specialist <b>within 2&ndash;3 days</b> of hospital discharge and <b>2 weeks</b> after the episode.<div class='small muted'>QLD protocol: review the management plan after ANY exacerbation, including when the patient self-initiates OCS.</div><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Who should be considered for <b>specialist referral</b> after acute asthma?", "Any first presentation of asthma; every episode needing hospital admission; ongoing poorly controlled asthma. Near-fatal or &lsquo;brittle&rsquo; asthma: specialist review <b>for life</b>. After a severe exacerbation: specialist review for <b>at least 1 year</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["asthma", "A woman at the pharmacy counter is wheezy, can only say a few words per breath and is using her neck muscles to breathe. SpO<sub>2</sub> is 92%. Using the eTG initial rapid assessment, how severe is this?", ["Mild to moderate", "Severe", "Life-threatening", "Cannot be graded without spirometry"], 1, "Unable to complete sentences in one breath, accessory muscle use and SpO<sub>2</sub> 90&ndash;94% each indicate <b>severe</b>. Life-threatening needs reduced consciousness, collapse, soft or absent breath sounds, poor effort, exhaustion, cyanosis or SpO<sub>2</sub> &lt;90%. Lung function testing is not needed to grade initial severity.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "A man with acute asthma is drowsy and his chest is quiet on auscultation, with little wheeze. What is the correct interpretation?", ["Improving, since the wheeze has settled", "Mild to moderate, since wheeze is absent", "Life-threatening: drowsiness and a silent chest", "Severe, but only if SpO<sub>2</sub> is under 90%"], 2, "Reduced consciousness and soft or absent breath sounds (&lsquo;silent chest&rsquo;) are <b>life-threatening</b> features. Wheeze is an unreliable indicator and may be absent in severe asthma. Any single feature is enough; you don&rsquo;t need the SpO<sub>2</sub>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "A parent asks what to do if their 15-year-old has an asthma attack at a sports carnival. Salbutamol and a spacer are available. Which is the National Asthma Council first aid plan?", ["2 puffs, repeat every 20 minutes, ambulance after 1 hour", "4 puffs one at a time with 4 breaths each, repeat after 4 minutes; ambulance if no response", "8 puffs at once, then call the GP for same-day review", "4 puffs, wait 20 minutes, then go to the GP if still wheezy"], 1, "4&times;4&times;4: four separate puffs of salbutamol, four breaths each from the spacer, repeat after four minutes. If still no response, <b>call an ambulance</b> and keep giving 4 puffs every 4 minutes.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "An adult with moderate acute asthma responds to salbutamol. Per eTG, which oral corticosteroid regimen is recommended?", ["Prednisolone 25 mg daily for 3 days", "Prednisolone 37.5&ndash;50 mg daily for a total of 5&ndash;10 days, started within 1 hour", "Prednisolone 1 mg/kg (up to 50 mg) daily for 3 days", "No corticosteroid needed once salbutamol works"], 1, "Adults: prednisolone <b>37.5&ndash;50 mg</b> within 1 hour, then daily for a <b>total of 5&ndash;10 days</b> (or dexamethasone 16 mg, repeated the next day). 1 mg/kg for 3 days is the paediatric regimen. Only the mildest cases, where response is quick and complete, may skip a steroid.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "Which is NOT an eTG risk factor for potentially fatal asthma?", ["Using more than one SABA canister a month", "Hospital admission for asthma in the last year", "Confirmed food allergy", "Exercise-induced symptoms that are well controlled with pre-exercise SABA"], 3, "Figure 9.7 lists previous ICU/ventilation, admission or repeated ED visits in the last year, frequent SABA use (&gt;1 canister a month), &ge;3 maintenance classes, poor lung function, brittle asthma, confirmed food allergy, recent OCS and rural or remote location. Well-controlled exercise-induced symptoms are not on it.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "A patient was seen in ED last week for acute asthma and uses only salbutamol. What is the key long-term change?", ["Add a long-acting beta2 agonist alone", "Start a regular inhaled corticosteroid", "Prescribe a 7-day antibiotic course", "No change if symptoms have resolved"], 1, "Any adult or adolescent needing ED care for acute asthma who is not already on an ICS should <b>start ICS therapy</b> to reduce the risk of another episode. Antibiotics have no role without strong evidence of infection.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"],
["asthma", "A patient with sudden breathlessness and wheeze after eating at a restaurant also has facial flushing and lip swelling. What is the first-line treatment?", ["Salbutamol 4&times;4&times;4 first, then reassess", "IM adrenaline", "Oral prednisolone 50 mg", "Oral antihistamine and observe"], 1, "Sudden-onset shortness of breath with typical skin features (flushing, angioedema) is <b>diagnostic of anaphylaxis</b>. IM adrenaline is first-line, and it should be given empirically if anaphylaxis can&rsquo;t be excluded.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: eTG Respiratory &mdash; Acute asthma (accessed 19 Oct 2026)</div>"]
);
if (typeof PACKS !== 'undefined' && PACKS.asthma) { PACKS.asthma.safety.push("<b>Acute attack at the counter</b> (eTG): start <b>4&times;4&times;4</b> salbutamol first aid; <b>call an ambulance</b> if there is no rapid, significant response or any severe/life-threatening feature (can&rsquo;t finish sentences, accessory muscles, drowsy, silent chest, cyanosis). Think <b>anaphylaxis</b> if skin, CV or GI signs: IM adrenaline first."); PACKS.asthma.refer.push("Responds to first aid but needs reliever <b>more than every 3&ndash;4 hours</b>: same-day medical review (eTG Acute asthma)"); }

/* ===== Section Y: PHAR6202 Week 10 (pathology & investigations) + Nov intensive Workshop II + respiratory checklist. Built 29 Oct 2026; every item quote-checked against coursework/_review/src/oct29 (drafts/week10_lab.py). ===== */
S2_CONDS.push({"id": "lab", "name": "Pathology & investigations", "tag": "6202 · Week 10", "body": "<p><b>Week 10 &mdash; Advanced Physical Assessment II.</b> Investigations answer a clinical question; they never replace history and examination. <b>Treat patients, not numbers.</b></p><h3>Expanded clinical reasoning framework</h3><p>Presenting complaint &rarr; focused history &rarr; initial differentials &rarr; focused physical assessment &rarr; pertinent positives/negatives &rarr; <b>investigations</b> &rarr; <b>refined differentials</b> &rarr; red flags &rarr; scope and management. Balance Occam&rsquo;s razor (one explanation) with Hickam&rsquo;s dictum (several conditions).</p><h3>Sodium (135&ndash;145 mmol/L; osmolality 275&ndash;295)</h3><p>Usually a <b>water</b> problem. Confirm &rarr; symptoms (confusion, seizures, reduced consciousness = urgent) &rarr; volume status &rarr; cause &rarr; medicines &rarr; investigations.</p><table><tr><th></th><th>Hyponatraemia</th><th>Hypernatraemia</th></tr><tr><td>Hypovolaemic</td><td>Vomiting, diarrhoea, diuretics</td><td>Dehydration, GI losses, sweating</td></tr><tr><td>Euvolaemic</td><td>SIADH, SSRIs, hypothyroidism</td><td>Diabetes insipidus</td></tr><tr><td>Hypervolaemic</td><td>HF, cirrhosis, CKD</td><td>Excess sodium</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>SSRIs, thiazides, SIADH-inducing</td><td>Lithium, SGLT2i, mannitol, excess Na</td></tr></table><h3>Potassium (Week 10: 3.5&ndash;5.0; QLD HTN protocol: 3.5&ndash;5.2) and magnesium</h3><p>Low K: loop/thiazide diuretics, vomiting, diarrhoea, steroids, insulin, beta-2 agonists. High K: ACEi, ARBs, spironolactone, CKD, NSAIDs, trimethoprim &mdash; often asymptomatic; <b>ECG changes matter more than the number</b>. Low Mg: PPIs, diuretics, malabsorption, diarrhoea, refeeding. If K won&rsquo;t correct, <b>check Mg</b>.</p><div class='box red'>K &gt;6.0 mmol/L is a red flag even if the patient feels well: prompt medical review, repeat test, cardiac risk, medicine review. Monitoring alone is not appropriate.</div><h3>Renal</h3><p>Creatinine/eGFR = <b>filtration</b>; ACR = <b>damage</b> (may rise first). eGFR &ge;90 normal; 60&ndash;89 mild; 30&ndash;59 moderate; 15&ndash;29 severe; &lt;15 failure. Trend beats a single value. Ask: is the medicine affecting the kidney, or the kidney affecting the medicine?</p><h3>Ketones</h3><p>On an SGLT2i, DKA can occur with near-normal glucose (<b>euDKA</b>). Use <b>blood</b> &beta;-hydroxybutyrate. &lt;0.6 normal; 0.6&ndash;1.5 mild; 1.6&ndash;3.0 significant ketosis possible; <b>&gt;3.0 urgent</b>.</p><div class='box red'>Unwell diabetic on an SGLT2i with high ketones &plusmn; acidosis &rarr; urgent medical assessment; not managed in the pharmacy.</div><h3>Calcium, phosphate, vitamin D</h3><p>Use corrected calcium (~40% albumin-bound). Patterns: &darr;Ca &darr;PO<sub>4</sub> vitamin D deficiency; &darr;Ca &uarr;PO<sub>4</sub> hypoparathyroidism; &uarr;Ca &darr;PO<sub>4</sub> primary hyperparathyroidism; &darr;Ca &darr;Mg magnesium-related hypocalcaemia. Hypocalcaemia: perioral tingling, cramps, tetany, seizures (e.g. after zoledronic acid). Hypercalcaemia: polyuria, constipation, dehydration, lethargy, confusion.</p><h3>Respiratory investigations</h3><table><tr><th>Question</th><th>Test</th></tr><tr><td>Airflow obstructed?</td><td>Spirometry (gold standard)</td></tr><tr><td>Needs formal spirometry?</td><td>PiKo-6 (screen only)</td></tr><tr><td>Control changing?</td><td>Peak flow</td></tr><tr><td>Eosinophilic inflammation?</td><td>FeNO (&lt;25 low, 25&ndash;50 intermediate, &gt;50 ppb high)</td></tr></table><p>Obstruction = reduced FEV<sub>1</sub> and FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC; reversibility &ge;12% <b>and</b> &ge;200 mL. Workshop II: ratio below the <b>LLN</b> defines obstruction (SPC01); COPD-X still uses post-BD &lt;0.70 &mdash; report both and flag the conflict. Screening devices triage only: a positive screen means arrange spirometry, not start a bronchodilator.</p>"});
BRIEFS.lab = {"mins": 7, "src": "PHAR6202 Week 10 + November intensive Workshop II", "pts": [["Investigations answer a question", "Why this test, what will it tell me, how will it change management? Read results with positives, negatives and the exam. <b>Treat patients, not numbers.</b>"], ["Sodium", "135&ndash;145 mmol/L. Usually a water problem. Urgency (confusion, seizures) &rarr; volume status &rarr; cause &rarr; medicines. Euvolaemic + SSRI = think SIADH; symptomatic needs medical review."], ["Potassium and magnesium", "Diuretics drop K and Mg; if K won&rsquo;t correct, check Mg. ACEi/ARB/spironolactone/CKD/NSAIDs/trimethoprim raise K. <div class='box red'>K &gt;6.0 = red flag even if well.</div>"], ["Renal", "eGFR = filtration; ACR = damage. Normal eGFR with raised ACR = early diabetic kidney disease. Trend over single values."], ["Ketones", "SGLT2i + unwell = test blood ketones. &gt;3.0 urgent. euDKA can have glucose ~10. Escalate; don&rsquo;t manage in pharmacy."], ["Calcium patterns", "&uarr;Ca &darr;PO<sub>4</sub> (+&uarr;PTH) = primary hyperparathyroidism; &darr;Ca &darr;Mg after zoledronic acid = symptomatic hypocalcaemia."], ["Respiratory tests", "Spirometry = obstruction; PiKo-6 = screen; peak flow = monitoring; FeNO = inflammation. Reversibility &ge;12% and &ge;200 mL. LLN vs 0.70: report both, flag conflict."], ["Respiratory exam", "Hands-wash, ID, explain, position &rarr; inspection &rarr; hands &rarr; vitals (SpO<sub>2</sub> &gt;95%) &rarr; face &rarr; chest &rarr; auscultate left vs right at each level."]]};
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["lab", "Week 10 expands the Week 9 framework to nine steps. List them. Where do investigations sit?", "1 Presenting complaint &rarr; 2 Focused history &rarr; 3 Initial differential diagnoses &rarr; 4 Focused physical assessment &rarr; 5 Pertinent positives and negatives &rarr; <b>6 Investigations</b> &rarr; <b>7 Refined differential diagnoses</b> &rarr; 8 Red flags &rarr; 9 Scope and management.<br>Investigations come <b>after</b> the history and examination, and are read alongside the pertinent positives and negatives.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Clinical Reasoning Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Before ordering or interpreting any test, which three questions does Week 10 tell you to ask?", "&bull; Why am I ordering this test?<br>&bull; What information am I trying to obtain?<br>&bull; How will the result influence management?<br>Investigations <b>complement</b> history and physical assessment; they do not replace them.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Clinical Reasoning Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Match the clinical question to the investigation (Week 10 table): diabetes? kidney disease? airflow obstruction? airway inflammation? electrolyte disturbance?", "Diabetes &mdash; HbA1c<br>Kidney disease &mdash; eGFR, ACR<br>Airflow obstruction &mdash; spirometry<br>Airway inflammation &mdash; FeNO<br>Electrolyte disturbance &mdash; UEC<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Clinical Reasoning Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Occam&rsquo;s razor vs Hickam&rsquo;s dictum: what does each say, and what Week 10 example illustrates each?", "<b>Occam&rsquo;s razor:</b> the simplest explanation that accounts for all findings may be the most likely (fever + productive cough + crackles &rarr; pneumonia). Limitation: patients do not always have one condition.<br><b>Hickam&rsquo;s dictum:</b> a patient can have as many diseases as they please (68-year-old with breathlessness, wheeze and peripheral oedema &rarr; COPD, heart failure, or both).<br>Safe prescribing balances both.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Clinical Reasoning Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Week 10 worked example: 56-year-old man, months of fatigue, thirst and urinating at night; on perindopril; father had T2D; BMI 32. Point-of-care RBG 16.2 mmol/L, HbA1c 8.9%. What is the most likely diagnosis and what does the pharmacist do?", "Most likely <b>previously undiagnosed type 2 diabetes</b>, explaining polyuria, polydipsia, fatigue and blurred vision. No red flags.<br>Further medical evaluation is still needed: communicate findings to the GP, refer for confirmatory laboratory testing if required, assess for diabetes complications and CV risk, plan ongoing management.<br>Key point: investigations support clinical reasoning; they do not replace it.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Clinical Reasoning Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "In a patient with likely new diabetes, which features does Week 10 list as needing urgent escalation?", "<div class='box red'>Altered conscious state, vomiting, significant dehydration, ketosis, abdominal pain, rapid weight loss, very high blood glucose.</div><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Clinical Reasoning Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "&ldquo;Treat patients, not numbers.&rdquo; Which five questions should you ask of every abnormal result?", "&bull; Is this result clinically significant for this patient?<br>&bull; What clinical question does it answer?<br>&bull; How does it fit with the history and assessment?<br>&bull; Are there red flags or signs of deterioration?<br>&bull; Does the patient need treatment, monitoring, further investigation or referral?<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Sodium Interpretation Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Sodium: what reference ranges does Week 10 use, and what do sodium abnormalities usually reflect?", "Serum sodium <b>135&ndash;145 mmol/L</b>; serum osmolality <b>275&ndash;295 mOsm/kg</b>.<br>Sodium abnormalities usually reflect disturbances in <b>water balance</b>, not sodium balance.<br>Memory aid: low sodium = water problem (&ldquo;why is there relatively too much water?&rdquo;); high sodium = water deficit (&ldquo;why is there not enough water?&rdquo;).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Sodium Interpretation Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Give the six steps of the Week 10 quick framework for an abnormal serum sodium.", "1 <b>Confirm</b> it (trend, previous results, glucose).<br>2 <b>Assess urgency</b>: confusion, seizures, reduced consciousness or severe symptoms &rarr; urgent medical review.<br>3 <b>Volume status</b>: hypo-, eu- or hypervolaemic.<br>4 <b>Likely mechanism</b>.<br>5 <b>Contributing medicines</b>.<br>6 <b>Plan investigation</b>: UEC/eGFR, glucose, serum osmolality, urine sodium/osmolality, thyroid function, cortisol if indicated.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Sodium Interpretation Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Hyponatraemia by volume status: common causes of hypo-, eu- and hypervolaemic hyponatraemia, and how each looks on examination.", "<b>Hypovolaemic</b> (dry, tachycardic, postural drop): vomiting, diarrhoea, diuretics.<br><b>Euvolaemic</b> (no obvious fluid excess or deficit): SIADH, SSRIs, hypothyroidism.<br><b>Hypervolaemic</b> (oedema, ascites, heart failure signs): heart failure, cirrhosis, CKD.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Sodium Interpretation Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Hypernatraemia by volume status, and which medicines can contribute to high vs low sodium?", "<b>Hypernatraemia:</b> hypovolaemic &mdash; dehydration, GI losses, sweating; euvolaemic &mdash; diabetes insipidus; hypervolaemic &mdash; excess sodium administration.<br><b>Medicines &rarr; high Na:</b> lithium, SGLT2 inhibitors, mannitol, excess sodium; occasionally loop diuretics through dehydration.<br><b>Medicines &rarr; low Na:</b> SSRIs, thiazide diuretics, SIADH-inducing medicines.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Sodium Interpretation Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Mrs Carter, 78: two weeks of increasing confusion, near-falls; sertraline started 3 months ago. Euvolaemic, afebrile. Na 122 mmol/L, osmolality low, K and creatinine normal. Diagnosis and management?", "Most likely <b>medication-induced SIADH causing euvolaemic hyponatraemia</b> (low Na, low osmolality, euvolaemic exam, sertraline). Low osmolality confirms a true hyponatraemia, not an artefact.<br>Symptomatic (confusion, falls) &rarr; not incidental. Needs <b>medical review</b>, investigation of the cause, medication review, SIADH assessment, falls risk addressed.<br>Scope: the pharmacist identifies the problem and likely medicine cause and refers; <b>monitoring alone is not appropriate</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Applying Sodium Results to Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Potassium reference range: what does Week 10 give, and how does it compare with the QLD Hypertension protocol?", "Week 10: <b>3.5&ndash;5.0 mmol/L</b> (&ldquo;most laboratories&rdquo;).<br>QLD HTN protocol Table 4 (RCPA): <b>3.5&ndash;5.2 mmol/L</b>.<br>They differ at the top end. In a Program consult quote the protocol figure; otherwise use the reporting laboratory&rsquo;s range.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Potassium and Magnesium Foundations; QLD Hypertension protocol V2, Table 4 (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Hypokalaemia: common causes, symptoms, and who is at highest arrhythmia risk?", "<b>Causes:</b> loop and thiazide diuretics, vomiting, diarrhoea, corticosteroids, insulin, beta-2 agonists.<br><b>Symptoms:</b> muscle weakness, cramps, fatigue, constipation.<br>Arrhythmia risk rises as K falls, especially with heart disease, <b>digoxin</b> or QT-prolonging medicines.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Potassium and Magnesium Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Hyperkalaemia: common causes, symptoms, and the Week 10 clinical pearl.", "<b>Causes:</b> ACE inhibitors, ARBs, spironolactone, CKD, NSAIDs, trimethoprim.<br><b>Symptoms:</b> many are asymptomatic; severe &mdash; weakness, paralysis, arrhythmia.<br><b>Pearl:</b> ECG changes may be more important than the potassium concentration.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Potassium and Magnesium Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Hypomagnesaemia: causes, features, and the prescribing pearl.", "<b>Causes:</b> proton pump inhibitors, loop and thiazide diuretics, malabsorption, diarrhoea, refeeding syndrome.<br><b>Features:</b> cramps, tremor, weakness, arrhythmias.<br><b>Pearl:</b> if potassium fails to respond to replacement, <b>check magnesium</b>; low magnesium makes hypokalaemia hard to correct.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Potassium and Magnesium Foundations; PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Potassium and Magnesium in Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Mrs Wilson, 68, on furosemide and perindopril: weeks of fatigue, leg cramps, weakness; no GI losses or cardiac symptoms. K 3.0, Mg 0.50 mmol/L. Most likely explanation and management?", "<b>Diuretic-induced hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia.</b> No red flags; stable but needs management.<br>Manage: review contributing medicines, correct the electrolytes, monitor response, assess ongoing potassium losses. If K does not improve as expected, check Mg.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Potassium and Magnesium in Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Mr Johnston, 76, CKD 3b and heart failure, on perindopril and spironolactone, feels well. Routine bloods: K 6.1 mmol/L, eGFR 36. What is the red flag and what is the right response?", "<div class='box red'>Red flag: <b>potassium &gt;6.0 mmol/L</b>.</div>Likely hyperkalaemia from CKD plus potassium-retaining medicines (ACEi, spironolactone). A patient with hyperkalaemia may appear completely well.<br>Needs <b>prompt action</b>: medical review, medication review, repeat testing, cardiac risk assessment, escalation per local protocols. <b>Monitoring alone would not be appropriate.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Potassium and Magnesium in Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Kidney function vs kidney damage: which tests measure which? Give the eGFR bands Week 10 uses.", "<b>Creatinine and eGFR</b> = filtration. <b>ACR</b> = kidney damage (albumin leaking into urine) and may rise before eGFR falls.<br>eGFR: &ge;90 normal or near-normal; 60&ndash;89 mild reduction; 30&ndash;59 moderate; 15&ndash;29 severe; &lt;15 kidney failure.<br>Pearl: a patient can have normal kidney function and still have kidney disease. <b>Trend</b> often matters more than one result.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Renal Function Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Reviewing renal results as a prescriber: which three questions, and the Week 10 tip?", "1 Could reduced kidney function affect <b>medicine choice</b>? (NSAIDs, metformin, some anticoagulants, certain antibiotics)<br>2 Could it affect the <b>dose</b>?<br>3 Could a <b>medicine be contributing</b>? (NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, diuretics, nephrotoxic medicines)<br>Tip: &ldquo;Could the medicine be affecting the kidney, or could the kidney be affecting the medicine?&rdquo; Also creatinine is influenced by age, sex and muscle mass.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Renal Function Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Michael, 48, T2D and hypertension on metformin and perindopril, feels well. BP 146/90, HbA1c 9.2%, eGFR 95, ACR 18 mg/mmol. Interpretation and management?", "<b>Early diabetic kidney disease</b>: ACR elevated (damage) despite normal eGFR (filtration). A normal eGFR read alone gives false reassurance. No immediate red flags, but damage is ongoing.<br>Manage: optimise diabetes, manage BP, repeat renal monitoring, CV risk assessment, communicate to the GP.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Renal Function in Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "What is euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis (euDKA), why is it missed, and when should you test ketones?", "Ketoacidosis with <b>normal or only mildly raised glucose</b>, seen in patients on <b>SGLT2 inhibitors</b> (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin). Missed because glucose looks reassuring.<br>Test ketones in a person with diabetes who has reduced oral intake, unexplained fatigue or dehydration &mdash; especially on an SGLT2i. Ask: &ldquo;Could this be more than gastroenteritis?&rdquo;<br>Interpret glucose and ketones <b>together</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Ketone Testing and Diabetic Emergencies Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Blood vs urine ketone testing: what does each measure, and which is preferred for suspected DKA/euDKA?", "<b>Blood</b> measures &beta;-hydroxybutyrate (the predominant ketone in ketoacidosis): detects ketosis earlier, more accurate, better for DKA/euDKA, useful to monitor treatment. <b>Preferred.</b><br><b>Urine</b> measures acetoacetate: less sensitive early, may not reflect current levels, can stay positive after recovery.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Ketone Testing and Diabetic Emergencies Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Blood ketone bands (Week 10): what does each range mean?", "&lt;0.6 mmol/L &mdash; normal<br>0.6&ndash;1.5 &mdash; mild elevation; review clinical context<br>1.6&ndash;3.0 &mdash; significant ketosis possible<br><b>&gt;3.0 &mdash; urgent assessment required</b><br>Never interpret ketones alone: consider symptoms, medicines, glucose, hydration and clinical state.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Ketone Testing and Diabetic Emergencies Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Sophia, 56, on metformin and empagliflozin: viral illness last week, now 2 days of nausea, vomiting, eating and drinking less; BP 106/68, HR 98, mildly dry. Glucose 10.2, ketones 3.5 mmol/L, bicarbonate 15, pH 7.28. Diagnosis and action?", "<b>Euglycaemic DKA</b> &mdash; significant ketosis and metabolic acidosis with only mild hyperglycaemia. Glucose is <b>not</b> the key finding; ketosis and acidosis make it dangerous.<br><div class='box red'>Immediate medical assessment / urgent referral; communicate concern about possible euDKA; document; reinforce sick-day rules.</div>Scope: the pharmacist recognises it, tests ketones, identifies the likely diagnosis and escalates. euDKA is <b>not managed in the pharmacy</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Applying Ketone Results to Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Calcium, phosphate and vitamin D: how do vitamin D, PTH, CKD and magnesium deficiency move calcium and phosphate? Why use corrected calcium?", "Vitamin D: Ca &uarr;, PO<sub>4</sub> &uarr;<br>PTH: Ca &uarr;, PO<sub>4</sub> &darr;<br>CKD: Ca &darr; or normal, PO<sub>4</sub> &uarr;<br>Mg deficiency: Ca &darr;, PO<sub>4</sub> variable<br>About 40% of circulating calcium is bound to albumin, so low albumin makes total calcium look falsely low &mdash; use <b>corrected calcium</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Calcium, Phosphate and Vitamin D Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Name the four calcium patterns Week 10 teaches.", "&darr; Ca + &darr; PO<sub>4</sub> &mdash; vitamin D deficiency<br>&darr; Ca + &uarr; PO<sub>4</sub> &mdash; hypoparathyroidism<br>&uarr; Ca + &darr; PO<sub>4</sub> &mdash; <b>primary hyperparathyroidism</b><br>&darr; Ca + &darr; Mg &mdash; magnesium-related hypocalcaemia<br>Look for patterns, not isolated numbers.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Calcium, Phosphate and Vitamin D Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Hypocalcaemia vs hypercalcaemia: common causes and symptoms of each.", "<b>Hypocalcaemia</b> &mdash; causes: vitamin D deficiency, Mg deficiency, CKD, hypoparathyroidism, bisphosphonate therapy. Symptoms: perioral tingling, paraesthesia, cramps, tetany, seizures.<br><b>Hypercalcaemia</b> &mdash; causes: primary hyperparathyroidism, malignancy, excess vitamin D, thiazide diuretics. Symptoms: polyuria, constipation, dehydration, lethargy, confusion.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Calcium, Phosphate and Vitamin D Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Mrs Adeyemi, 74: perioral tingling, cramps, hand spasms 2 days after a zoledronic acid infusion; positive Chvostek sign. Corrected Ca 1.90 (2.20&ndash;2.60), Mg 0.55 (0.70&ndash;1.00) mmol/L. Diagnosis and management?", "<b>Symptomatic hypocalcaemia</b>, contributors: recent zoledronic acid, possible vitamin D deficiency, concurrent hypomagnesaemia. No tetany, seizures or arrhythmia, but not an incidental finding.<br>Manage: medical review, check vitamin D, correct magnesium, monitor calcium.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Calcium, Phosphate and Vitamin D in Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Mr Foster, 66: months of constipation, lethargy, thirst, passing more urine; mildly dry. Corrected Ca 2.82 mmol/L, phosphate low, PTH elevated. Diagnosis?", "<b>Primary hyperparathyroidism</b> (&uarr; Ca + &darr; PO<sub>4</sub> + &uarr; PTH, the classic pattern). No immediate red flags, but further investigation of the cause, monitoring and assessment for complications are needed.<br>Red flags to check: severe dehydration, significant confusion, markedly raised calcium.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Calcium, Phosphate and Vitamin D in Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Which respiratory test answers which question: airflow obstruction? need for formal spirometry? asthma control over time? eosinophilic airway inflammation?", "Airflow obstructed &mdash; <b>spirometry</b> (gold standard)<br>Needs formal spirometry? &mdash; <b>PiKo-6</b> (screening only; cannot diagnose asthma or COPD)<br>Control changing over time &mdash; <b>peak flow</b> (monitoring, not diagnosis)<br>Eosinophilic inflammation &mdash; <b>FeNO</b> (measures inflammation, not airflow)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Respiratory Investigations Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Spirometry: what do FEV<sub>1</sub>, FVC and FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC tell you, what is an obstructive pattern, and what is significant bronchodilator reversibility?", "FEV<sub>1</sub> &mdash; how quickly air gets out; FVC &mdash; how much altogether; ratio &mdash; is airflow obstructed?<br><b>Obstructive:</b> reduced FEV<sub>1</sub> and reduced FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC (asthma, COPD).<br><b>Significant reversibility:</b> FEV<sub>1</sub> rise of <b>&ge;12% and &ge;200 mL</b>, commonly seen in asthma &mdash; but a bronchodilator response does not automatically confirm asthma.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Respiratory Investigations Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "FeNO: what does it measure, how is an adult result interpreted, and what can&rsquo;t it do?", "Measures nitric oxide in exhaled breath = <b>airway inflammation</b>, not airflow.<br>Adults: &lt;25 ppb low likelihood of eosinophilic inflammation; 25&ndash;50 intermediate; <b>&gt;50 high likelihood</b>.<br>High FeNO suggests eosinophilic (type 2) inflammation and likely ICS responsiveness, but <b>does not diagnose asthma on its own</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Respiratory Investigations Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Maria, 52, non-smoker, allergic rhinitis, childhood eczema: variable wheeze, worse in spring and with dust. FEV<sub>1</sub> 2.0 &rarr; 2.45 L post-BD; ratio 0.61 &rarr; 0.71; FeNO 68 ppb; eosinophils 0.8 &times;10<sup>9</sup>/L. Diagnosis and management?", "<b>Asthma</b>: reversibility +450 mL / +22.5%, high FeNO, raised eosinophils, atopy, variable symptoms. No single result confirms it; the diagnosis comes from integrating them. No red flags.<br>Manage: review current therapy, inhaler technique, adherence, anti-inflammatory needs, monitoring and follow-up.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Applying Respiratory Investigations to Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Workshop II: what alone defines an obstructive pattern on spirometry, and why can&rsquo;t one fixed ratio cut-off fit everyone?", "<b>FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC below the lower limit of normal (LLN)</b> = obstructive pattern; nothing else in spirometry defines obstruction.<br>The ratio is age-dependent (about 0.85 at 25, about 0.70 at 80). A normal ratio is not normal lungs, in restriction it is often normal or high, and <b>severity comes from the FEV<sub>1</sub>, not the ratio</b>. LLN = 5th percentile = z &minus;1.645.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 November intensive &mdash; Workshop II: Lung Function &amp; Spirometry slides (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Workshop II: the two Australian standards disagree on defining obstruction. Which says what, and what should you do when they conflict?", "<b>SPC01 and ERS/ATS 2022:</b> interpret against the LLN and z-score; a fixed 0.70 over-diagnoses after about 50 and misses obstruction before about 45.<br><b>COPD-X:</b> confirm COPD with post-BD FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC &lt;0.70.<br>Report the ratio, LLN and z-score, and <b>flag the conflict</b> (e.g. 72-year-old, ratio 0.66, above her LLN: COPD by COPD-X, normal by SPC01 &mdash; say so, don&rsquo;t pick).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 November intensive &mdash; Workshop II: Lung Function &amp; Spirometry slides (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Workshop II: what can peak flow, PiKo-6 and COPD-6 do, and what should a positive screen lead to?", "They are <b>triage only</b>: one or two numbers, no curve, effort-dependent, no quality grading or reference-based interpretation. They cannot diagnose or grade.<br>A positive screen is a reason to <b>arrange spirometry &mdash; not to start a bronchodilator</b>. In a pharmacy, if unsure whether testing is safe: don&rsquo;t test, refer and document why.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 November intensive &mdash; Workshop II: Lung Function &amp; Spirometry slides (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Respiratory examination (intensive checklist): list the seven sections in order.", "1 <b>Introduction + positioning</b>: wash hands, confirm name and age, introduce and explain, sit upright or at 45&deg;.<br>2 <b>General inspection</b>: effort, accessory muscles, cyanosis, cough, posture, speaking in sentences, adjuncts (sputum cup, O<sub>2</sub>).<br>3 <b>Hands</b>: tar staining, clubbing, wasting.<br>4 <b>Vital signs</b>: RR, pulse rate and rhythm, SpO<sub>2</sub> (normal &gt;95%), peripheral temperature.<br>5 <b>Face</b>: conjunctival pallor, nasal patency, central cyanosis.<br>6 <b>Chest inspection</b>: trachea midline, scars, deformities, symmetry.<br>7 <b>Auscultation</b>: anterior/lateral and posterior, left vs right at each level; quality, volume, added sounds.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 intensive &mdash; Respiratory Assessment checklist (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["lab", "A patient has sodium 128 mmol/L, no oedema and no dehydration. What additional information would you want next?", ["Serum osmolality", "Height", "Pulse rhythm", "Cholesterol"], 0, "Serum osmolality shows whether this is a true (hypotonic) hyponatraemia. With a euvolaemic exam, think SIADH, SSRIs, hypothyroidism.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Sodium Interpretation Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "A patient&rsquo;s potassium is not improving despite replacement. What should you check?", ["Calcium", "Magnesium", "Phosphate", "Vitamin D"], 1, "Low magnesium can make hypokalaemia difficult to correct &mdash; if K fails to respond, check magnesium.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Potassium and Magnesium in Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "eGFR 98 mL/min/1.73m&sup2; and ACR 15 mg/mmol. Which statement is most accurate?", ["Kidney function and kidney health are both normal", "Kidney disease is excluded", "Filtration appears normal, but kidney damage may be present", "Consistent with end-stage kidney disease"], 2, "An elevated ACR may indicate kidney damage despite preserved filtration. Kidney function and kidney damage are not the same thing.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Renal Function Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "A 74-year-old with CKD 3b on perindopril and spironolactone feels well. K 6.1 mmol/L, eGFR 34. What should be prioritised?", ["Reassure and repeat bloods in three months", "Continue therapy; dietary potassium advice only", "Prompt medical assessment, repeat potassium, review medicines contributing to hyperkalaemia", "Focus on improving eGFR first"], 2, "The K is significantly raised with CKD, an ACE inhibitor and spironolactone. Confirm severity, assess cardiac risk, review medicines, arrange prompt medical assessment. Absence of symptoms does not exclude serious risk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Renal Function Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "A 58-year-old on empagliflozin is unwell with blood glucose 9.8 mmol/L. Which test is most useful next?", ["HbA1c", "Peak flow", "Blood ketones", "ACR"], 2, "SGLT2 inhibitor users can develop euglycaemic DKA with only mildly raised glucose. Blood ketones (&beta;-hydroxybutyrate) are preferred.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Ketone Testing and Diabetic Emergencies Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Patient A: glucose 18, ketones 0.2, feels well. Patient B: glucose 10, ketones 3.5, nausea and vomiting, on empagliflozin. Who is more concerning?", ["Patient A &mdash; higher glucose", "Patient B", "Both equally", "Neither &mdash; neither glucose is above 20"], 1, "Patient B may be at greater immediate risk: ketones &gt;3.0 mmol/L need urgent assessment, and SGLT2i use is a risk factor for euDKA. Glucose and ketones must be read together.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Ketone Testing and Diabetic Emergencies Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Corrected calcium high, phosphate low. Which diagnosis is most strongly suggested?", ["Vitamin D deficiency", "Hypoparathyroidism", "Primary hyperparathyroidism", "Magnesium deficiency"], 2, "High calcium with low phosphate is the classic pattern of primary hyperparathyroidism.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Calcium, Phosphate and Vitamin D Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Which investigation answers &lsquo;Is eosinophilic airway inflammation present?&rsquo;", ["Spirometry", "Peak flow", "PiKo-6", "FeNO"], 3, "FeNO measures airway inflammation, not airflow. Spirometry = obstruction; peak flow = control over time; PiKo-6 = need for formal spirometry.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Respiratory Investigations Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "After a bronchodilator, which FEV<sub>1</sub> change meets the Week 10 definition of significant reversibility?", ["+8% and +150 mL", "+12% and +150 mL", "+12% and +200 mL", "+10% only"], 2, "Both conditions are needed: &ge;12% <b>and</b> &ge;200 mL. Even then, it does not automatically confirm asthma.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Respiratory Investigations Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "A 76-year-old with no symptoms has FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC 0.68. Is this obstruction?", ["Yes &mdash; below 0.70", "Not necessarily &mdash; check it against the LLN for that age", "No &mdash; ratios are irrelevant", "Yes &mdash; start a bronchodilator"], 1, "The ratio falls with age; check it against the LLN. Report ratio, LLN and z-score, and flag where COPD-X (fixed 0.70) and SPC01 (LLN) disagree.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 November intensive &mdash; Workshop II: Lung Function &amp; Spirometry slides (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Sophia (empagliflozin): glucose 10.2, ketones 3.5, bicarbonate 15, pH 7.28, vomiting. What is the right action?", ["Withhold empagliflozin and review in 24 hours", "Advise fluids and sick-day rules at home", "Urgent referral for immediate medical assessment of possible euDKA", "Start a sulfonylurea"], 2, "Significant ketosis plus metabolic acidosis = euDKA, which needs urgent medical management and is not managed in the pharmacy. Communicate the concern and document.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Applying Ketone Results to Clinical Reasoning (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"],
["lab", "Which medicine is listed in Week 10 as a cause of HYPERnatraemia rather than hyponatraemia?", ["Sertraline", "Hydrochlorothiazide", "Lithium", "Carbamazepine"], 2, "Week 10 lists lithium, SGLT2 inhibitors, mannitol and excess sodium for hypernatraemia; SSRIs, thiazides and SIADH-inducing medicines for hyponatraemia.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: PHAR6202 Week 10 &mdash; Sodium Interpretation Foundations (Oct 29 2026 export)</div>"]
);
S2_OSCE.push(
{"id": "vy1", "title": "Worsening wheeze: focused respiratory examination", "cond": "lab", "time": "8 min", "type": "exam", "brief": "Maria, 52, attends the GP clinic where you work as a pharmacist prescriber. She reports increasing breathlessness, wheeze, intermittent chest tightness and cough, worse in spring. Perform a focused respiratory examination, explaining what you are doing, then summarise your findings to the examiner.", "patient": "<b>Findings, given on request:</b><ul><li>Sitting comfortably, speaking in full sentences, no accessory muscle use, no cyanosis.</li><li>No tar staining or clubbing.</li><li>RR 18/min, HR 84 bpm regular, SpO<sub>2</sub> 96%.</li><li>Trachea midline, no scars or deformity, symmetrical expansion.</li><li>Auscultation: bilateral expiratory wheeze, no crackles.</li></ul><b>History if asked:</b> symptoms vary day to day, worse with dust and in spring; allergic rhinitis; childhood eczema; non-smoker.", "marks": ["Washes hands, confirms name and age, introduces self and explains the examination, gains consent", "Positions her sitting upright or at 45 degrees", "General inspection: respiratory effort, accessory muscles, cyanosis, cough, ability to speak in sentences, adjuncts", "Hands: tar staining, clubbing, muscle wasting", "Vital signs: respiratory rate, pulse rate and rhythm, SpO2 (states normal >95%), temperature", "Face: conjunctival pallor, nasal airway patency, central cyanosis", "Chest inspection: trachea midline, scars, deformities, symmetry of movement", "Auscultates anterior/lateral and posterior chest, comparing left and right at each level", "Describes added sounds correctly: bilateral expiratory wheeze, no crackles", "SAFETY: Checks for and states the absence of red flags (SpO2 <92%, RR >30, silent chest, cannot speak in full sentences, cyanosis)", "Summarises pertinent positives (wheeze, variable symptoms, atopy, triggers) and negatives (non-smoker, no chronic productive cough)", "Names the next investigation to answer the question: spirometry with bronchodilator reversibility (± FeNO)"], "model": "<ul><li>&ldquo;Hi, I&rsquo;m Uzair, one of the pharmacists. Can I confirm your name and age? I&rsquo;d like to listen to your chest and check your breathing &mdash; is that OK?&rdquo; Wash hands; sit her upright.</li><li><b>Narrate:</b> &ldquo;You&rsquo;re speaking in full sentences, no extra muscles working, no blue tinge.&rdquo; Check hands, then RR, pulse rate and rhythm, SpO<sub>2</sub>.</li><li>Face, trachea, chest shape and symmetry; auscultate front, sides and back, left vs right each level.</li><li><b>Summary to examiner:</b> &ldquo;Stable: RR 18, HR 84 regular, SpO<sub>2</sub> 96%. Bilateral expiratory wheeze, no crackles. No red flags. With variable symptoms, atopy and no smoking history, asthma is most likely; COPD is less likely. I&rsquo;d confirm with spirometry and bronchodilator reversibility, and FeNO would show eosinophilic inflammation.&rdquo;</li></ul>", "src": "PHAR6202 intensive Respiratory Assessment checklist; Week 10: Applying Respiratory Investigations to Clinical Reasoning; Week 9: Respiratory Assessment (red flags)"},
{"id": "vy2", "title": "Unwell on empagliflozin: glucose 10, ketones 3.5", "cond": "lab", "time": "8 min", "type": "interpret", "brief": "Sophia, 56, has type 2 diabetes. She comes to your pharmacist prescribing clinic saying she has felt sick and has vomited for two days. Take a focused history, interpret the results you are given, and decide what happens next.", "patient": "<b>Reveals if asked:</b><ul><li>Viral illness last week; eating less and drinking less than usual.</li><li>Medicines: metformin 1 g twice daily, empagliflozin 10 mg daily &mdash; still taking both.</li><li>No chest pain, no severe abdominal pain.</li></ul><b>Observations (on request):</b> BP 106/68, HR 98, T 37.1&deg;C, RR 20; tired, mildly dry mucous membranes, alert and orientated.<br><b>Results (only if requested):</b> blood glucose 10.2 mmol/L; blood ketones 3.5 mmol/L; bicarbonate 15 mmol/L; pH 7.28.<br><b>If told to go home:</b> &ldquo;OK, I&rsquo;ll just rest and drink some water.&rdquo;", "marks": ["Focused history: onset, intake, fluids, recent illness", "Full medicine history, identifying empagliflozin (SGLT2 inhibitor)", "Asks about red flags: abdominal pain, chest pain, altered consciousness", "Checks observations and hydration", "Requests BLOOD ketones (not only glucose) because of SGLT2i use", "Interprets: glucose only mildly raised; ketones >3.0 = urgent; low bicarbonate and pH = metabolic acidosis", "SAFETY: Names euglycaemic DKA as the likely diagnosis and does not dismiss it as gastroenteritis", "SAFETY: Arranges immediate medical assessment / urgent referral — does not manage in the pharmacy or send home", "Communicates the concern (possible euDKA) clearly to the receiving clinician", "Explains to Sophia why she needs urgent care, in plain language", "Documents findings and actions; reinforces sick-day management principles"], "model": "<ul><li><b>History:</b> &ldquo;When did it start? Are you keeping fluids down? Any tummy pain or chest pain? Which diabetes medicines are you taking, and have you kept taking them?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Reason aloud:</b> &ldquo;She&rsquo;s on empagliflozin, unwell, eating and drinking less &mdash; I need to rule out euglycaemic DKA, so I&rsquo;ll check blood ketones, not just glucose.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Interpret:</b> &ldquo;Glucose 10.2 is only mildly high, but ketones 3.5 with bicarbonate 15 and pH 7.28 show ketosis and acidosis. This is likely euDKA.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Act:</b> &ldquo;Sophia, your blood is showing ketones at a level that needs urgent hospital treatment today. I&rsquo;m arranging that now.&rdquo; Urgent referral / immediate medical assessment; hand over &lsquo;possible euDKA on empagliflozin&rsquo;.</li><li><b>Close:</b> document; reinforce sick-day rules for later.</li></ul>", "src": "PHAR6202 Week 10: Ketone Testing and Diabetic Emergencies Foundations; Applying Ketone Results to Clinical Reasoning"},
{"id": "vy3", "title": "Routine bloods: potassium 6.1 in a patient who feels well", "cond": "lab", "time": "7 min", "type": "interpret", "brief": "You are a pharmacist prescriber in a GP clinic. The GP asks you to do a preliminary review of Mr Johnston, 76, whose routine pathology shows a high serum potassium. He is with you now. Assess him, interpret the results, and explain your plan to the examiner.", "patient": "<b>Reveals if asked:</b><ul><li>Feels well.</li><li>History: CKD stage 3b, heart failure.</li><li>Medicines: perindopril, spironolactone.</li><li>Denies chest pain, palpitations, weakness, dizziness.</li></ul><b>Observations:</b> BP 132/76, HR 74, RR 16; appears well.<br><b>Results:</b> potassium 6.1 mmol/L; eGFR 36 mL/min/1.73m&sup2;.<br><b>If reassured:</b> &ldquo;Good, so I can just carry on as normal?&rdquo;", "marks": ["Asks about symptoms of hyperkalaemia: palpitations, weakness, syncope, chest pain", "Takes a medicine history including potassium-retaining medicines (perindopril, spironolactone) and asks about NSAIDs or potassium supplements", "Identifies contributors: reduced renal excretion (CKD 3b) plus ACEi and spironolactone", "Interprets potassium >6.0 mmol/L as a red flag even though he is asymptomatic", "SAFETY: Does NOT reassure or plan monitoring alone — states this needs prompt action", "SAFETY: Arranges prompt medical review / escalation per local protocol, with repeat potassium and cardiac (ECG) assessment", "Flags the prescribing decisions to reconsider: spironolactone, any NSAID use, potassium supplements, monitoring interval", "Explains to the patient in plain language why this matters despite feeling well", "Documents and communicates findings to the GP"], "model": "<ul><li><b>Assess:</b> &ldquo;Any palpitations, weakness or fainting? Which medicines do you take &mdash; any anti-inflammatories or potassium tablets?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Reason aloud:</b> &ldquo;Potassium 6.1 is above 6.0, a red flag. With CKD 3b, perindopril and spironolactone, he can&rsquo;t clear potassium well. Feeling well doesn&rsquo;t make this low risk &mdash; ECG changes matter more than symptoms.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Act:</b> &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll ask the GP to review you today: we need to repeat the test, check your heart tracing and review the spironolactone and perindopril.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Patient:</b> &ldquo;Even though you feel fine, a high potassium can affect your heart rhythm, so we need to act today rather than wait.&rdquo;</li><li><b>Close:</b> document; communicate to the GP; set the monitoring plan once managed.</li></ul>", "src": "PHAR6202 Week 10: Potassium and Magnesium in Clinical Reasoning (Case 2); Renal Function Foundations; Renal Function in Clinical Reasoning (integrated challenge)"}
);

/* ===== Section Z: EPA chronic conditions pilot scope (Appendix 1 + Explanatory Guide). 30 Oct 2026; quote-checked by drafts/epa_scope.py ===== */
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["safe", "What legally authorises a QLD pharmacist to prescribe in the chronic conditions pilot, and what does Appendix 1 contain?", "The <b>Extended Practice Authority &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo;</b> (EPA), under the Medicines and Poisons (Medicines) Regulation 2021.<br>Appendix 1 has three columns: <b>1 medicine</b> (a class), <b>2 indication</b>, <b>3 restrictions</b> (the eTG section to follow + an age range). The pharmacist must prescribe in line with the current online eTG section named in Column 3.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1</div>"],
["safe", "Is the EPA a list of individual drugs or of classes? What does the Explanatory Guide say?", "<b>Classes.</b> &ldquo;A pharmacist may only prescribe a medicine from the listed pharmacological classes&rdquo;, in line with the specified eTG section, <b>and</b> the service must follow the relevant Pilot clinical protocol.<br>So the cap is layered: <b>EPA class + indication + age</b> &rarr; <b>eTG section</b> &rarr; <b>protocol</b> (often narrower).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Health Explanatory Guide &ndash; Prescribing by Queensland community pharmacists (Jul 2025)</div>"],
["safe", "Before a pharmacist can prescribe under the chronic conditions EPA, what must be in place?", "&bull; Meet the pilot&rsquo;s <b>Participation Requirements</b> (incl. training).<br>&bull; Prescribe only in a <b>participating pharmacy</b>.<br>&bull; Be listed on the <b>Queensland Community Pharmacy Prescriber Register</b>.<br>&bull; <b>Document consent</b>: evaluation, financial and clinical.<br>&bull; Never prescribe for an animal.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1; QLD Health Explanatory Guide &ndash; Prescribing by Queensland community pharmacists (Jul 2025)</div>"],
["safe", "What must be documented every time you prescribe under the EPA (clause 2.2)?", "Relevant patient history; an assessment of the condition; and the management plan including the medicine&rsquo;s <b>name, strength, formulation, instructions for use and amount</b>.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1</div>"],
["safe", "Prescription limits under the EPA and Explanatory Guide: supply, format, setting, and what you can never prescribe.", "&bull; <b>&le;12 months&rsquo; supply</b> per prescription; quantities should match standard packs or the PBS and last until the next review.<br>&bull; A written prescription compliant with the Medicines Regulation (handwritten, computer-generated or electronic).<br>&bull; Patient must be <b>physically present</b>.<br>&bull; Best practice: <b>another pharmacist dispenses</b> it.<br>&bull; <b>Never</b> a restricted or diversion-risk medicine.<br>&bull; Pharmacist prescriptions may not be dispensable outside Queensland.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1; QLD Health Explanatory Guide &ndash; Prescribing by Queensland community pharmacists (Jul 2025)</div>"],
["htn", "EPA Appendix 1 &mdash; hypertension: which classes, which age range, which eTG section? Is atenolol allowed?", "<b>ACE inhibitors, ARBs, dihydropyridine CCBs, thiazide and thiazide-like diuretics</b>; ages <b>18&ndash;79</b>; per eTG &lsquo;Cardiovascular: Blood pressure reduction: Hypertension and blood pressure reduction&rsquo;.<br>Atenolol (beta blocker) is <b>not</b> an Appendix 1 class &rarr; outside scope.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1</div>"],
["lipid", "EPA Appendix 1 &mdash; lipid modification: which classes and ages?", "<b>Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors), ezetimibe, fibrates</b>; ages <b>18&ndash;79</b>; per eTG &lsquo;Cardiovascular: Lipid modification&rsquo;. PCSK9 inhibitors are not listed.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1</div>"],
["bgl", "EPA Appendix 1 &mdash; blood glucose: which classes and ages? Sulfonylureas or insulin?", "<b>Metformin, SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 analogues, DPP-4 inhibitors</b>; ages <b>18&ndash;79</b>; per eTG &lsquo;Diabetes: Type 2 diabetes in adults&rsquo;.<br>Sulfonylureas and insulin are <b>not</b> Appendix 1 classes &rarr; outside scope.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1; QLD Health Explanatory Guide &ndash; Prescribing by Queensland community pharmacists (Jul 2025)</div>"],
["asthma", "EPA Appendix 1 &mdash; asthma: which classes and ages? How does the protocol narrow it?", "<b>Inhaled anticholinergics, inhaled beta-2 agonists, inhaled corticosteroids, montelukast, prednisolone, prednisone</b>; ages <b>16&ndash;65</b>; per eTG &lsquo;Respiratory: Asthma&rsquo;.<br>Protocol narrows it: <b>do not initiate montelukast</b>; prednis(ol)one up to 5 days, no repeats.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1; QLD Asthma protocol V2</div>"],
["copd", "EPA Appendix 1 &mdash; COPD: which classes and ages? Can you start an ICS under the Program?", "<b>Inhaled anticholinergics, inhaled beta-2 agonists, inhaled corticosteroids, prednisolone, prednisone</b>; ages <b>35 and older</b>; per eTG &lsquo;Respiratory: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease&rsquo;.<br>The EPA lists ICS, but the <b>protocol limits stable COPD to Steps 1&ndash;3</b> (short-acting, then long-acting mono, then LAMA+LABA) &rarr; needing ICS = refer. Prednis(ol)one up to 5 days, no repeats, via the action plan.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1; QLD COPD protocol V2</div>"],
["smoke", "Is smoking cessation prescribing covered by the chronic conditions EPA?", "<b>No.</b> The chronic conditions EPA covers only CVD (HTN, lipids, glucose), asthma and COPD. Smoking cessation runs under its own QLD <b>Smoking Cessation Clinical Practice Guideline</b> (from 1 July 2025; patients <b>18 or over</b>), prescribing per the specified eTG sections. Its own legal authority (the separate EPA &ndash; Pharmacists) is not yet in the app.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD Smoking Cessation CPG (1 Jul 2025)</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["asthma", "A 15-year-old with asthma requests a pharmacist review under the Program. Can you prescribe?", ["Yes &mdash; ICS are listed for asthma", "No &mdash; the EPA asthma age range is 16 to 65", "Yes, with parental consent", "Only a reliever"], 1, "EPA Appendix 1 restricts asthma prescribing to ages 16&ndash;65.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1</div>"],
["copd", "Which EPA age restriction applies to COPD medicines?", ["18 to 79", "16 to 65", "35 and older", "40 and older"], 2, "COPD medicines may only be prescribed to patients aged 35 and older (EPA Appendix 1).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1</div>"],
["safe", "Under the EPA, what is the maximum supply in one prescription?", ["1 month", "6 months", "12 months", "No limit if within the protocol"], 2, "Clause 2.4: not more than 12 months&rsquo; supply of an Appendix 1 medicine in one prescription.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1</div>"],
["htn", "Which medicine is OUTSIDE pharmacist scope for hypertension under the EPA?", ["Perindopril", "Candesartan", "Atenolol", "Indapamide"], 2, "Appendix 1 lists ACE inhibitors, ARBs, DHP CCBs and thiazide/thiazide-like diuretics. Beta blockers are not listed.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1</div>"],
["copd", "A 62-year-old with mild COPD is still breathless on LAMA+LABA. The EPA lists ICS for COPD. What do you do?", ["Add an ICS &mdash; it is EPA-listed", "Refer: the protocol limits stable COPD to Steps 1&ndash;3", "Double the LAMA dose", "Start montelukast"], 1, "The EPA lists the class, but the protocol must also be followed, and it stops at Step 3 (LAMA+LABA). The protocol is the narrower rule.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: QLD COPD protocol V2; QLD Health Explanatory Guide &ndash; Prescribing by Queensland community pharmacists (Jul 2025)</div>"],
["lipid", "An 81-year-old with high CVD risk asks you to start a statin under the Program. Correct response?", ["Start atorvastatin 40 mg", "Start a low-dose statin", "Refer &mdash; lipid medicines are restricted to ages 18&ndash;79", "Start ezetimibe instead"], 2, "EPA Appendix 1 restricts lipid-modifying medicines to ages 18&ndash;79.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1</div>"],
["safe", "Which is NOT a requirement when prescribing under the chronic conditions EPA?", ["Documenting evaluation, financial and clinical consent", "Recording strength, formulation and amount", "The patient being physically present", "A GP co-signature on every prescription"], 3, "The EPA requires consent, documentation and a compliant prescription; the guide requires the patient to be present. No GP co-signature is required.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1; QLD Health Explanatory Guide &ndash; Prescribing by Queensland community pharmacists (Jul 2025)</div>"]
);
(function(){var p={"htn": "<b>Legal scope (EPA Appendix 1):</b> ACE inhibitors, ARBs, DHP CCBs, thiazide/thiazide-like diuretics only; ages <b>18&ndash;79</b>; &le;12 months&rsquo; supply per prescription. ", "lipid": "<b>Legal scope (EPA Appendix 1):</b> statins, ezetimibe, fibrates only; ages <b>18&ndash;79</b>; &le;12 months&rsquo; supply per prescription. ", "bgl": "<b>Legal scope (EPA Appendix 1):</b> metformin, SGLT2i, GLP-1 analogues, DPP-4i only (no sulfonylureas or insulin); ages <b>18&ndash;79</b>; &le;12 months&rsquo; supply. ", "asthma": "<b>Legal scope (EPA Appendix 1):</b> inhaled anticholinergics, inhaled beta-2 agonists, ICS, montelukast, prednisolone/prednisone; ages <b>16&ndash;65</b>. The protocol is narrower (no montelukast initiation). ", "copd": "<b>Legal scope (EPA Appendix 1):</b> inhaled anticholinergics, inhaled beta-2 agonists, ICS, prednisolone/prednisone; ages <b>35+</b>. The protocol is narrower (stable COPD Steps 1&ndash;3, so no ICS initiation). ", "smoke": "<b>Legal scope:</b> not part of the chronic conditions EPA; this is a separate service under the QLD Smoking Cessation CPG (ages 18+). "};if(typeof PACKS!=='undefined'){for(var k in p){if(PACKS[k]){PACKS[k].rxnote=p[k]+(PACKS[k].rxnote||'');}}}var d={"su": "<b>EPA:</b> sulfonylureas are not an Appendix 1 class &mdash; outside pharmacist scope. ", "ins": "<b>EPA:</b> insulin is not an Appendix 1 class. ", "tzd": "<b>EPA:</b> not an Appendix 1 class. ", "agi": "<b>EPA:</b> not an Appendix 1 class. ", "bb": "<b>EPA:</b> beta blockers are not an Appendix 1 class &mdash; outside scope. ", "mont": "<b>EPA</b> lists montelukast for asthma, but the protocol says don&rsquo;t initiate &mdash; the narrower rule wins. ", "ics": "<b>EPA</b> lists ICS for asthma (16&ndash;65) and COPD (35+), but the COPD protocol stops at Step 3, so no ICS initiation for COPD. ", "ocs": "<b>EPA</b> lists prednisolone and prednisone for asthma and COPD. ", "abx": "<b>EPA:</b> antibiotics are not an Appendix 1 class for COPD &mdash; referral only. "};if(typeof DRUGS!=='undefined'){for(var i=0;i<DRUGS.length;i++){var x=DRUGS[i];if(d[x.id]){x.prog=d[x.id]+(x.prog||'');}}}})();
if (typeof OSCE_GUIDE === 'string') { OSCE_GUIDE += "<div class='box blu'><b>Scope check before any prescription (EPA chronic conditions pilot):</b><ol><li>Is the medicine in an <b>Appendix 1 class</b> for this indication? (HTN: ACEi, ARB, DHP CCB, thiazide &middot; Lipids: statin, ezetimibe, fibrate &middot; Glucose: metformin, SGLT2i, GLP-1, DPP-4i &middot; Asthma/COPD: inhaled anticholinergic, beta-2 agonist, ICS, prednis(ol)one; montelukast asthma only)</li><li>Is the patient in the <b>age range</b>? CVD 18&ndash;79 &middot; Asthma 16&ndash;65 &middot; COPD 35+</li><li>Does the <b>eTG section</b> named in Column 3 support it?</li><li>Does the <b>protocol</b> allow it? (max 2 CVD agents; COPD Steps 1&ndash;3; no montelukast initiation; prednis(ol)one &le;5 days, no repeats)</li><li>Prescription: &le;12 months&rsquo; supply, patient present, consent documented (evaluation, financial, clinical), name/strength/form/directions/amount recorded.</li></ol>Say it aloud: &ldquo;This is within scope because&hellip;&rdquo; or &ldquo;This is outside scope, so I&rsquo;ll refer.&rdquo;<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: EPA &lsquo;Pharmacists &ndash; Community pharmacy chronic conditions management pilot&rsquo; v1; QLD Explanatory Guide (Jul 2025); QLD COPD and Asthma protocols V2</div></div>"; }

/* ============================================================
   SECTION AA - AMH 2021 (print) drug-level detail. Built by
   coursework/_review/drafts/amh2021.py; quotes fuzzy-checked vs OCR of photos.
   ============================================================ */
(function(){var A={"acei": "<b>How it works:</b> blocks conversion of angiotensin I to II <i>and</i> inhibits breakdown of bradykinin (hence cough/angioedema).<br><b>Counselling:</b> may feel dizzy when starting &mdash; get up gradually from sitting or lying; sit or lie down if dizzy or light-headed. Don&rsquo;t take potassium supplements unless the doctor says so.<br><b>When starting:</b> stop potassium supplements and potassium-sparing diuretics; review NSAIDs (incl. COX-2); start low; check renal function and electrolytes before and review after 1&ndash;2 weeks.<br><b>Cough:</b> persistent, nonproductive, common; not dose-dependent; can start days to months in; usually settles 1&ndash;4 weeks after stopping.<br><b>Angioedema:</b> face, lips, tongue, upper airway &mdash; or gut (abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhoea); usually week 1 but can be months or years later. DPP-4 inhibitors (eg sitagliptin) may also raise the risk. Sacubitril/valsartan: 36-hour gap.<br><b>Creatinine after starting (AMH):</b> rise &lt;30% (or GFR fall &lt;25%) &rarr; no need to adjust; rise &gt;30% (or GFR fall &gt;25%) &rarr; look for other causes, reduce or stop, consider referral. <i>QLD Table 4 uses up to 25% &mdash; use the protocol figure in the Program.</i><br><b>Pregnancy:</b> avoid in women planning to conceive or with inadequate contraception; switch as soon as possible in the first trimester (2nd/3rd trimester: fetal renal dysfunction, oligohydramnios, fetal death).<br><b>Less effective as monotherapy</b> in people of Black African or Caribbean descent (CCB or thiazide better).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 254&ndash;255. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "arb": "<b>How it works:</b> blocks angiotensin II at AT<sub>1</sub> receptors; does <b>not</b> inhibit bradykinin breakdown &rarr; less cough than an ACEi.<br><b>Use instead of an ACEi</b> if ACEi not tolerated; can be used after ACEi-angioedema <i>with close monitoring</i> (small risk of recurrence).<br><b>Counselling and starting checks:</b> same as ACE inhibitors (dizziness on standing; no potassium supplements; renal function + electrolytes before and at 1&ndash;2 weeks; review NSAIDs).<br><b>Onset:</b> maximum BP effect about <b>4&ndash;6 weeks</b> after starting.<br><b>Common AEs:</b> dizziness, headache, hyperkalaemia. Olmesartan rarely causes a sprue-like enteropathy (severe chronic diarrhoea, weight loss) months&ndash;years in &mdash; &lsquo;tell your doctor if you have severe or chronic diarrhoea&rsquo;.<br><b>Never with an ACE inhibitor</b> outside specialist care: worse renal function, more hypotension and hyperkalaemia, no added benefit.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 259&ndash;262. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "dhpccb": "<b>How it works:</b> blocks L-type calcium channels; dihydropyridines act mainly on arteriolar smooth muscle &rarr; lower peripheral resistance, minimal effect on the heart.<br><b>Ankle oedema:</b> common with dihydropyridines; caused by fluid <b>redistribution</b>, not retention &mdash; it does <b>not</b> respond to diuretics (which risk volume depletion).<br><b>Vasodilatory AEs</b> (headache, flushing, dizziness, palpitations) usually settle with continued treatment; may need a lower dose. Gingival hyperplasia (usually reversible).<br><b>Counselling:</b> felodipine and nifedipine CR &mdash; swallow whole, don&rsquo;t crush or chew; avoid grapefruit juice. Lercanidipine &mdash; take at least 15 minutes before a meal.<br><b>Interaction:</b> amlodipine raises simvastatin levels &mdash; simvastatin max 40 mg with amlodipine.<br><b>Doses (AMH 2021):</b> amlodipine 2.5&ndash;5 mg, increase after &ge;1&ndash;2 weeks to max 10 mg (elderly/hepatic 2.5 mg); felodipine CR 5&ndash;10 mg (max 20); lercanidipine 10&ndash;20 mg; nifedipine CR 30&ndash;120 mg once daily.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 264&ndash;267, 297. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "thiaz": "<b>How it works:</b> blocks Na/Cl reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule; at low antihypertensive doses BP falls mostly by vasodilation.<br><b>AEs are dose-related:</b> dizziness, cramps, polyuria, postural hypotension; low Na, low K, low Mg, high Ca, high uric acid (gout), raised glucose and lipids, impotence, photosensitivity.<br><b>Counselling:</b> take in the <b>morning</b> (if twice daily, second dose before 6 pm); get up slowly if dizzy. Report dizziness, thirst, or fluid loss from vomiting, diarrhoea or heavy sweating. Indapamide CR &mdash; swallow whole.<br><b>Low K</b> is less likely with an ACEi/ARB.<br><b>Comparative:</b> chlortalidone 25 mg &asymp; hydrochlorothiazide 50 mg; chlortalidone and indapamide have outcome data, hydrochlorothiazide monotherapy does not.<br><b>Elderly:</b> lower start; more hyponatraemia, hypokalaemia and orthostatic hypotension. Less diuretic effect at CrCl &lt;30, but BP effect may persist.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 251&ndash;253. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "bb": "<b>Role (AMH 2021):</b> less effective at reducing stroke and associated with more new diabetes than other classes &mdash; reserved for compelling indications (angina, post-MI, heart failure with carvedilol, bisoprolol, CR metoprolol or nebivolol).<br><b>Unfavourable:</b> asthma/COPD (cardioselective may be used cautiously), bradycardia, 2nd/3rd-degree heart block. Never with verapamil or diltiazem (bradycardia, heart block).<br>May <b>mask hypoglycaemia</b> warning signs.<br><i>Beta-blocker monographs were not in the photographed pages.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 248&ndash;249, 425. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "statin": "<b>Counselling:</b> seek medical advice promptly if <b>urine is dark (brown)</b> or there is any <b>muscle pain, tenderness or weakness</b>. Atorvastatin and simvastatin: avoid grapefruit juice.<br><b>Monitoring:</b> AST/ALT and CK at baseline; repeat only if clinically indicated. Lipids every 4&ndash;6 weeks while titrating (full effect after &ge;4 weeks), then every 6&ndash;12 months.<br><b>Stop the statin if:</b> aminotransferases persistently &gt;3&times; ULN; CK &gt;10&times; ULN; or persistent unexplained muscle pain/weakness (even with normal CK). May restart after &ge;4 weeks if mild and CK normal &mdash; lower dose, different statin, or alternate-day/twice-weekly atorvastatin or rosuvastatin.<br><b>Muscle symptoms</b> are dose-related and most likely in the first 4&ndash;6 weeks after starting or increasing. CoQ10 and vitamin D have not been shown to help.<br><b>Dose:</b> &gt;80% of the LDL effect comes at 50% of the maximum dose (eg atorvastatin 40 mg); adding ezetimibe then lowers LDL more than doubling the statin.<br><b>Timing:</b> adherence matters most; short-acting simvastatin/pravastatin may be slightly better in the evening.<br><b>Interactions:</b> fewer with fluvastatin, pravastatin, rosuvastatin. Systemic fusidic acid &rarr; stop the statin until 1 week after the course. Rosuvastatin: lowest start in people of Asian ancestry; CrCl &lt;30 start 5 mg (max 10).<br><b>Pregnancy:</b> contraindicated; avoid in women planning to conceive. Don&rsquo;t stop a statin during suspected ACS.<br><b>Diabetes:</b> small rise in new diabetes (1 extra case per 255 treated for 4 years vs &gt;5 major coronary events prevented).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 293&ndash;296. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "ezet": "<b>AMH 2021 Table 6-10:</b> lowers LDL by about <b>15&ndash;25%</b>; once daily; well tolerated. Use when a statin is contraindicated or not tolerated, or add to a statin when the statin alone is insufficient. With fenofibrate: may further increase risk of gall bladder disease.<br><i>The ezetimibe monograph (p 300) was not in the photographed pages.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 292, 297. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "fenof": "<b>How it works:</b> activates PPAR nuclear receptors &rarr; lowers TG (40&ndash;80%), raises HDL; variable LDL effect.<br><b>Outcomes:</b> fibrates have <b>not</b> been shown to reduce mortality, fatal MI or stroke. Main use: severe hypertriglyceridaemia (TG &gt;10 mmol/L) to prevent pancreatitis &mdash; specialist advice.<br><b>Contraindications:</b> photosensitivity to a fibrate or ketoprofen; severe renal impairment; hepatic impairment, gallstones or gall bladder disease; pancreatitis not due to high TG.<br><b>Counselling:</b> dark (brown) urine or muscle pain/tenderness/weakness &rarr; seek advice promptly; avoid sun, wear protective clothing and sunscreen.<br><b>Monitoring (AMH):</b> FBC and LFT at baseline and during treatment; CK at baseline. Fenofibrate: aminotransferases every 3 months in year 1 (stop if &gt;3&times; ULN). Creatinine may rise (reversible). Can lower uric acid ~25%. Paradoxical HDL fall &mdash; stop if marked.<br><b>Dose:</b> 145 mg daily; CrCl 20&ndash;60 96 mg; CrCl 10&ndash;20 48 mg.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 297&ndash;298. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "met": "<b>How it works:</b> lowers hepatic glucose output and increases peripheral glucose use.<br><b>Counselling:</b> take with or after food; swallow XR whole. Tell the doctor straight away about loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, cramps, tiredness, diarrhoea or weight loss. Alcohol: limit it, avoid binge drinking, eat when drinking.<br><b>Practice points:</b> onset is slow &mdash; control may take up to 2 weeks; titrate slowly for GI effects (if XR in the morning upsets, try the evening meal); renal function before starting and every 4&ndash;6 months; B12 malabsorption.<br><b>Sick days / surgery:</b> stop if acutely unwell and at risk of renal deterioration (dehydration, sepsis, shock, MI, severe HF); stop before surgery and restart once eating and renal function recovered.<br><b>Lactic acidosis:</b> rare; causal link not established.<br><b>Renal (AMH 2021):</b> max 2 g at CrCl 60&ndash;90, 1 g at 30&ndash;60; generally not recommended below 30. <i>eGFR &lt;45 excludes the patient from the QLD Program anyway.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 424, 440&ndash;441. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "sglt2": "<b>How it works:</b> blocks SGLT2 &rarr; less renal glucose reabsorption, more glucose in urine (so efficacy falls as renal function falls).<br><b>Counselling:</b> urine will test positive for glucose; drink enough water to control thirst; genital thrush is more likely in men and women &mdash; good hygiene; seek advice for infection, especially fever with pain, tenderness or swelling in the genital area. With or without food.<br><b>Withhold</b> in acute serious illness, prolonged fasting, bowel prep, low-carbohydrate diet, <b>excessive alcohol</b>; stop <b>3 days before surgery</b>, restart when eating and drinking normally.<br><b>Euglycaemic ketoacidosis:</b> glucose normal or only mildly raised. Unexplained severe vomiting or abdominal pain &rarr; stop and test <b>blood</b> ketones even if glucose is normal (urine ketones may be unreliable).<br><b>Volume:</b> osmotic diuresis &mdash; check volume status before starting; care with loop diuretics and in the elderly (hypotension, fainting).<br><b>Also:</b> creatinine may rise early (usually transient); weight &minus;2&ndash;3 kg; SBP &minus;3&ndash;5 mmHg; foot care (amputation signal with canagliflozin).<br><i>AMH 2021 lists dapagliflozin/empagliflozin as contraindicated at eGFR &lt;45 &mdash; older labelling; the Program excludes eGFR &lt;45 regardless.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 434&ndash;435. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "glp1": "<b>How it works:</b> incretin analogue &mdash; glucose-dependent insulin release, less glucagon, slower gastric emptying, less appetite.<br><b>Counselling:</b> nausea and sometimes vomiting/diarrhoea are common at the start and usually settle &mdash; drink plenty of fluids to avoid dehydration. <b>Tell the doctor immediately about unexplained severe abdominal pain</b> (pancreatitis). Weekly agents: same day each week, with or without a meal; the day can change if &ge;3 days (72 h) between doses.<br><b>Precautions:</b> avoid in severe GI disease (gastroparesis); caution with previous pancreatitis; gall bladder disease; hypo risk only with a sulfonylurea or insulin (reduce their dose). Semaglutide: retinopathy may worsen &mdash; monitor; stop &ge;2 months before planned pregnancy.<br><b>Effects:</b> modest weight loss (~2&ndash;5 kg), SBP &minus;1&ndash;5 mmHg, HR +2&ndash;4 bpm. Dulaglutide and liraglutide (and SC semaglutide) reduced major CV events.<br><b>Titration:</b> semaglutide 0.25 mg weekly for 4 weeks, then 0.5 mg (then 1 mg if needed after &ge;4 weeks).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 430&ndash;434. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "dpp4": "<b>How it works:</b> blocks DPP-4 &rarr; more GLP-1 and GIP &rarr; glucose-dependent insulin release, less glucagon.<br><b>Counselling:</b> with or without food. Weight-neutral; hypoglycaemia mainly with a sulfonylurea or insulin.<br><b>Practice points:</b> less HbA1c-lowering than metformin, sulfonylurea or pioglitazone; no CV benefit in outcome trials (saxagliptin increased HF hospitalisation); pancreatitis risk likely very low; starting with DPP-4i + metformin together is not recommended. Vildagliptin (and possibly others) linked to ACEi angioedema.<br><b>Renal:</b> linagliptin needs no adjustment (biliary); sitagliptin 100 mg &rarr; 50 mg (eGFR 30&ndash;45) &rarr; 25 mg (&lt;30).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 427&ndash;429. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "saba": "<b>Onset/duration:</b> salbutamol/terbutaline act in 5&ndash;15 min, last 3&ndash;6 h.<br><b>Key message:</b> increasing SABA use (especially daily) means worsening asthma &mdash; prompt review. Most adults should not use a SABA without an ICS; over-reliance &rarr; tolerance, more exacerbations and asthma deaths.<br><b>Counselling:</b> have a written action plan; tell the doctor ASAP if needing it more often; use a spacer with a pMDI (not Autohaler); clean the plastic mouthpiece regularly to stop the nozzle blocking.<br><b>AEs:</b> tremor, palpitations, headache; high doses &rarr; hypokalaemia (worse with corticosteroids, diuretics, theophylline, hypoxia).<br><b>First aid (AMH 2021):</b> 4 puffs (400 micrograms) via spacer, repeat after 4 minutes; no improvement &rarr; call an ambulance and keep giving 4 puffs every 4 minutes.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 890, 896&ndash;898. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "laba": "<b>Asthma:</b> &lsquo;If using a LABA for asthma, always use with an ICS&rsquo; &mdash; LABA alone increases serious exacerbations and asthma deaths. Use a combination inhaler.<br><b>Onset:</b> formoterol 1&ndash;3 min (so usable as reliever in ICS-formoterol); salmeterol 10&ndash;30 min (never a reliever). Indacaterol and olodaterol are COPD-only.<br><b>Counselling:</b> maintenance &mdash; use every day even if you feel better; salmeterol &mdash; don&rsquo;t use to relieve an attack. Indacaterol: brief post-inhalation cough.<br><b>COPD:</b> LABA monotherapy appears safe; combinations are more convenient.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 890, 896&ndash;898. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "lama": "<b>How it works:</b> blocks muscarinic receptors &rarr; less cholinergic bronchomotor tone.<br><b>COPD:</b> LAMAs are often used first (may prevent exacerbations slightly better than LABAs); tiotropium has the strongest evidence. <b>Stop ipratropium</b> when a LAMA starts.<br><b>Counselling:</b> keep powder/mist out of the eyes; report eye pain, blurred vision or halos, or difficulty passing urine; don&rsquo;t drive until you know how it affects you; not for immediate relief.<br><b>Precautions:</b> angle-closure glaucoma risk, bladder outlet obstruction, cardiac disease; tiotropium and glycopyrronium &mdash; more anticholinergic effects in renal impairment.<br><b>Common AEs:</b> dry mouth, throat irritation. Only use Spiriva capsules in the HandiHaler and Braltus capsules in the Zonda.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 893, 899&ndash;901. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "sama": "<b>Ipratropium:</b> short-acting alternative to a SABA for mild COPD; can be combined with a SABA in exacerbations, but <b>stop it when a LAMA is started</b> (additive anticholinergic effects). In asthma, mainly for severe acute attacks.<br>Counselling as for LAMAs (eyes, urinary retention, dizziness).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 894, 899&ndash;900. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "ics": "<b>Counselling:</b> use a <b>spacer</b> with an MDI; <b>rinse the mouth with water, gargle and spit out</b> after use; use every day even if well; don&rsquo;t reduce or stop unless told; have an action plan.<br><b>Local AEs:</b> dysphonia, oral thrush, bruising. Systemic effects depend on dose &mdash; reduced by spacer + mouth rinse.<br><b>High dose:</b> adrenal suppression (don&rsquo;t stop suddenly), bone loss (consider osteoporosis screening), cataract/glaucoma, skin thinning. In COPD: <b>pneumonia</b> risk.<br><b>Dosing:</b> start low; doses above the low range add little benefit but more AEs; when controlled, step down by 25&ndash;50% every 3 months. Smokers may respond less well.<br><b>Equivalence (AMH):</b> 100 beclometasone = 200 budesonide = 80 ciclesonide = 20 fluticasone furoate = 100 fluticasone propionate (micrograms).<br><b>COPD:</b> no role for ICS monotherapy; add to long-acting bronchodilators only with persistent symptoms and frequent exacerbations.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 894, 903&ndash;904. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "icslaba": "<b>Budesonide/formoterol or beclometasone/formoterol</b> can be used for relief as well as maintenance; tell the doctor ASAP if you need it more often or at higher doses.<br><b>Review triggers (AMH 2021):</b> budesonide/formoterol &mdash; within 2&ndash;3 days if &gt;6 DPI inhalations (or &gt;12 MDI) are needed in 24 h for relief; Fostair &mdash; if 6 inhalations/day for 2 consecutive days.<br><b>Devices:</b> Turbuhaler and Spiromax differ in design and technique &mdash; check technique if the brand changes. Can be used 15 minutes before exercise instead of a SABA by people using it as their reliever.<br><b>Don&rsquo;t double up:</b> when starting a combination inhaler, stop other inhalers containing the same class.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 892&ndash;893, 905&ndash;906. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "mont": "<b>AMH 2021:</b> generally <b>less effective than low-dose ICS</b>; in adults sometimes an add-on in severe asthma, with no data supporting long-term use unless there is clear symptomatic improvement. Also an option for exercise-induced bronchoconstriction (no tolerance develops, but less effective than SABA).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 890, 892. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "ocs": "<b>COPD flare:</b> short oral courses shorten recovery and reduce early relapse; <b>5-day regimens may suffice</b>; courses &gt;14 days add no benefit but more AEs; consider osteoporosis prevention with frequent courses. No role for long-term oral corticosteroids in COPD.<br><b>Asthma:</b> a short course (&lt;14 days) of prednisolone can be stopped without tapering.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 892, 894. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "nrt-patch": "<b>Counselling:</b> apply to non-hairy, clean, dry skin on the upper body or outer upper arm; <b>different place each day</b>; dispose of safely. Skin redness/itch &mdash; stop if severe.<br><b>Choice:</b> 16-hour patch as effective as 24-hour; 24-hour suits people who smoke soon after waking (more vivid dreams); 16-hour if sleep is disturbed.<br><b>Dose (AMH 2021):</b> moderate&ndash;high dependence 21 mg/24 h or 15&ndash;25 mg/16 h; low&ndash;moderate 14 mg/24 h or 10&ndash;15 mg/16 h; stop within 12 weeks.<br><b>Precautions:</b> recent MI or stroke, unstable angina, severe arrhythmia &mdash; NRT risk is outweighed by the risk of smoking (non-drug first). Pregnancy: short-acting forms preferred; remove patches at night.<br><b>AEs:</b> usually minor; sleep disturbance, dizziness, weight gain and headache may be from quitting itself. Vivid dreams with 24-hour patches.<br><b>Practice:</b> up to 12 weeks (6&ndash;8 for most) incl. taper. NRT delivers less nicotine than smoking &mdash; make sure the dose is enough.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 879&ndash;881. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "nrt-oral": "<b>All oral forms:</b> don&rsquo;t eat or drink while using (less absorption through the mouth lining).<br><b>Gum:</b> &lsquo;chew and park&rsquo; &mdash; chew slowly ~10 times until tingling/peppery, park between cheek and gum until it fades, repeat; ~30 min per piece; not for dentures.<br><b>Lozenge:</b> let it dissolve, don&rsquo;t chew or swallow (up to 30 min). Avoid in phenylketonuria (aspartame).<br><b>Spray:</b> aim at the inside of the cheek or under the tongue, avoid lips, hold breath, don&rsquo;t swallow for a few seconds; max 4 sprays/hour, 64/day.<br><b>Inhalator:</b> avoid in asthma or chronic throat conditions; helps hand-to-mouth habit.<br><b>Precautions:</b> recent MI or stroke, unstable angina, severe arrhythmia &mdash; NRT risk is outweighed by the risk of smoking (non-drug first). Pregnancy: short-acting forms preferred; remove patches at night.<br><b>AEs:</b> usually minor; sleep disturbance, dizziness, weight gain and headache may be from quitting itself. Vivid dreams with 24-hour patches.<br><b>Practice:</b> up to 12 weeks (6&ndash;8 for most) incl. taper. NRT delivers less nicotine than smoking &mdash; make sure the dose is enough.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 879&ndash;881. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "nrt-combo": "<b>AMH 2021:</b> combining patches with a faster-acting form (gum, inhaler, lozenge, spray) raises quit rates over monotherapy and can be first line &mdash; especially for people with previous failed attempts or ongoing cravings. If still unsuccessful, consider adding a <b>second patch</b>. Fast-acting forms can also be used with bupropion or varenicline.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 878, 880&ndash;881. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "varenicline": "<b>How it works:</b> partial agonist at neuronal nicotinic receptors &mdash; blocks the reward from smoking while easing withdrawal.<br><b>Counselling:</b> start <b>at least 1 week before</b> the quit date (it takes that long to work).<br><b>Titration:</b> 0.5 mg daily &times; 3 days &rarr; 0.5 mg twice daily &times; 4 days &rarr; 1 mg twice daily for 11&ndash;23 weeks. Nausea is dose-related (30% vs 10% placebo), usually transient; if intolerable drop to 1 mg daily. CrCl &lt;30: max 1 mg daily.<br><b>AEs:</b> nausea, abnormal dreams, insomnia, headache, weight gain; rare seizures, angioedema/SJS. Caution with a seizure history. Avoid in pregnancy.<br><b>Efficacy:</b> more than doubles quitting vs placebo; similar to combination NRT; better than bupropion. Withdrawal may increase when it&rsquo;s stopped.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 878, 881. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "bupropion": "<b>Contraindications:</b> seizure history, CNS tumour, bulimia or anorexia nervosa, abrupt alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal, MAOI within 14 days. Caution: other seizure risks (head injury, alcohol misuse, interacting drugs), bipolar disorder, hypertension (control BP first).<br><b>Counselling:</b> start <b>at least a week before</b> quitting; take in the <b>morning</b> for the first few days (insomnia); swallow whole; only small amounts of alcohol (seizures); rash, lip/mouth swelling or breathing difficulty &rarr; stop and contact the doctor; dizziness/poor concentration &mdash; care driving.<br><b>Dose:</b> 150 mg each morning &times; 3 days, then 150 mg twice daily (&ge;8 hours apart) for 7&ndash;9 weeks; elderly/renal/mild hepatic 150 mg daily.<br><b>Practice:</b> insomnia &gt;40%; false-positive urine amphetamine screens; stop if no marked reduction in smoking; adding NRT improves effect.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 878&ndash;879. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"};if(typeof DRUGS!=='undefined'){for(var i=0;i<DRUGS.length;i++){var x=DRUGS[i];if(A[x.id]){x.amh=A[x.id];x.src=(x.src?x.src+'; ':'')+'AMH 2021 (print)';}}}})();
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["htn", "Counselling an ACE inhibitor or ARB start (AMH): what do you tell the patient, and what do you do before and after?", "<b>Tell:</b> may feel dizzy at first &mdash; get up slowly, sit/lie down if light-headed; no potassium supplements unless the doctor says.<br><b>Do:</b> stop K supplements and K-sparing diuretics; review NSAIDs (incl. COX-2); start low; renal function + electrolytes <b>before</b> and <b>1&ndash;2 weeks after</b>.<br><b>Safety-net:</b> swelling of face, lips, tongue or throat &rarr; urgent care (angioedema can occur months to years in).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 254&ndash;255, 260. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["htn", "Creatinine rises after starting an ACE inhibitor. Compare the AMH 2021 rule with QLD HTN protocol Table 4.", "<b>AMH 2021:</b> rise &lt;30% (or GFR fall &lt;25%) &rarr; no need to adjust; &gt;30% (or GFR fall &gt;25%) &rarr; look for other causes, reduce or stop, consider referral. Usually stabilises within 2 months.<br><b>QLD Table 4:</b> a creatinine rise <b>up to 25%</b> or potassium within range should not necessarily prompt reduction/cessation.<br><b>In the Program, use the protocol&rsquo;s 25%.</b><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 255. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["htn", "ACE inhibitor cough: features and what to expect after stopping (AMH).", "Persistent, <b>nonproductive</b>, common; <b>not dose-dependent</b> and unlikely to respond to treatment; can start days to months after starting; usually improves <b>1&ndash;4 weeks after stopping</b>. An ARB causes less cough (doesn&rsquo;t block bradykinin breakdown).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 255, 260. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["htn", "Why doesn&rsquo;t amlodipine ankle swelling get a diuretic? (AMH)", "Dihydropyridine oedema comes from <b>redistribution</b> of extracellular fluid, not fluid retention &mdash; it doesn&rsquo;t respond to diuretics, and a diuretic risks volume depletion. Options: lower dose or change class.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 265. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["htn", "AMH 2021: which antihypertensive combinations should you avoid, and why?", "&bull; <b>Verapamil or diltiazem + beta-blocker</b> &mdash; severe bradycardia and heart block.<br>&bull; <b>ACEi/ARB + potassium-sparing diuretic</b> &mdash; hyperkalaemia.<br>&bull; <b>ACEi + ARB</b> &mdash; questionable benefit, more renal complications; specialist only.<br>Preferred pairs: ACEi/ARB + DHP CCB or thiazide; DHP CCB + thiazide.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 249. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["htn", "BP not at target on one drug (AMH 2021): add a second drug or push the dose? How long before judging response?", "Allow <b>4&ndash;6 weeks</b> to judge response. If inadequate, <b>add a second drug</b> rather than increasing the first (higher doses add AEs without much more BP lowering). If still above target, increase one (non-thiazide) toward max, and check adherence, salt, drug causes (NSAIDs, corticosteroids), sleep apnoea.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 248&ndash;249. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["htn", "Thiazide counselling points (AMH).", "Take in the <b>morning</b> (2nd dose before 6 pm if twice daily); get up slowly; report dizziness, thirst or fluid loss (vomiting, diarrhoea, sweating). AEs: low Na/K/Mg, high uric acid (gout), glucose; photosensitivity. Elderly: lower dose, more hyponatraemia and postural hypotension.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 251&ndash;253. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["lipid", "Statin: when does AMH say to STOP it?", "&bull; Aminotransferases <b>persistently &gt;3&times; ULN</b><br>&bull; CK <b>&gt;10&times; ULN</b><br>&bull; Persistent unexplained muscle pain or weakness <b>even if CK is normal</b>.<br>May resume after &ge;4 weeks if mild and CK normal &mdash; lower dose, another statin, or alternate-day/twice-weekly atorvastatin or rosuvastatin.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 295. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["lipid", "Statin counselling one-liner (AMH) and the monitoring schedule.", "&lsquo;Seek medical advice promptly if your urine is <b>dark (brown)</b> or you have any <b>muscle pain, tenderness or weakness</b>.&rsquo; Atorvastatin/simvastatin: avoid grapefruit juice.<br>Monitoring: ALT/AST + CK at baseline (repeat if clinically indicated); lipids every 4&ndash;6 weeks while titrating, then every 6&ndash;12 months.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 293&ndash;295. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["lipid", "Atorvastatin 40 mg isn&rsquo;t enough. Double the statin or add ezetimibe? (AMH)", "&gt;80% of a statin&rsquo;s LDL-lowering comes at 50% of max dose (eg atorvastatin 40 mg). Adding ezetimibe (LDL &minus;15&ndash;25%) is then <b>more effective at lowering LDL</b> than raising the statin (clinical-outcome data for combinations limited). Check adherence first.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 292, 294. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["lipid", "Which statins have fewer CYP interactions, and two named interactions to know? (AMH)", "Fewer: <b>fluvastatin, pravastatin, rosuvastatin</b> (vs atorvastatin, simvastatin). Simvastatin: max 40 mg with amlodipine; max 20 mg with amiodarone, diltiazem, verapamil. Systemic <b>fusidic acid</b>: stop the statin until 1 week after the course (rhabdomyolysis).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 294, 297. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["lipid", "Fenofibrate: outcomes, counselling and monitoring (AMH).", "No reduction in mortality, fatal MI or stroke shown &mdash; main role is TG &gt;10 mmol/L (pancreatitis risk). Counsel: dark urine/muscle symptoms &rarr; seek advice; sun protection. Monitor FBC + LFT baseline and during; CK baseline; ALT/AST 3-monthly in year 1. Dose 145 mg; reduce in renal impairment.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 297&ndash;298. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["bgl", "SGLT2 inhibitor counselling (AMH): five points.", "1. Urine will test positive for glucose.<br>2. Drink enough water to control thirst.<br>3. Genital thrush more likely &mdash; hygiene; seek advice for genital pain, swelling or fever.<br>4. Sick days, fasting, low-carb diet, heavy alcohol &rarr; withhold (ketoacidosis).<br>5. Stop 3 days before surgery; restart when eating and drinking normally.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 434&ndash;435. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["bgl", "Euglycaemic ketoacidosis on an SGLT2i: what does it look like and what do you do? (AMH)", "BGL <b>normal or only mildly raised</b>. Triggers: surgery, infection, dehydration, missed/reduced insulin. Unexplained severe vomiting, abdominal pain or high anion gap acidosis &rarr; <b>stop the SGLT2i, test blood ketones even if glucose is normal</b> (urine ketones may be unreliable); urgent care. Report to TGA.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 434. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["bgl", "GLP-1 RA counselling (AMH).", "Nausea (up to 50%), sometimes vomiting/diarrhoea at the start &mdash; usually settles; drink fluids to avoid dehydration. <b>Unexplained severe abdominal pain &rarr; tell the doctor immediately</b>. Weekly: same day each week; can switch day if &ge;72 h between doses. Hypo only with SU/insulin. Semaglutide: retinopathy may worsen; stop &ge;2 months before planned pregnancy.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 431&ndash;433. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["bgl", "Metformin counselling (AMH).", "With or after food; swallow XR whole; GI effects settle with slow titration (try evening meal); control takes up to 2 weeks. Report loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, cramps, fatigue, diarrhoea, weight loss. Limit alcohol, no binges, eat when drinking. Sick day: stop if acutely unwell/dehydrated.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 441. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["bgl", "Which drugs raise blood glucose, and what does alcohol do? (AMH)", "Raise BGL: <b>glucocorticoids, antipsychotics, calcineurin inhibitors, somatropin, high-dose thiazides</b>. Beta-blockers can mask hypo symptoms. <b>Alcohol lowers BGL</b> (inhibits hepatic glucose output), raises hypo risk and masks warning signs &mdash; limit alcohol and eat with it.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 425. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["bgl", "AMH 2021: second-line choice in T2D by comorbidity.", "Metformin first for all. Then: <b>atherosclerotic CVD</b> &rarr; SGLT2i or long-acting GLP-1 RA; <b>heart failure</b> &rarr; SGLT2i (fewer HF admissions); <b>CKD</b> &rarr; SGLT2i or long-acting GLP-1 RA may slow progression. Add a second agent if not at target after about 3 months.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 424. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["asthma", "ICS counselling (AMH): what reduces thrush, hoarseness and systemic effects?", "Use a <b>spacer</b> with an MDI and <b>rinse the mouth with water, gargle and spit out</b> after each use. Use every day even when well; don&rsquo;t stop without advice. Smokers may respond less well. High doses: adrenal suppression, bone loss, cataract, bruising.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 903&ndash;904. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["asthma", "&lsquo;If using a LABA for asthma&hellip;&rsquo; finish the AMH sentence, and explain why.", "&lsquo;&hellip;always use with an ICS.&rsquo; LABA without ICS is linked to serious exacerbations and asthma deaths (greatest in children &lt;12). With an ICS the risk is not significantly higher than ICS alone &mdash; so use a combination inhaler.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 890. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["asthma", "Spacer care and who needs one (AMH).", "For: children, poor coordination, acute attacks, anyone on ICS (esp. higher doses), oral thrush/hoarseness. <b>Wash before first use, then monthly</b> in warm water with dishwashing detergent, <b>don&rsquo;t rinse</b>, <b>air dry</b> (wiping causes static so medicine sticks); check for cracks and valve function.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 895. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["asthma", "Drugs and products that can trigger bronchospasm in asthma (AMH practice points).", "<b>Beta-blockers (including eye drops)</b>; complementary medicines such as <b>royal jelly and echinacea</b>; <b>aspirin and NSAIDs</b> in those who have reacted to them before.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 892. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["copd", "LAMA counselling (AMH).", "Keep powder/mist out of the eyes; report <b>eye pain, blurred vision or halos</b> (angle-closure) or <b>difficulty urinating</b>; dry mouth is common; don&rsquo;t use for immediate relief. Stop ipratropium when a LAMA starts.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 899. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["copd", "COPD: what does AMH 2021 say about ICS, smoking cessation and oral steroid course length?", "&bull; <b>Smoking cessation</b> halves the rate of lung-function decline and prolongs survival &mdash; the most effective intervention.<br>&bull; ICS: no role as monotherapy; add to long-acting bronchodilators with persistent symptoms and frequent exacerbations; <b>pneumonia</b> risk.<br>&bull; Oral corticosteroids for flares: 5 days may suffice; &gt;14 days adds no benefit.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 893&ndash;894. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["smoke", "AMH 2021: grade nicotine dependence from two questions.", "<b>High:</b> waking at night to smoke or within 5 min of waking; usually &gt;30/day.<br><b>Moderate:</b> within 30 min of waking; 20&ndash;30/day.<br><b>Low&ndash;moderate:</b> not needed within 30 min; 10&ndash;20/day.<br><b>Low:</b> not in the first hour; &lt;10/day.<br>Drug therapy for those smoking &ge;10/day (no evidence of benefit in low dependence); always with behavioural support (Quitline 13 78 48).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 878. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["smoke", "Nicotine gum: how do you teach &lsquo;chew and park&rsquo;? (AMH)", "Chew slowly about 10 times until tingling or a bitter taste, then park between cheek and gum until the tingling fades, then chew again; each piece lasts ~30 min. Don&rsquo;t eat or drink while using. Over-chewing &rarr; salivation, hiccups, indigestion. Not for dentures.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 880&ndash;881. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["smoke", "Patient quitting smoking takes clozapine or theophylline. Why does it matter? (AMH)", "Tobacco <b>smoke</b> induces <b>CYP1A2</b>, speeding their metabolism. After quitting, levels rise &mdash; doses may need reducing soon after stopping. (NRT doesn&rsquo;t reverse this &mdash; it&rsquo;s the smoke, not the nicotine.)<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 878. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["smoke", "Bupropion for smoking: contraindications and counselling (AMH).", "<b>CI:</b> seizure history, CNS tumour, bulimia/anorexia nervosa, abrupt alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal, MAOI in last 14 days.<br><b>Counsel:</b> start &ge;1 week before quitting; take in the morning for the first few days; swallow whole; minimal alcohol; rash/swelling/breathing difficulty &rarr; stop and seek help. Dose 150 mg mane &times; 3 days then 150 mg bd (&ge;8 h apart) for 7&ndash;9 weeks.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 879. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["smoke", "Varenicline: how does it work, how is it started, and what is the main side effect? (AMH)", "Partial agonist at nicotinic receptors &mdash; blocks smoking reward and eases withdrawal. Start &ge;1 week before quit date: 0.5 mg daily &times; 3 days, 0.5 mg bd &times; 4 days, then 1 mg bd (total 12&ndash;24 weeks). <b>Nausea</b> (30% vs 10% placebo), dose-related and usually transient &mdash; drop to 1 mg daily if intolerable. Also abnormal dreams, insomnia.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 881. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["smoke", "NRT and cardiovascular disease or pregnancy (AMH).", "Recent MI/stroke, unstable angina, severe arrhythmia: the risk of NRT is <b>outweighed by the risk of smoking</b> &mdash; but try non-drug treatment first. Pregnancy: NRT is acceptable if needed; prefer <b>short-acting</b> forms; patches removed at night. Breastfeeding: short-acting form, dose just after a feed.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 879&ndash;880. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["htn", "A patient on amlodipine 10 mg has bilateral ankle oedema. What does AMH 2021 say about adding a diuretic?", ["It is first-line for CCB oedema", "It usually won&rsquo;t help, because the oedema is from fluid redistribution, and risks volume depletion", "Only loop diuretics work", "Add spironolactone"], 1, "Dihydropyridine oedema is due to redistribution of extracellular fluid, not retention, so it doesn&rsquo;t respond to diuretics.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 265. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["htn", "AMH 2021 &mdash; after starting an ACE inhibitor, creatinine rises 20%. What does AMH advise?", ["Stop the ACE inhibitor", "Halve the dose", "No need to adjust the dose (rise &lt;30%)", "Switch to an ARB"], 2, "AMH: rise &lt;30% (or GFR fall &lt;25%) needs no adjustment; &gt;30% investigate. QLD Table 4 frames it as up to 25% &mdash; 20% is fine under both.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 255. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["htn", "Which combination does AMH 2021 say to avoid because of severe bradycardia and heart block?", ["Amlodipine + perindopril", "Verapamil + a beta-blocker", "Indapamide + perindopril", "Amlodipine + a thiazide"], 1, "Diltiazem or verapamil with a beta-blocker risks severe bradycardia and heart block.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 249. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["lipid", "Per AMH 2021, which finding alone is a reason to stop a statin?", ["CK 3&times; ULN with no symptoms", "ALT 2&times; ULN once", "Persistent unexplained muscle weakness with a normal CK", "LDL at target"], 2, "Stop if aminotransferases persistently &gt;3&times; ULN, CK &gt;10&times; ULN, or persistent unexplained muscle pain/weakness even if CK is normal.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 295. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["lipid", "A patient on simvastatin is prescribed systemic fusidic acid for 10 days. AMH 2021 advice?", ["Continue both, monitor CK", "Stop the statin and restart 1 week after the fusidic acid finishes", "Halve the statin", "Switch to rosuvastatin during the course"], 1, "Risk of rhabdomyolysis; stop the statin for 1 week longer than the fusidic acid course (combined use is contraindicated by manufacturers).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 293. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["bgl", "A patient on empagliflozin has vomiting and abdominal pain; BGL 9 mmol/L. Most appropriate per AMH 2021?", ["Reassure &mdash; glucose is near normal", "Check urine ketones only", "Stop the SGLT2i, check blood ketones and seek urgent care", "Double fluids and continue"], 2, "Euglycaemic ketoacidosis: glucose may be normal or mildly raised; stop and test blood ketones (urine ketones may be unreliable).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 434. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["bgl", "Which counselling point belongs to a GLP-1 receptor agonist (AMH 2021)?", ["Your urine will test positive for glucose", "Tell your doctor immediately if you develop unexplained severe abdominal pain", "Take at least 15 minutes before a meal", "Rinse your mouth after use"], 1, "GLP-1 RAs have been linked to pancreatitis &mdash; severe unexplained abdominal pain needs prompt review. Glucose in urine is the SGLT2i point.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 431. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["asthma", "Per AMH 2021, how should a spacer be cleaned?", ["Rinse with water and towel dry after every use", "Wash monthly in warm water with detergent, don&rsquo;t rinse, air dry", "Sterilise weekly in boiling water", "Wipe with alcohol"], 1, "Wash before first use then monthly; don&rsquo;t rinse; air dry (cloth-drying adds static so medicine sticks).<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 895. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["copd", "Which symptom on a LAMA should prompt urgent review (AMH 2021)?", ["Dry mouth", "Mild throat irritation", "Eye pain with blurred vision or halos", "Taste disturbance"], 2, "Possible acute angle-closure glaucoma; also report difficulty urinating. Dry mouth is the common, expected AE.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 899. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["smoke", "A patient smoking 25/day first lights up 20 minutes after waking. AMH 2021 dependence grade?", ["Low", "Low&ndash;moderate", "Moderate", "High"], 2, "Moderate = smoking within 30 minutes of waking, usually 20&ndash;30/day. High = within 5 minutes or waking at night, &gt;30/day.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 878. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["smoke", "Which is a contraindication to bupropion (AMH 2021)?", ["Asthma", "History of an eating disorder such as bulimia", "Type 2 diabetes", "Age over 65"], 1, "Bupropion is contraindicated with seizure history, CNS tumour, bulimia or anorexia nervosa, abrupt alcohol/benzodiazepine withdrawal, or MAOI within 14 days.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 879. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG (doses, eGFR cut-offs, risk tables, asthma steps), the protocol and eTG win.</div>"]
);

/* SECTION AB - AMH 2021 ezetimibe (p300) + corticosteroids (p670-675). drafts/amh2021_ab.py */
(function(){var A={"ezet": "<b>How it works:</b> blocks absorption of dietary and biliary cholesterol across the gut wall &rarr; the liver takes up more LDL from the plasma.<br><b>Counselling:</b> tell your doctor if you have any muscle pain, tenderness or weakness.<br><b>Dose:</b> 10 mg once daily.<br><b>Common AEs:</b> headache, diarrhoea. Infrequent: myalgia, ALT/AST &gt;3&times; ULN. Rare: angioedema/rash, pancreatitis, gallstones, myopathy, raised CK.<br><b>Precautions:</b> with fenofibrate &mdash; may further increase gall bladder disease risk. Moderate&ndash;severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh &gt;6) &mdash; manufacturer discourages use. Pregnancy &mdash; no human data, avoid.<br><b>Practice points:</b> lowers LDL and is well tolerated, but on its own there is <b>no evidence it reduces clinical events</b> &mdash; consider alone only if statins aren&rsquo;t tolerated. Added to a statin it lowers LDL by about another <b>20%</b>, or lets a lower statin dose do the job (useful if high doses aren&rsquo;t tolerated). After ACS, simvastatin 40 mg + ezetimibe modestly reduced MI and stroke vs simvastatin alone, with no effect on mortality. Don&rsquo;t <b>start</b> treatment with a fixed-dose combination.<br><b>Also (Table 6-10):</b> LDL &minus;15&ndash;25% alone; once daily.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 292, 300. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG, the protocol and eTG win.</div>", "ocs": "<b>How it works:</b> changes gene expression &rarr; glucocorticoid effects (gluconeogenesis, protein and fat breakdown, suppressed inflammation and immunity) and mineralocorticoid effects (hypertension, Na and water retention, K loss). Prednisone is converted to active prednisolone.<br><b>Counselling:</b> take with food to reduce stomach upset; tell the doctor immediately about any signs of infection; mood may change (happier or sadder) and sleep may be affected; don&rsquo;t stop suddenly unless told; tell every doctor, surgeon and dentist you take (or have taken) corticosteroids; consider a Medic Alert bracelet.<br><b>Adrenal suppression:</b> doses &lt;7.5 mg prednisolone daily, or courses &lt;3 weeks, are unlikely to cause it. Give in the morning. Longer/higher courses &mdash; withdraw gradually (abrupt stop can cause adrenal crisis); extra dose needed in stress (infection, surgery, trauma); response may stay depressed &gt;1 year.<br><b>Precautions:</b> diabetes (hyperglycaemia, worse control), hypertension or heart failure (fluid retention), peptic ulcer, psychiatric disorders, glaucoma, osteoporosis, latent TB, active infection (signs may be masked), myasthenia gravis.<br><b>AEs:</b> depend on dose and duration &mdash; short high-dose courses cause fewer than long low-dose courses. Short-term: raised glucose, fluid retention, high BP, low K, dyspepsia, increased appetite, mood/sleep change (euphoria, hypomania, depression). Long-term: osteoporosis and fractures, cushingoid fat redistribution, weight gain, skin thinning, bruising, myopathy, cataract, glaucoma, infection.<br><b>Monitoring (pharmacological doses):</b> BGL, electrolytes, lipids, weight and BP at baseline then regularly; BMD if &gt;3 months or repeated courses.<br><b>Dose (AMH 2021):</b> acute asthma 40&ndash;50 mg once daily for 5&ndash;10 days; COPD exacerbation 40&ndash;50 mg once daily for 5&ndash;14 days. <i>QLD protocols: prednis(ol)one up to 5 days, no repeats &mdash; use the protocol in the Program.</i> A short course (&lt;14 days) can be stopped without tapering (p 892).<br><b>Equivalence:</b> prednisolone 5 mg = hydrocortisone 20 mg = methylprednisolone 4 mg = dexamethasone 0.75 mg.<br><b>Pregnancy/breastfeeding:</b> considered safe (lowest dose, shortest time; prednisolone preferred for maternal disorders). Breastfeeding safe; at high maternal doses avoid feeding for 4 hours after a dose.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 670&ndash;675, 892, 894. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG, the protocol and eTG win.</div>"};if(typeof DRUGS!=='undefined'){for(var i=0;i<DRUGS.length;i++){var x=DRUGS[i];if(A[x.id]){x.amh=A[x.id];if(x.src&&x.src.indexOf('AMH 2021')<0)x.src+='; AMH 2021 (print)';else if(!x.src)x.src='AMH 2021 (print)';}}}})();
COURSE_CARDS.push(
["lipid", "Ezetimibe alone vs added to a statin &mdash; what does AMH 2021 say about benefit?", "<b>Alone:</b> lowers LDL (15&ndash;25%) and is well tolerated, but there is <b>no evidence it reduces clinical events</b> &mdash; use alone only if statins aren&rsquo;t tolerated.<br><b>With a statin:</b> about 20% more LDL lowering, or allows a lower statin dose. After ACS, simvastatin 40 mg + ezetimibe modestly reduced MI and stroke (no mortality effect).<br><b>Counsel:</b> report muscle pain, tenderness or weakness. Don&rsquo;t start with a fixed-dose combination.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 292, 300. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG, the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["copd", "Prednisolone course for a flare: what to tell the patient (AMH 2021).", "Take in the <b>morning with food</b>. A short course (&lt;14 days) can be stopped without tapering (p 892); courses &lt;3 weeks are unlikely to suppress the adrenals (p 673). Expect possible raised blood glucose (check if diabetic), fluid retention, indigestion, increased appetite, mood or sleep changes. Report signs of infection.<br><i>QLD protocol: up to 5 days, no repeats.</i><div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 672&ndash;673, 892. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG, the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["bgl", "A patient with T2D is started on prednisolone for an asthma flare. What does AMH 2021 warn about?", "Corticosteroids cause <b>hyperglycaemia and worsen diabetes control</b> &mdash; monitor BGL during the course (they are listed among drugs that raise BGL, p 425). Also watch BP, fluid retention and K.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 425, 671&ndash;672. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG, the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["asthma", "Prednisolone equivalence and potency (AMH Table 14-6/14-7).", "Prednisolone 5 mg &asymp; hydrocortisone 20 mg &asymp; methylprednisolone 4 mg &asymp; dexamethasone 0.75 mg. Prednisolone anti-inflammatory potency 4, sodium-retaining 0.8 (hydrocortisone = 1). Prednisone is an inactive prodrug converted to prednisolone.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 672, 675. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG, the protocol and eTG win.</div>"]
);
COURSE_MCQ.push(
["copd", "Per AMH 2021, which prednisolone course is <i>unlikely</i> to cause adrenal suppression?", ["Any course over 5 days", "Less than 7.5 mg daily or less than 3 weeks", "Only doses under 1 mg daily", "Any dose taken in the evening"], 1, "Doses &lt;7.5 mg prednisolone daily (or equivalent) or treatment &lt;3 weeks are unlikely to cause adrenal suppression; morning dosing minimises the risk.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 673. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG, the protocol and eTG win.</div>"],
["lipid", "Per AMH 2021, when is ezetimibe used <b>alone</b>?", ["First line for everyone with high LDL", "Only if statins are not tolerated", "Only with fenofibrate", "Only in children"], 1, "Alone it lowers LDL but there is no evidence it reduces clinical events &mdash; consider only if statins are not tolerated.<div class='small muted' style='margin-top:6px'>Source: AMH 2021 (print), p 300. Drug-level detail only &mdash; where AMH 2021 differs from the QLD protocol or current eTG, the protocol and eTG win.</div>"]
);

